var TUTORIAL_REGISTRY = {"Tut-P001":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to solve one-step linear equations by identifying the operation attached to the variable, applying the inverse operation to both sides, and checking the solution.","Why It Matters":"One-step equations are the first reliable \"unlock\" skill for algebra. In an RPG, they act like simple gates: if the player understands how to undo one operation while keeping balance, later gates with multiple operations, functions, inequalities, and modeling become much less mysterious.","Prerequisite Check":"Before starting, ask the player:\n\n1. What number makes `x + 3 = 10` true?\n2. What operation undoes subtraction?\n3. What operation undoes multiplication?\n4. What is the opposite of `-8`?\n\nIf the player misses two or more, recommend a short arithmetic review on inverse operations and signed numbers before continuing.","Core Concept":"An equation says two expressions are equal. Solving means finding the value of the variable that makes the equality true.\n\nFor a one-step equation, the variable has one operation attached to it:\n\n- `x + 5 = 12`: 5 is added, so subtract 5.\n- `x - 5 = 12`: 5 is subtracted, so add 5.\n- `5x = 20`: `x` is multiplied by 5, so divide by 5.\n- `x / 5 = 20`: `x` is divided by 5, so multiply by 5.\n\nThe move must be balanced. If you subtract 5 from the left side, subtract 5 from the right side too. Balanced moves keep the equation true while making the variable easier to see.","Worked Example":"Solve `x - 7 = 18`.\n\nStep 1: Identify the operation.\nThe variable has `-7` attached, so 7 is being subtracted.\n\nStep 2: Choose the inverse operation.\nSubtraction is undone by addition, so add 7.\n\nStep 3: Apply it to both sides.\n`x - 7 + 7 = 18 + 7`\n\nStep 4: Simplify.\n`x = 25`\n\nStep 5: Check.\nReplace `x` with 25: `25 - 7 = 18`. The check works, so `x = 25`.","Fraction/Decimal Bridge":"Some one-step equations use fractions or decimals, but the idea is unchanged.\n\nExample: `(3/4)p = 12`\n\nThe variable is multiplied by `3/4`. The inverse of multiplying by `3/4` is multiplying by `4/3`.\n\n`p = 12 * 4/3 = 16`\n\nCheck: `(3/4)(16) = 12`.\n\nExample: `0.5m = 6`\n\nSince `0.5` is one half, `m` must be twice 6, so `m = 12`.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Adding when the equation already has addition.\n  Correction: Use the inverse operation. For `x + 5 = 12`, subtract 5.\n- Mistake: Changing only one side.\n  Correction: Do the same valid operation to both sides.\n- Mistake: Dropping a negative sign.\n  Correction: Keep the sign attached to the coefficient. In `-4x = 20`, divide by `-4`.\n- Mistake: Treating a fraction coefficient as if it should be multiplied again.\n  Correction: Multiply by the reciprocal.\n- Mistake: Not checking.\n  Correction: Substitute your answer into the original equation, not into a changed version you made by mistake.","Guided Practice":"1. Solve `u + 9 = 14`.\n   - Operation attached to variable: add 9.\n   - Inverse operation: subtract 9.\n   - Solution: `u = 5`.\n   - Check: `5 + 9 = 14`.\n\n2. Solve `w / 5 = -3`.\n   - Operation attached to variable: divide by 5.\n   - Inverse operation: multiply by 5.\n   - Solution: `w = -15`.\n   - Check: `-15 / 5 = -3`.\n\n3. Solve `-2r = 18`.\n   - Operation attached to variable: multiply by -2.\n   - Inverse operation: divide by -2.\n   - Solution: `r = -9`.\n   - Check: `-2(-9) = 18`.","Independent Practice":"1. Solve `x + 11 = 4`. Answer: `x = -7`.\n2. Solve `n - 6 = 15`. Answer: `n = 21`.\n3. Solve `3a = -24`. Answer: `a = -8`.\n4. Solve `b / -4 = 7`. Answer: `b = -28`.\n5. Solve `(2/3)y = 10`. Answer: `y = 15`.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Solve at least 4 of 5 mixed one-step equations correctly.\n2. Choose the correct inverse operation before solving.\n3. Check at least one answer by substitution.\n4. Explain why changing only one side is invalid.\n5. Correctly handle at least one negative coefficient or fraction coefficient.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `x - 8 = -2`; solution `x = 6`.\n2. `-5m = 30`; solution `m = -6`.\n3. `q / 7 = -4`; solution `q = -28`.\n4. `(5/6)r = 20`; solution `r = 24`.\n5. A player starts with `c` coins, gains 12, and has 31. Equation `c + 12 = 31`; solution `c = 19`.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player repeatedly uses the same operation instead of the inverse: \"You are spotting the number correctly. Now ask: what operation would undo that number\u0027s effect on the variable?\"\n- If the player changes only one side: \"The equation is like a balance. Your move can be useful only if both sides receive it.\"\n- If the player makes sign errors: \"Pause on the sign before calculating. Is the coefficient positive or negative?\"\n- If the player overuses hints 4-6: \"Try naming the attached operation before seeing the next step. That decision is the main skill.\"\n- If the player solves correctly but cannot check: \"Substitute your answer into the original equation. The original equation is the truth test.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready for mixed one-step equations with negatives, fractions, and short contexts.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P001\n- Estimated duration: 4 minutes\n- Target player state: first exposure or early review\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 001 question\n- Remediation trigger: two consecutive inverse-operation errors, two sign errors in five questions, or use of Hint 5 or 6 on three consecutive templates\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed practice plus successful explanation of one balanced inverse move","tutorialId":"Tut-P001","phaseId":"P001","title":"One-step linear equations"},"Tut-P002":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to solve multi-step linear equations by simplifying when needed, undoing operations in a logical order, and checking the final answer in the original equation.","Why It Matters":"Multi-step equations are the bridge between arithmetic puzzles and real algebra. They support later work with variables on both sides, formulas, functions, inequalities, modeling, and Equation Battle challenges where one wrong move can change the entire path.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. Solve `x + 5 = 12`. Expected: `x = 7`.\n2. Solve `3x = 18`. Expected: `x = 6`.\n3. Combine `4x + 2x`. Expected: `6x`.\n4. Simplify `2(x + 3)`. Expected: `2x + 6`.\n5. What operation undoes division by `-4`? Expected: multiplication by `-4`.\n\nIf the player misses one-step solving, route to Phase 001 review. If the player misses distribution or combining like terms, show a short simplify-first mini-lesson before continuing.","Core Concept":"Multi-step equations are solved by peeling away operations from the variable.\n\nFor `2x + 5 = 17`:\n\n1. The variable is multiplied by 2.\n2. Then 5 is added.\n3. To undo the expression, reverse that order:\n   - subtract 5,\n   - then divide by 2.\n\nThis is like unlocking a chest with two locks. Remove the outer lock first, then the inner lock.","Worked Example":"Solve `3x - 8 = 19`.\n\nStep 1: Identify the structure.\n`3x` has `-8` attached.\n\nStep 2: Remove the constant.\nAdd 8 to both sides:\n`3x - 8 + 8 = 19 + 8`\n`3x = 27`\n\nStep 3: Remove the coefficient.\nDivide both sides by 3:\n`x = 9`\n\nStep 4: Check in the original equation.\n`3(9) - 8 = 27 - 8 = 19`, so `x = 9` is correct.","Parentheses Strategy":"For equations like `2(x + 3) = 14`, the whole group is multiplied by 2. You may divide first:\n\n`2(x + 3) = 14`\n`x + 3 = 7`\n`x = 4`\n\nDistribution also works:\n\n`2x + 6 = 14`\n`2x = 8`\n`x = 4`\n\nBoth paths are valid when every operation is applied correctly.","Simplify First":"If one side has like terms, combine them before solving:\n\n`9x - 2x + 5 = 26`\n`7x + 5 = 26`\n`7x = 21`\n`x = 3`\n\nDo not combine unlike terms such as `7x + 5`; they are different kinds of objects.","Fraction and Decimal Strategy":"For `(2/3)p - 5 = 7`, remove the constant first:\n\n`(2/3)p = 12`\n\nThen undo multiplication by `2/3` using the reciprocal:\n\n`p = 12 * 3/2 = 18`\n\nFor `0.5m + 4 = 11`, subtract 4, then divide by `0.5` or multiply by 2.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Dividing first in `2x + 7 = 15`.\n  Correction: Remove `+7` first unless you divide the entire side correctly.\n- Mistake: Dividing only one term.\n  Correction: An operation applied to a side applies to the entire side.\n- Mistake: Dropping the negative coefficient.\n  Correction: In `-4r + 6 = 22`, divide by `-4`, not 4.\n- Mistake: Distributing to only one term.\n  Correction: `2(x + 3)` becomes `2x + 6`, not `2x + 3`.\n- Mistake: Stopping after the first inverse operation.\n  Correction: Continue until the variable is alone.\n- Mistake: Checking in a later line.\n  Correction: Always check in the original equation.","Guided Practice":"1. Solve `4x + 3 = 23`.\n   - Subtract 3: `4x = 20`.\n   - Divide by 4: `x = 5`.\n   - Check: `4(5) + 3 = 23`.\n\n2. Solve `2(y - 6) = 10`.\n   - Divide by 2: `y - 6 = 5`.\n   - Add 6: `y = 11`.\n   - Check: `2(11 - 6) = 10`.\n\n3. Solve `-3n - 4 = 14`.\n   - Add 4: `-3n = 18`.\n   - Divide by `-3`: `n = -6`.\n   - Check: `-3(-6) - 4 = 14`.","Independent Practice":"1. Solve `5x + 2 = 32`. Answer: `x = 6`.\n2. Solve `7a - 9 = 26`. Answer: `a = 5`.\n3. Solve `3(q + 4) = 30`. Answer: `q = 6`.\n4. Solve `(x - 5)/2 = 8`. Answer: `x = 21`.\n5. Solve `6m - 2m + 7 = 31`. Answer: `m = 6`.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Solve at least 4 of 5 mixed multi-step equations.\n2. Correctly identify the first inverse move.\n3. Simplify same-side like terms before solving.\n4. Handle a negative coefficient or negative denominator.\n5. Check one solution in the original equation.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `2x + 9 = 25`; solution `x = 8`.\n2. `-5r + 3 = 28`; solution `r = -5`.\n3. `4(t - 2) = 20`; solution `t = 7`.\n4. `(p + 10)/-2 = 6`; solution `p = -22`.\n5. `3q + 6 = 30` for a 6-coin fee and 3 coins per quest; solution `q = 8`.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player divides before removing constants: \"You found the coefficient. Now look for the operation outside the variable term; remove that first.\"\n- If the player stops after one step: \"You are halfway there. Is the variable alone yet?\"\n- If the player drops negative signs: \"Carry the sign with the coefficient until the division step is complete.\"\n- If the player mishandles parentheses: \"Treat the parentheses as one package, or distribute to every term inside.\"\n- If the player checks in the wrong line: \"Use the original equation as the truth test.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"Move into equations with variables on both sides next; the same balance idea will still work.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P002\n- Estimated duration: 5 minutes\n- Target player state: knows one-step equations but needs sequencing strategy\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 002 question\n- Remediation trigger: two wrong first moves, two sign errors, two distribution errors, or repeated stopping before the variable is isolated\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed multi-step equations plus one successful original-equation check","tutorialId":"Tut-P002","phaseId":"P002","title":"Multi-step linear equations"},"Tut-P003":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to solve equations with variables on both sides by collecting variable terms, collecting constants, simplifying when needed, and recognizing one-solution, no-solution, and infinitely-many-solution cases.","Why It Matters":"Variables-on-both-sides equations are the first place where an equation can behave in three different ways: one answer, no answer, or every answer. This skill also supports comparing two plans, finding graph intersections, and solving later equations with formulas and systems.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. Solve `2x + 5 = 17`. Expected: `x = 6`.\n2. Combine `3x - x`. Expected: `2x`.\n3. Simplify `2(x + 4)`. Expected: `2x + 8`.\n4. Is `5 = 5` always true, sometimes true, or never true? Expected: always true.\n5. Is `5 = -2` ever true? Expected: never true.\n\nIf the player misses the first item, route to Phase 002. If the player misses the truth-statement items, provide a special-case mini-lesson before classification questions.","Core Concept":"When a variable appears on both sides, use balanced operations to collect the variable terms on one side.\n\nExample: `3x + 5 = x + 13`\n\n1. Subtract `x` from both sides: `2x + 5 = 13`.\n2. Subtract 5 from both sides: `2x = 8`.\n3. Divide by 2: `x = 4`.\n4. Check: `3(4) + 5 = 17` and `4 + 13 = 17`.\n\nThe goal is not to \"move terms magically.\" The goal is to apply inverse operations to both sides.","Choosing a Side":"Both variable-collection directions can be valid, but one direction may be cleaner.\n\nFor `4x + 9 = 6x - 3`, subtracting `4x` from both sides gives a positive coefficient:\n\n`9 = 2x - 3`\n`12 = 2x`\n`x = 6`\n\nSubtracting `6x` also works, but it creates `-2x + 9 = -3`, which is more sign-heavy.","Distribution and Simplifying":"If there are parentheses or same-side like terms, simplify before collecting:\n\n`4x + 2(x - 3) = 3x + 9`\n`4x + 2x - 6 = 3x + 9`\n`6x - 6 = 3x + 9`\n`3x - 6 = 9`\n`3x = 15`\n`x = 5`","Special Cases":"If variable terms cancel, inspect the number statement.\n\nIdentity:\n`2x + 5 = x + x + 5`\n`2x + 5 = 2x + 5`\n`0 = 0`\nThis is always true, so there are infinitely many solutions.\n\nContradiction:\n`3x + 4 = 3x - 8`\n`4 = -8`\nThis is false, so there is no solution.","Graph Connection":"Solving `2x + 1 = -x + 10` finds where the lines `y = 2x + 1` and `y = -x + 10` have the same y-value. The solution `x = 3` is the x-coordinate of their intersection.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Erasing a variable term from one side only.\n  Correction: Subtract that term from both sides.\n- Mistake: Forgetting the sign of a term.\n  Correction: Keep the sign attached to the term while moving or combining.\n- Mistake: Distributing to only the variable.\n  Correction: Multiply every term inside parentheses.\n- Mistake: Treating `0 = 0` as no solution.\n  Correction: `0 = 0` is always true, so every value works.\n- Mistake: Treating a false number statement as a solution.\n  Correction: A false statement like `4 = -8` means no solution.","Guided Practice":"1. Solve `5x + 1 = 2x + 13`.\n   - Subtract `2x`: `3x + 1 = 13`.\n   - Subtract 1: `3x = 12`.\n   - Divide by 3: `x = 4`.\n\n2. Solve `2(x + 1) = x + 8`.\n   - Distribute: `2x + 2 = x + 8`.\n   - Subtract `x`: `x + 2 = 8`.\n   - Subtract 2: `x = 6`.\n\n3. Classify `4x - 3 = 4x + 9`.\n   - Subtract `4x`: `-3 = 9`.\n   - False, so no solution.","Independent Practice":"1. Solve `6x - 5 = 2x + 7`. Answer: `x = 3`.\n2. Solve `x + 14 = 4x - 1`. Answer: `x = 5`.\n3. Solve `3(x - 2) = x + 8`. Answer: `x = 7`.\n4. Classify `2x + 6 = 2(x + 3)`. Answer: infinitely many solutions.\n5. Classify `5x + 1 = 5x - 4`. Answer: no solution.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Solve at least 4 of 5 variables-on-both-sides equations.\n2. Explain why variable terms must be moved with balanced operations.\n3. Correctly solve one equation involving distribution.\n4. Correctly classify one identity and one contradiction.\n5. Check a one-solution answer in the original equation.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `4x + 3 = x + 18`; solution `x = 5`.\n2. `2(x - 5) = x + 1`; solution `x = 11`.\n3. `0.25x + 6 = 1.25x - 2`; solution `x = 8`.\n4. `3x + 7 = 3x + 7`; infinitely many solutions.\n5. `2x - 4 = 2x + 9`; no solution.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player erases a variable term: \"A term can disappear only because you applied its inverse to both sides.\"\n- If the player makes sign errors: \"Carry the sign with the term. Think of `-3x` as one signed object.\"\n- If the player struggles with side choice: \"Try moving the smaller variable coefficient so the collected coefficient is positive.\"\n- If the player misclassifies `0 = 0`: \"A true statement with no variable left means every value works.\"\n- If the player misclassifies a contradiction: \"A false number statement means no value can repair the equation.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready to rearrange formulas, where choosing a target variable becomes the main challenge.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P003\n- Estimated duration: 5 minutes\n- Target player state: understands multi-step equations and is ready to compare two expressions\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 003 question\n- Remediation trigger: two one-sided variable moves, two sign errors, one identity/contradiction reversal, or two distribution errors\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed variables-on-both-sides problems plus correct classification of identity and contradiction","tutorialId":"Tut-P003","phaseId":"P003","title":"Variables on both sides"},"Tut-P004":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to rearrange formulas by isolating a chosen target variable while treating every other letter as a known quantity.","Why It Matters":"Precalculus uses formulas constantly. Rearranging them lets a player solve for the quantity they actually need: radius from circumference, input from output, rate from interest, or a hidden stat before a transformation. This is also early preparation for inverse functions.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. Solve `2x + 5 = 17` for `x`. Expected: `x = 6`.\n2. Solve `A = 3x` for `x`. Expected: `x = A/3`.\n3. Solve `y = mx + b` for the expression before division by `m`. Expected: `y - b = mx`.\n4. Factor `x + bx`. Expected: `x(1+b)`.\n5. Why can we not divide by 0? Expected: division by zero is undefined.\n\nIf the player misses factoring, route to a common-factor mini-lesson. If they miss restrictions, give a nonzero denominator reminder.","Core Concept":"A literal equation has several letters. The prompt tells you which one is the target.\n\nExample: Solve `P = 2L + 2W` for `L`.\n\nThe target is `L`; `P` and `W` behave like known quantities.\n\n1. Remove the term not containing `L`: `P - 2W = 2L`.\n2. Divide by the coefficient of `L`: `(P - 2W)/2 = L`.\n3. Write the target on the left: `L = (P - 2W)/2`.","Restrictions":"Whenever you divide by an expression, state that it cannot be zero.\n\nExample: `A = lw` solved for `l` gives `l = A/w`, with `w != 0`.\n\nThe restriction is not decorative. If `w = 0`, the original formula becomes `A = 0`, and division by `w` is not allowed.","Grouping and Parentheses":"When subtracting before dividing, keep the numerator grouped.\n\n`y = mx + b`\n`y - b = mx`\n`x = (y - b)/m`\n\nThe expression `(y - b)/m` means the entire difference is divided by `m`.","Factoring the Target":"If the target appears more than once, collect it by factoring.\n\n`A = x + bx`\n`A = x(1 + b)`\n`x = A/(1 + b)`\n\nThis works because both terms contain `x`.","Reciprocal Formulas":"For formulas like `1/f = 1/u + 1/v`, isolate the reciprocal first.\n\n`1/v = 1/f - 1/u`\n`1/v = (u - f)/(fu)`\n`v = fu/(u - f)`\n\nRestrictions matter: `f`, `u`, and `u - f` cannot be zero in the final form.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Solving for the wrong letter.\n  Correction: Circle or name the target before moving anything.\n- Mistake: Dividing by only one term.\n  Correction: Use parentheses around grouped numerators, such as `(y-b)/m`.\n- Mistake: Dropping non-target variables.\n  Correction: Other letters stay in the formula unless they cancel by valid algebra.\n- Mistake: Forgetting restrictions.\n  Correction: Every denominator must be nonzero.\n- Mistake: Not factoring when the target appears twice.\n  Correction: Factor the target first, then divide.\n- Mistake: Flipping a reciprocal too early.\n  Correction: Isolate the reciprocal expression first.","Guided Practice":"1. Solve `A = bh` for `h`.\n   - Divide by `b`.\n   - `h = A/b`, with `b != 0`.\n\n2. Solve `y = 4x + b` for `x`.\n   - Subtract `b`: `y - b = 4x`.\n   - Divide by 4: `x = (y - b)/4`.\n\n3. Solve `M = (a + b)/2` for `b`.\n   - Multiply by 2: `2M = a + b`.\n   - Subtract `a`: `b = 2M - a`.","Independent Practice":"1. Solve `C = 2*pi*r` for `r`. Answer: `r = C/(2*pi)`.\n2. Solve `V = lwh` for `w`. Answer: `w = V/(lh)`, with `l != 0`, `h != 0`.\n3. Solve `F = (9/5)C + 32` for `C`. Answer: `C = (5/9)(F - 32)`.\n4. Solve `m = (y2 - y1)/(x2 - x1)` for `y1`. Answer: `y1 = y2 - m(x2 - x1)`.\n5. Solve `R = x + ax` for `x`. Answer: `x = R/(1+a)`, with `a != -1`.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Rearrange at least 4 of 5 literal equations correctly.\n2. Identify the target variable before solving.\n3. Preserve grouping in at least one answer like `(y-b)/m`.\n4. State nonzero restrictions when dividing by a variable expression.\n5. Factor a repeated target variable when needed.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `P = 2L + 2W` for `W`: `W = (P - 2L)/2`.\n2. `d = rt` for `r`: `r = d/t`, `t != 0`.\n3. `A = (1/2)bh` for `b`: `b = 2A/h`, `h != 0`.\n4. `y = mx + b` for `m`: `m = (y-b)/x`, `x != 0`.\n5. `Q = x - kx` for `x`: `x = Q/(1-k)`, `k != 1`.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player solves for the wrong variable: \"Pause and name the target. Every move should isolate that letter.\"\n- If the player loses grouping: \"The entire expression you created must move together. Use parentheses.\"\n- If the player omits restrictions: \"You divided by a symbol. What value would make that divisor zero?\"\n- If the player drops a non-target variable: \"That letter represents an unknown quantity, so it must remain unless algebra removes it.\"\n- If the player struggles with repeated targets: \"The target appears in multiple terms. Factor it before dividing.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready to use rearranged formulas inside modeling problems.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P004\n- Estimated duration: 5 minutes\n- Target player state: can solve linear equations and is learning symbolic rearrangement\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 004 question\n- Remediation trigger: two wrong target-variable choices, two missing restrictions, two grouping errors, or one repeated-target factoring error\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed literal equations plus correct restrictions on at least two answers","tutorialId":"Tut-P004","phaseId":"P004","title":"Literal equations"},"Tut-P005":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to translate situations into linear equations, solve them, and interpret the answer in the original context.","Why It Matters":"Algebra in the game is not only symbol manipulation. Modeling lets a player turn a story, table, graph, or diagram into a solvable structure. This skill powers quest costs, resource planning, break-even decisions, travel estimates, and later systems of equations.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. Solve `12 + 4q = 40`. Expected: `q = 7`.\n2. What does \"5 coins per quest\" mean algebraically? Expected: `5q`.\n3. What does \"costs the same\" tell us to do? Expected: set expressions equal.\n4. If a player spends 8 coins, should the model add or subtract 8? Expected: subtract.\n5. Why should an answer include units? Expected: units tell what the number means.\n\nIf the player misses the equation solving but can translate, route to Phase 002 or 003 review. If the player solves but cannot translate, begin with fixed-rate models.","Core Concept":"A modeling problem has four steps:\n\n1. Define the variable.\n2. Build expressions from the story.\n3. Write an equation from the relationship.\n4. Solve and interpret the result.\n\nExample: \"A guild charges 12 coins plus 4 coins per quest. Total cost is 40.\"\n\nLet `q` be quests.\n\nFixed cost: 12.\nPer-quest cost: `4q`.\nTotal: 40.\n\nEquation: `12 + 4q = 40`.\nSolve: `4q = 28`, so `q = 7`.\nInterpretation: the player completed 7 quests.","Fixed-Rate Models":"Use `fixed + rate*x = total`.\n\nFixed values happen once. Rates repeat for each unit.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Entry fee plus cost per quest.\n- Base pay plus commission per sale.\n- Starting tokens plus tokens earned each week.\n- Taxi fee plus cost per mile.","Start-Change-End Models":"Use:\n\n- start + gain = final\n- start - loss = final\n- start + rate*time = final\n\nThe sign comes from the story, not from where the number appears in the sentence.","Equal Expressions":"When two plans are equal, write one expression for each plan and set them equal.\n\nExample:\n`12 + 3d = 4 + 5d`\n\nThis type of model often uses variables on both sides.","Define Related Quantities":"Sometimes one variable describes several quantities.\n\nIf a rectangle\u0027s length is 5 more than its width:\n\n- width: `w`\n- length: `w + 5`\n- perimeter: `2(w+5) + 2w`\n\nIf two consecutive integers are needed:\n\n- smaller: `n`\n- larger: `n + 1`","Model Checking":"A model is valid only if it matches the story. The equation `x + 8 = 30` is solvable, but it does not describe \"spent 8 and had 30 left.\" The correct model is `x - 8 = 30`.\n\nAfter solving, check in the story:\n\nIf `x = 38`, then starting with 38 and spending 8 leaves 30. The story check works.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Swapping fixed cost and rate.\n  Correction: Ask which quantity happens once and which repeats.\n- Mistake: Ignoring the starting value.\n  Correction: Include the initial amount as the intercept or fixed segment.\n- Mistake: Reversing gain and loss.\n  Correction: Match the sign to the action.\n- Mistake: Answering the wrong quantity.\n  Correction: Re-read the variable definition and the final question.\n- Mistake: Accepting impractical answers.\n  Correction: Counts of boxes, people, or quests must usually be whole and nonnegative.","Guided Practice":"1. A shop charges 9 coins plus 2 coins per item for a total of 25.\n   - Let `i` be items.\n   - Equation: `9 + 2i = 25`.\n   - Solution: `i = 8` items.\n\n2. Plan A costs `5 + 6x`; Plan B costs `17 + 3x`. When are they equal?\n   - Equation: `5 + 6x = 17 + 3x`.\n   - Solution: `x = 4`.\n\n3. The sum of a number and the next integer is 41.\n   - Equation: `n + (n+1) = 41`.\n   - Solution: `n = 20`, so the integers are 20 and 21.","Independent Practice":"1. A pass costs 20 coins plus 3 coins per ride. Total is 44. Answer: 8 rides.\n2. A player had coins, gained 17, and ended with 52. Answer: 35 coins.\n3. A rectangle has length 4 more than width and perimeter 36. Answer: width 7, length 11.\n4. Two plans cost `15 + 2x` and `5 + 4x`. Answer: equal at `x = 5`.\n5. A table starts at 10 and increases by 6 per level. When does it reach 52? Answer: Level 7.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Define a variable correctly.\n2. Build an equivalent equation before solving.\n3. Solve fixed-rate and comparison models.\n4. Interpret the answer with units.\n5. Reject at least one model that conflicts with the story.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `18 + 5q = 63`; solution `q = 9` quests.\n2. `40 - 3d = 16`; solution `d = 8` days.\n3. `7 + 4x = 19 + x`; solution `x = 4`.\n4. `(88 + x)/2 = 91`; solution `x = 94`.\n5. `3n + 2 = 29`; solution `n = 9`.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player swaps fixed and rate: \"Which number happens once, and which number repeats for every unit?\"\n- If the player reverses gain/loss: \"Act out the story: did the amount go up or down?\"\n- If the player solves without defining a variable: \"Name what the variable means before writing the equation.\"\n- If the player gives a number without units: \"What does this number count or measure?\"\n- If the player accepts an invalid model: \"Check the equation against the story before solving.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready for Equation Battle, where model moves become tactical algebra moves.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P005\n- Estimated duration: 5 minutes\n- Target player state: can solve equations but needs help translating stories\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 005 question\n- Remediation trigger: two fixed/rate swaps, two gain/loss reversals, two missing variable definitions, or one accepted invalid model\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed modeling prompts plus successful story check on one solution","tutorialId":"Tut-P005","phaseId":"P005","title":"Linear equation modeling"},"Tut-P006":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to play Equation Battle legally and strategically by choosing action cards that preserve equality, simplify expressions, isolate variables, and classify special battle states.","Why It Matters":"Equation Battle turns algebra into visible decisions. The goal is not to memorize card orders; it is to understand why each move is legal and why it helps. This makes later equation types, inequalities, systems, and boss challenges feel like extensions of the same balance system.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. What operation undoes `+7`?\n2. What operation undoes multiplication by `-4`?\n3. Is changing only one side of an equation legal?\n4. What does `4x + 3x` simplify to?\n5. What does `0 = 0` mean as a final battle state?\n\nIf the player misses inverse operations, route to Phase 001 or 002. If they miss special states, review Phase 003 identity and contradiction cases.","Core Concept":"An Equation Battle card is legal if it creates an equivalent equation.\n\nLegal card types:\n\n- Same operation on both sides: `-7 both sides`, `/5 both sides`, `+2x both sides`.\n- Simplify one expression without changing its value: combine like terms, distribute, factor, or reduce a fraction.\n- Classify a final state: no solution for a false statement, infinitely many solutions for an identity.\n\nIllegal card types:\n\n- Apply a move to only one side.\n- Divide one term of a sum while leaving the rest unchanged.\n- Erase a term without an inverse operation.\n- Distribute to only part of a group.","Worked Example":"Battle state: `2x + 5 = 17`\n\nGoal: isolate `x`.\n\nCard 1: `-5 both sides`\nNew state: `2x = 12`\n\nCard 2: `/2 both sides`\nNew state: `x = 6`\n\nCheck card:\n`2(6) + 5 = 17`, so the battle is won.","Simplify Cards":"Some cards rewrite an expression instead of acting on both sides.\n\nExamples:\n\n- `4x + 3x = 35` becomes `7x = 35`.\n- `2(x + 3) = 14` becomes `2x + 6 = 14`.\n\nThese are legal because the expression keeps the same value.","Efficient Paths":"Some battles have more than one valid path.\n\nFor `2(x + 3) = 14`:\n\n- Distribute path: distribute, subtract 6, divide by 2.\n- Group path: divide by 2, subtract 3.\n\nBoth are legal, but the group path is shorter.","Classification Cards":"When variables disappear, stop and classify.\n\n- `4 = -8` means no solution.\n- `0 = 0` means infinitely many solutions.\n\nDo not invent an `x` value after the target variable has vanished.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Choosing the same operation instead of the inverse.\n  Correction: Ask what operation would undo the current one.\n- Mistake: Playing a one-sided card.\n  Correction: Use both-side cards unless you are simplifying an expression.\n- Mistake: Dividing before removing an outside constant.\n  Correction: Remove the farthest operation first.\n- Mistake: Dropping a negative sign on a card.\n  Correction: Keep the sign attached to the coefficient.\n- Mistake: Continuing after a contradiction or identity.\n  Correction: Classify the battle state.","Guided Practice":"1. Battle state `x - 8 = 3`.\n   - Card: `+8 both sides`.\n   - Result: `x = 11`.\n\n2. Battle state `3x + 4 = 19`.\n   - Cards: `-4 both sides`, `/3 both sides`.\n   - Result: `x = 5`.\n\n3. Battle state `5x + 2 = 2x + 14`.\n   - Cards: `-2x both sides`, `-2 both sides`, `/3 both sides`.\n   - Result: `x = 4`.","Independent Practice":"1. Choose cards for `x + 9 = 20`. Answer: `-9`; `x = 11`.\n2. Choose cards for `4x - 7 = 9`. Answer: `+7`, `/4`; `x = 4`.\n3. Choose cards for `2(x - 5) = 18`. Answer: `/2`, `+5`; `x = 14`.\n4. Classify `3x + 1 = 3x - 6`. Answer: no solution.\n5. Classify `2x + 8 = 2(x + 4)`. Answer: infinitely many solutions.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Identify legal and illegal cards.\n2. Sequence cards for one-step and multi-step equations.\n3. Use simplify cards correctly.\n4. Collect variable terms legally.\n5. Classify identity and contradiction states.\n6. Check a final numeric solution in the original equation.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `x + 6 = 18`: card `-6`; solution `x = 12`.\n2. `-3x + 5 = 20`: cards `-5`, `/-3`; solution `x = -5`.\n3. `4x + x = 30`: combine, `/5`; solution `x = 6`.\n4. `2x + 7 = x + 15`: `-x`, `-7`; solution `x = 8`.\n5. `5x - 2 = 5x + 3`: no solution.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player makes one-sided moves: \"The card changed only one side. Try the matching both-side card.\"\n- If the player picks wrong inverse cards: \"Name the operation attached to the variable, then pick the card that undoes it.\"\n- If the player stops early: \"Is the variable alone yet?\"\n- If the player distributes partially: \"The multiplier must hit every term in the group.\"\n- If the player misclassifies special states: \"When the variable disappears, the truth of the number statement decides the battle.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"Try efficiency challenges where more than one legal path exists.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P006\n- Estimated duration: 5 minutes\n- Target player state: knows equation solving and is learning game-action representations\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 006 battle prompt\n- Remediation trigger: two illegal one-sided cards, two wrong inverse cards, two premature completions, or one identity/contradiction reversal\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent success on mixed card-selection battles plus one correct check in the original equation","tutorialId":"Tut-P006","phaseId":"P006","title":"Equation Battle fundamentals"},"Tut-P007":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to solve, graph, and interpret linear inequalities while remembering when the inequality direction must reverse.","Why It Matters":"Inequalities describe limits, requirements, safe zones, budgets, thresholds, and ranges. They are the algebra of \"more than enough,\" \"not too much,\" and \"allowed values,\" which makes them essential for game constraints and later domain/range reasoning.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. Solve `x + 5 = 12`. Expected: `x = 7`.\n2. Which values are less than 7: values left or right of 7 on a number line? Expected: left.\n3. Does `x \u003c 7` include 7? Expected: no.\n4. Does `x \u003c= 7` include 7? Expected: yes.\n5. What happens to an inequality when dividing by a negative? Expected: it reverses.","Core Concept":"Solving an inequality uses the same inverse-operation ideas as solving an equation, but the answer is a set of values.\n\nExample:\n\n`2x + 5 \u003c 17`\n`2x \u003c 12`\n`x \u003c 6`\n\nThe boundary is 6. Since the symbol is `\u003c`, use an open circle at 6 and shade left.","The Flip Rule":"The inequality direction reverses only when multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative.\n\nExample:\n\n`-2x \u003c 8`\n`x \u003e -4`\n\nA quick reason: if `2 \u003c 5`, multiplying by `-1` gives `-2 \u003e -5`. The order reverses on the number line.","Graphing Solutions":"- `\u003c`: open circle, shade left.\n- `\u003c=`: closed circle, shade left.\n- `\u003e`: open circle, shade right.\n- `\u003e=`: closed circle, shade right.\n\nOpen means the boundary is not included. Closed means the boundary is included.","Interval Notation":"- `x \u003c 6` is `(-infinity, 6)`.\n- `x \u003c= 6` is `(-infinity, 6]`.\n- `x \u003e -2` is `(-2, infinity)`.\n- `x \u003e= -2` is `[-2, infinity)`.\n\nInfinity always uses a parenthesis.","Checking Inequalities":"Test values in the original inequality.\n\nFor `2x + 5 \u003c 17`, the solution is `x \u003c 6`.\n\nTry `x = 5`: `2(5)+5 = 15`, and `15 \u003c 17` is true.\nTry boundary `x = 6`: `17 \u003c 17` is false, so 6 is not included.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Flipping after adding or subtracting.\n  Correction: Flip only after multiplying or dividing by a negative.\n- Mistake: Forgetting to flip after dividing by a negative.\n  Correction: Circle the negative divisor before dividing.\n- Mistake: Using a closed circle for `\u003c`.\n  Correction: Strict inequalities use open circles.\n- Mistake: Treating the boundary as the only solution.\n  Correction: Inequalities usually describe infinitely many values.\n- Mistake: Confusing \"at most\" and \"at least.\"\n  Correction: At most is `\u003c=`; at least is `\u003e=`.","Guided Practice":"1. Solve `x - 3 \u003e 10`.\n   - Add 3: `x \u003e 13`.\n   - Open circle at 13, shade right.\n\n2. Solve `4x \u003c= 20`.\n   - Divide by positive 4: `x \u003c= 5`.\n   - Closed circle at 5, shade left.\n\n3. Solve `-5x \u003e= 15`.\n   - Divide by `-5` and flip: `x \u003c= -3`.\n   - Closed circle at -3, shade left.","Independent Practice":"1. `x + 8 \u003c 20`; answer `x \u003c 12`.\n2. `3x \u003e= -9`; answer `x \u003e= -3`.\n3. `-2x \u003c= 10`; answer `x \u003e= -5`.\n4. `5 + 2x \u003e 17`; answer `x \u003e 6`.\n5. `-4(x+1) \u003c 8`; answer `x \u003e -3`.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Solve at least 4 of 5 mixed linear inequalities.\n2. Correctly flip after multiplying or dividing by a negative.\n3. Graph with correct endpoint and shading direction.\n4. Convert one solution to interval notation.\n5. Translate at least one \"at most\" or \"at least\" context.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `x - 6 \u003c= 4`; solution `x \u003c= 10`.\n2. `2x + 3 \u003e 15`; solution `x \u003e 6`.\n3. `-3x \u003c 12`; solution `x \u003e -4`.\n4. `x/(-5) \u003e= 2`; solution `x \u003c= -10`.\n5. `9 + 2p \u003c= 25`; solution `p \u003c= 8`.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player flips after adding: \"Addition and subtraction keep the order; the flip only comes from multiplying or dividing by a negative.\"\n- If the player forgets the negative flip: \"You divided by a negative. The number line order reverses.\"\n- If the player uses the wrong endpoint: \"Check whether equality is included in the symbol.\"\n- If the player shades the wrong way: \"Pick a test value on that side and see whether it works.\"\n- If the player gives only the boundary: \"The boundary helps draw the solution, but the answer is a whole region.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready for compound inequalities, where two bounds work together.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P007\n- Estimated duration: 5 minutes\n- Target player state: can solve equations and is learning inequality solution sets\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 007 question\n- Remediation trigger: two missed flips, two endpoint errors, two shading errors, or one at most/at least reversal\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed inequalities plus correct graphing of strict and inclusive examples","tutorialId":"Tut-P007","phaseId":"P007","title":"Linear inequalities"},"Tut-P008":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to solve, graph, and interpret compound inequalities joined by \"and\" or \"or,\" including bounded three-part inequalities and interval notation.","Why It Matters":"Compound inequalities describe ranges and split regions: safe temperature zones, allowed item counts, restricted domains, danger zones, and acceptable stat windows. They are a direct bridge to domain/range work and absolute value inequalities.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. Graph `x \u003c 4`. Expected: open at 4, shade left.\n2. Graph `x \u003e= -1`. Expected: closed at -1, shade right.\n3. What does \"and\" mean for two conditions? Expected: both must be true.\n4. What does \"or\" mean? Expected: either condition can be true.\n5. What happens when dividing an inequality by a negative? Expected: the direction reverses.","Core Concept":"Compound inequalities combine two inequality ideas.\n\n`1 \u003c x \u003c 6` means `x \u003e 1 and x \u003c 6`.\n\nThe graph is the overlap: values between 1 and 6.\n\n`x \u003c -1 or x \u003e= 4` means values can be in either region.\n\nThe graph is the union: shade left of -1 and right from 4.","Three-Part Inequalities":"Solve a three-part inequality by applying each operation to all three parts.\n\nExample:\n\n`1 \u003c x + 3 \u003c 9`\n`-2 \u003c x \u003c 6`\n\nDo not subtract 3 only from the middle.","Negative Three-Part Inequalities":"If you divide all parts by a negative, flip both inequality symbols.\n\n`-8 \u003c= -2x \u003c 6`\n`4 \u003e= x \u003e -3`\n\nThen rewrite in increasing order:\n\n`-3 \u003c x \u003c= 4`","And Means Overlap":"For `x \u003e 1 and x \u003c= 6`, both conditions must be true. The solution is `(1,6]`.\n\nIf there is no overlap, there is no solution:\n\n`x \u003c 2 and x \u003e 5` has no solution.","Or Means Union":"For `x \u003c -1 or x \u003e= 4`, either condition can be true. The solution is `(-infinity,-1) union [4,infinity)`.\n\nIf the union covers all values, the answer is all real numbers:\n\n`x \u003c 3 or x \u003e= 3`.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Solving only one side of a three-part inequality.\n  Correction: Apply operations to all three parts.\n- Mistake: Treating \"and\" as \"or.\"\n  Correction: \"And\" means overlap.\n- Mistake: Treating \"or\" as \"and.\"\n  Correction: \"Or\" means union.\n- Mistake: Forgetting to reorder after dividing by a negative in a three-part inequality.\n  Correction: Flip both symbols, then write lower bound first.\n- Mistake: Losing endpoint types.\n  Correction: Brackets/closed circles mean included; parentheses/open circles mean excluded.","Guided Practice":"1. Solve `2 \u003c x - 4 \u003c= 9`.\n   - Add 4 to all parts: `6 \u003c x \u003c= 13`.\n\n2. Solve `x \u003e= -2 and x \u003c 5`.\n   - Overlap: `[-2,5)`.\n\n3. Solve `x \u003c= 1 or x \u003e 7`.\n   - Union: `(-infinity,1] union (7,infinity)`.","Independent Practice":"1. `-3 \u003c x + 2 \u003c 8`; answer `-5 \u003c x \u003c 6`.\n2. `4 \u003c= 2x \u003c 14`; answer `2 \u003c= x \u003c 7`.\n3. `x \u003e -1 and x \u003c= 3`; answer `(-1,3]`.\n4. `x \u003c -4 or x \u003e= 6`; answer `(-infinity,-4) union [6,infinity)`.\n5. `-6 \u003c -3x \u003c= 9`; answer `-3 \u003c= x \u003c 2`.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Solve three-part inequalities.\n2. Correctly handle negative division in a three-part inequality.\n3. Distinguish \"and\" overlap from \"or\" union.\n4. Graph bounded intervals and disjoint unions.\n5. Convert between inequality, graph, interval, and set-builder notation.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `0 \u003c= x + 5 \u003c 12`; solution `-5 \u003c= x \u003c 7`.\n2. `-10 \u003c -2x \u003c= 4`; solution `-2 \u003c= x \u003c 5`.\n3. `x \u003e 2 and x \u003c 9`; solution `(2,9)`.\n4. `x \u003c= -3 or x \u003e 4`; solution `(-infinity,-3] union (4,infinity)`.\n5. `15 \u003c= 4q + 3 \u003c= 31`; solution `3 \u003c= q \u003c= 7`.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player treats \"and\" as \"or\": \"Look for values that satisfy both conditions at the same time.\"\n- If the player treats \"or\" as \"and\": \"A value only needs to satisfy one branch.\"\n- If the player changes only the middle of a three-part inequality: \"All three parts must receive the operation.\"\n- If the player misses a negative flip: \"You divided by a negative. Both inequality signs reverse.\"\n- If endpoint errors repeat: \"Translate brackets and inequality symbols before shading.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready for absolute value equations and inequalities, where distance creates two cases.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P008\n- Estimated duration: 5 minutes\n- Target player state: understands one-sided inequalities and graphing\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 008 question\n- Remediation trigger: two and/or confusions, two endpoint errors, two three-part operation errors, or one missed negative flip\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed compound inequalities plus correct graphing of one bounded interval and one union","tutorialId":"Tut-P008","phaseId":"P008","title":"Compound inequalities"},"Tut-P009":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to solve absolute value equations by using distance meaning, isolating the absolute value, splitting into branches, and checking all candidates.","Why It Matters":"Absolute value is the algebra of distance. It appears in tolerance ranges, error from a target, distance from a checkpoint, piecewise functions, and later absolute value inequalities.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. What is `|6|`? Expected: 6.\n2. What is `|-6|`? Expected: 6.\n3. Can a distance be negative? Expected: no.\n4. Solve `x - 3 = 7`. Expected: `x = 10`.\n5. Solve `x - 3 = -7`. Expected: `x = -4`.","Core Concept":"Absolute value measures distance.\n\n`|x| = 6` means `x` is 6 units from 0, so `x = 6` or `x = -6`.\n\n`|x - 3| = 7` means `x` is 7 units from 3, so `x = 3 + 7 = 10` or `x = 3 - 7 = -4`.","Branch Method":"If `|A| = k` and `k \u003e 0`, then:\n\n`A = k` or `A = -k`.\n\nExample:\n\n`|2x - 3| = 7`\n\nBranches:\n\n`2x - 3 = 7` gives `x = 5`.\n`2x - 3 = -7` gives `x = -2`.","Isolate First":"Do not branch until the absolute value is alone.\n\nExample:\n\n`3|x - 4| = 18`\n`|x - 4| = 6`\n\nNow branch:\n\n`x - 4 = 6` or `x - 4 = -6`.","Zero and Negative Cases":"- `|A| = 0` gives one equation: `A = 0`.\n- `|A| = negative` has no solution.\n\nExample:\n\n`|x - 4| + 3 = 1`\n`|x - 4| = -2`\n\nNo solution.","Equal Distances":"Equations like `|x - 3| = |x + 1|` ask for a point equally distant from two centers. The solution is the midpoint between 3 and -1, which is 1.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Only solving the positive branch.\n  Correction: Use both `A = k` and `A = -k`.\n- Mistake: Branching before isolating.\n  Correction: Make the absolute value expression stand alone first.\n- Mistake: Solving when the isolated distance is negative.\n  Correction: Stop and say no solution.\n- Mistake: Treating `|x + 5|` as distance from 5.\n  Correction: It is distance from -5.\n- Mistake: Forgetting to check.\n  Correction: Substitute every candidate into the original equation.","Guided Practice":"1. Solve `|x - 2| = 4`.\n   - `x - 2 = 4` or `x - 2 = -4`.\n   - `x = 6` or `x = -2`.\n\n2. Solve `|3x| = 12`.\n   - `3x = 12` or `3x = -12`.\n   - `x = 4` or `x = -4`.\n\n3. Solve `|x + 1| - 5 = 0`.\n   - `|x + 1| = 5`.\n   - `x + 1 = 5` or `x + 1 = -5`.\n   - `x = 4` or `x = -6`.","Independent Practice":"1. `|x| = 9`; answer `x = 9` or `x = -9`.\n2. `|x - 6| = 2`; answer `x = 8` or `x = 4`.\n3. `|2x + 4| = 10`; answer `x = 3` or `x = -7`.\n4. `5|x + 3| = 20`; answer `x = 1` or `x = -7`.\n5. `|x - 1| + 4 = 2`; answer no solution.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Explain absolute value as distance.\n2. Solve basic and shifted absolute value equations.\n3. Isolate absolute value before branching.\n4. Identify zero and negative isolated-distance cases.\n5. Check all candidate solutions.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `|x + 4| = 8`; solutions `x = 4`, `x = -12`.\n2. `|3x - 6| = 12`; solutions `x = 6`, `x = -2`.\n3. `2|x - 5| + 1 = 9`; solutions `x = 9`, `x = 1`.\n4. `|x + 7| = 0`; solution `x = -7`.\n5. `|x - 2| = -3`; no solution.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player gives one solution: \"Absolute value is distance. Is there another point the same distance on the other side?\"\n- If the player branches too early: \"First isolate the absolute value expression; then decide the branches.\"\n- If the player solves a negative-distance case: \"An isolated absolute value cannot equal a negative number.\"\n- If the player has sign errors with `x+h`: \"Rewrite `x+h` as `x - (-h)` to see the center.\"\n- If the player skips checking: \"Substitute both candidates into the original equation.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready for absolute value inequalities, where distance becomes inside or outside a range.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P009\n- Estimated duration: 5 minutes\n- Target player state: understands linear equations and number-line distance\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 009 question\n- Remediation trigger: two missing-branch errors, two isolate-before-branch errors, one negative-distance error, or two center-sign errors\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed absolute value equations plus correct checking of both branches","tutorialId":"Tut-P009","phaseId":"P009","title":"Absolute value equations"},"Tut-P010":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to solve absolute value inequalities by interpreting them as distance conditions: inside a range for less-than, outside a range for greater-than.","Why It Matters":"Absolute value inequalities describe tolerances, errors, safe zones, target ranges, and excluded danger zones. They connect directly to domain restrictions, graph intervals, and piecewise thinking.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. What does `|x - 4|` measure? Expected: distance from 4.\n2. Solve `|x - 4| = 3`. Expected: `x = 1` or `x = 7`.\n3. What does \"and\" mean in a compound inequality? Expected: overlap.\n4. What does \"or\" mean? Expected: union.\n5. Can absolute value be negative? Expected: no.","Core Concept":"Absolute value is distance.\n\nIf the distance is less than a number, the values are inside a band.\n\n`|x| \u003c 6` means `-6 \u003c x \u003c 6`.\n\nIf the distance is greater than a number, the values are outside a band.\n\n`|x| \u003e 6` means `x \u003c -6 or x \u003e 6`.","Inside Inequalities":"Use inside ranges for:\n\n- `|A| \u003c k`: `-k \u003c A \u003c k`\n- `|A| \u003c= k`: `-k \u003c= A \u003c= k`\n\nExample:\n\n`|2x - 1| \u003c 7`\n`-7 \u003c 2x - 1 \u003c 7`\n`-6 \u003c 2x \u003c 8`\n`-3 \u003c x \u003c 4`","Outside Inequalities":"Use outside unions for:\n\n- `|A| \u003e k`: `A \u003c -k or A \u003e k`\n- `|A| \u003e= k`: `A \u003c= -k or A \u003e= k`\n\nExample:\n\n`|2x - 1| \u003e 7`\n`2x - 1 \u003c -7 or 2x - 1 \u003e 7`\n`x \u003c -3 or x \u003e 4`","Isolate First":"For `3|2x - 1| + 4 \u003c= 19`, isolate first:\n\n`3|2x - 1| \u003c= 15`\n`|2x - 1| \u003c= 5`\n\nThen use the inside rule.","Special Cases":"- `|A| \u003c negative`: no solution.\n- `|A| \u003e negative`: all real numbers.\n- `|A| \u003e 0`: all real numbers except where `A = 0`.\n- `|A| \u003e= 0`: all real numbers.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Using `or` for less-than.\n  Correction: Less-than means inside, an \"and\" interval.\n- Mistake: Using `and` for greater-than.\n  Correction: Greater-than means outside, an \"or\" union.\n- Mistake: Forgetting to isolate first.\n  Correction: Make the absolute value expression stand alone.\n- Mistake: Using the wrong endpoint.\n  Correction: Strict symbols use open endpoints; inclusive symbols use closed endpoints.\n- Mistake: Ignoring special cases with negative or zero bounds.\n  Correction: Use distance meaning before branching.","Guided Practice":"1. Solve `|x - 2| \u003c 5`.\n   - Inside: `-5 \u003c x - 2 \u003c 5`.\n   - Add 2: `-3 \u003c x \u003c 7`.\n\n2. Solve `|x + 1| \u003e= 4`.\n   - Outside: `x + 1 \u003c= -4 or x + 1 \u003e= 4`.\n   - `x \u003c= -5 or x \u003e= 3`.\n\n3. Solve `2|x| - 3 \u003c 5`.\n   - `2|x| \u003c 8`.\n   - `|x| \u003c 4`.\n   - `-4 \u003c x \u003c 4`.","Independent Practice":"1. `|x| \u003c= 8`; answer `[-8,8]`.\n2. `|x - 5| \u003e 2`; answer `x \u003c 3 or x \u003e 7`.\n3. `|2x + 4| \u003c 10`; answer `-7 \u003c x \u003c 3`.\n4. `|x + 6| \u003e= 1`; answer `x \u003c= -7 or x \u003e= -5`.\n5. `|x - 1| \u003c -3`; answer no solution.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Identify inside versus outside rules.\n2. Isolate absolute value before solving.\n3. Graph bounded and split solutions.\n4. Handle negative and zero special cases.\n5. Translate a tolerance context into an absolute value inequality.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `|x - 3| \u003c= 6`; solution `-3 \u003c= x \u003c= 9`.\n2. `|x + 2| \u003e 5`; solution `x \u003c -7 or x \u003e 3`.\n3. `2|x - 1| + 4 \u003c 12`; solution `-3 \u003c x \u003c 5`.\n4. `|3x| \u003e= 12`; solution `x \u003c= -4 or x \u003e= 4`.\n5. `|x + 1| \u003e= 0`; all real numbers.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player uses `or` for less-than: \"Less than means close to the center, so use the inside interval.\"\n- If the player uses `and` for greater-than: \"Greater than means far from the center, so use outside rays.\"\n- If the player branches before isolating: \"First make the absolute value expression stand alone.\"\n- If endpoint errors repeat: \"Check whether equality is included.\"\n- If special-case errors occur: \"Before splitting, ask whether the distance bound is negative, zero, or positive.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready to use intervals as domains and restrictions.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P010\n- Estimated duration: 5 minutes\n- Target player state: knows absolute value equations and compound inequalities\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 010 question\n- Remediation trigger: two inside/outside reversals, two isolate-first errors, two endpoint errors, or one negative-bound classification error\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed absolute value inequalities plus correct graphing of one inside and one outside solution","tutorialId":"Tut-P010","phaseId":"P010","title":"Absolute value inequalities"},"Tut-P011":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to solve systems of linear equations by substitution: replacing one variable with an equal expression so the system becomes one equation in one variable.","Why It Matters":"Substitution is the algebra version of merging two clues. If one rule tells you what a variable equals, you can place that rule inside the other equation. This method powers price puzzles, comparison problems, graph intersections, and later work with functions, inverse functions, and nonlinear systems.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. If `y=2x+1`, what can replace `y` in another equation? Expected: `2x+1`.\n2. Solve `3x+1=10`. Expected: `x=3`.\n3. Evaluate `2(3x-4)` correctly. Expected: `6x-8`.\n4. What does the ordered pair `(3,7)` mean? Expected: `x=3`, `y=7`.\n5. What does a false equation like `2=8` mean? Expected: no value can make it true.","Core Concept":"Substitution works because equal things may replace each other.\n\nFor the system:\n\n`y = 2x + 1`\n\n`x + y = 10`\n\nthe first equation says that `y` and `2x+1` always match on that line. Replace `y` in the second equation:\n\n`x + (2x+1) = 10`\n\nNow there is only one variable. Solve for `x`, then use the isolated equation to find `y`.","Standard Method":"1. Pick an equation with a variable already isolated or easy to isolate.\n2. Substitute that expression into the other equation.\n3. Use parentheses around multi-term expressions.\n4. Solve the one-variable equation.\n5. Back-substitute to find the other variable.\n6. Check both original equations.\n7. Write the answer as `(x,y)`.","Already Isolated Variables":"Example:\n\n`y=2x+1`\n\n`x+y=10`\n\nSubstitute:\n\n`x+(2x+1)=10`\n\n`3x+1=10`\n\n`x=3`\n\nBack-substitute:\n\n`y=2(3)+1=7`\n\nSolution: `(3,7)`.","Isolate Before Substituting":"Sometimes neither equation is written with an isolated variable.\n\nExample:\n\n`x+y=9`\n\n`2x-y=3`\n\nFrom the first equation:\n\n`y=9-x`\n\nSubstitute:\n\n`2x-(9-x)=3`\n\n`2x-9+x=3`\n\n`3x=12`\n\n`x=4`\n\nThen `y=9-4=5`, so `(4,5)`.","Parentheses Matter":"If `y=3x-4` and the other equation uses `2y`, then write:\n\n`2(3x-4)`\n\nnot:\n\n`2(3x)-4`\n\nThe first multiplies the whole expression by 2. The second only multiplies the first term and changes the value.","Fractions and Decimals":"Fractions and decimals do not change the logic. They only require careful arithmetic.\n\nExample:\n\n`y=(1/2)x+2`\n\n`x+2y=12`\n\nSubstitute:\n\n`x+2((1/2)x+2)=12`\n\n`x+x+4=12`\n\n`x=4`\n\n`y=4`\n\nSolution: `(4,4)`.","Special Cases":"After substitution, the variable terms may cancel.\n\nIf the result is false, such as `2=8`, the system has no solution. The lines are parallel.\n\nIf the result is always true, such as `6x-4=6x-4`, the system has infinitely many solutions. The equations describe the same line.\n\nDo not write a single ordered pair for these cases.","Modeling With Substitution":"A context often gives one relationship equation and one total equation.\n\nExample:\n\nAdult tickets cost 4 more than student tickets:\n\n`a=s+4`\n\nThree student and two adult tickets cost 46:\n\n`3s+2a=46`\n\nSubstitute:\n\n`3s+2(s+4)=46`\n\n`5s+8=46`\n\n`s=7.60`\n\n`a=11.60`\n\nUse units in the final answer.","Strategy Choice":"Choose the variable that is easiest to isolate. A coefficient of 1 or -1 is usually best.\n\nFor `x+4y=14`, solving for `x` gives `x=14-4y` without fractions.\n\nFor `3x-2y=8`, solving for either variable introduces division. That may work, but it is less efficient.","Graph Connection":"The solution to a two-variable system is the point where the graphs intersect.\n\nFor:\n\n`y=x+2`\n\n`y=-2x+8`\n\nboth expressions equal `y`, so set them equal:\n\n`x+2=-2x+8`\n\n`x=2`\n\nThen `y=4`, so the intersection is `(2,4)`.","Checking Solutions":"Always check both original equations.\n\nFor `(3,7)` in `y=2x+1` and `x+y=10`:\n\n`7=2(3)+1`, true.\n\n`3+7=10`, true.\n\nChecking both equations catches reversed coordinates and arithmetic slips.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Substituting into the same equation used to isolate.\n  Correction: Substitute into the other equation.\n- Mistake: Dropping parentheses.\n  Correction: Put the entire replacement expression in parentheses before multiplying or subtracting.\n- Mistake: Stopping after finding one variable.\n  Correction: Back-substitute to find the ordered pair.\n- Mistake: Writing `(y,x)`.\n  Correction: Ordered pairs are always `(x,y)`.\n- Mistake: Calling `0=0` the point `(0,0)`.\n  Correction: A true identity means infinitely many solutions.\n- Mistake: Calling `2=8` a normal equation.\n  Correction: A false statement means no solution.","Guided Practice":"1. Solve `y=x+5` and `2x+y=14`.\n   - Substitute: `2x+(x+5)=14`.\n   - `3x=9`, so `x=3`.\n   - `y=8`.\n   - Solution: `(3,8)`.\n\n2. Solve `x+y=11` and `x-y=3`.\n   - From `x+y=11`, `x=11-y`.\n   - Substitute: `(11-y)-y=3`.\n   - `11-2y=3`, so `y=4`.\n   - `x=7`.\n   - Solution: `(7,4)`.\n\n3. Classify `y=4x+1` and `2y=8x+7`.\n   - Substitute: `2(4x+1)=8x+7`.\n   - `8x+2=8x+7`.\n   - `2=7`, false.\n   - No solution.","Independent Practice":"1. `y=x+1` and `x+y=9`; answer `(4,5)`.\n2. `x=2y+3` and `x+y=12`; answer `(9,3)`.\n3. `y=-x+6` and `2x+y=10`; answer `(4,2)`.\n4. `x+y=15` and `x=2y`; answer `(10,5)`.\n5. `y=2x-1` and `4y=8x-4`; infinitely many solutions.\n6. `y=2x-1` and `4y=8x+4`; no solution.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Choose a useful variable to isolate.\n2. Substitute into the other equation.\n3. Use parentheses correctly.\n4. Solve the resulting one-variable equation.\n5. Back-substitute and write `(x,y)`.\n6. Classify no-solution and infinitely-many-solution cases.\n7. Build and solve a context system.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `y=3x-2`, `x+y=10`; solution `(3,7)`.\n2. `x-2y=1`, `x+y=10`; solution `(7,3)`.\n3. `y=-2x+5`, `3x+y=9`; solution `(4,-3)`.\n4. `y=x+4`, `2y=2x+8`; infinitely many solutions.\n5. `y=x+4`, `2y=2x+10`; no solution.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player substitutes into the same equation: \"Use the expression from one equation inside the other equation so the two clues interact.\"\n- If parentheses are missing: \"The entire expression replaces the variable. Wrap it before multiplying or subtracting.\"\n- If only one coordinate is found: \"A system solution is an ordered pair. Back-substitute to find the second coordinate.\"\n- If coordinates are reversed: \"Read the pair as `(x,y)`: first coordinate for `x`, second for `y`.\"\n- If a contradiction appears: \"When variables cancel and the statement is false, the lines never meet.\"\n- If an identity appears: \"When variables cancel and the statement is always true, both equations describe the same line.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready to compare substitution with elimination and graphing.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P011\n- Estimated duration: 6 minutes\n- Target player state: knows linear equations and ordered pairs\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 011 question\n- Remediation trigger: two parenthesis errors, two incomplete ordered-pair answers, one special-case classification error, or repeated coordinate reversal\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed substitution systems, including at least one special-case classification and one modeling item","tutorialId":"Tut-P011","phaseId":"P011","title":"Systems by substitution"},"Tut-P012":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to solve systems of linear equations by elimination: combining equations so one variable disappears and the system becomes one equation in one variable.","Why It Matters":"Elimination is powerful when equations are in standard form, especially in comparison, bundle, and balance problems. It also prepares players for matrices, linear combinations, and later algebra where combining equations reveals hidden structure.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. What is `3y + (-3y)`? Expected: `0`.\n2. What is `(4x+2y)-(x+2y)`? Expected: `3x`.\n3. If an equation is multiplied by 2, what must be multiplied? Expected: every term on both sides.\n4. What does the ordered pair `(5,1)` mean? Expected: `x=5`, `y=1`.\n5. What does `0=4` mean? Expected: impossible, so no solution.","Core Concept":"Elimination removes a variable by combining equations.\n\nFor:\n\n`2x + y = 11`\n\n`3x - y = 14`\n\nthe `y` terms are opposites. Add the equations:\n\n`5x = 25`\n\n`x = 5`\n\nThen back-substitute:\n\n`2(5)+y=11`\n\n`y=1`\n\nSolution: `(5,1)`.","Direct Addition":"Use addition when one variable has opposite coefficients.\n\nExample:\n\n`3x+4y=18`\n\n`-3x+y=-3`\n\nAdd:\n\n`5y=15`\n\n`y=3`\n\nThen `x=2`, so `(2,3)`.","Direct Subtraction":"Use subtraction when one variable has matching coefficients.\n\nExample:\n\n`4x+2y=18`\n\n`x+2y=9`\n\nSubtract the second equation from the first:\n\n`3x=9`\n\n`x=3`\n\nThen `y=3`, so `(3,3)`.","Scaling One Equation":"If coefficients do not cancel yet, multiply one entire equation.\n\nExample:\n\n`x+2y=11`\n\n`3x+y=13`\n\nMultiply the first equation by `-3`:\n\n`-3x-6y=-33`\n\nAdd:\n\n`-5y=-20`\n\n`y=4`\n\nThen `x=3`.","Scaling Both Equations":"Sometimes both equations need new coefficients.\n\nExample:\n\n`2x+3y=13`\n\n`3x-2y=0`\n\nMake `+6y` and `-6y`:\n\n`4x+6y=26`\n\n`9x-6y=0`\n\nAdd:\n\n`13x=26`\n\n`x=2`\n\nThen `y=3`.","Strategy Choice":"Look for:\n\n1. Opposite coefficients: add.\n2. Matching coefficients: subtract.\n3. A small multiplier that creates opposites.\n4. A variable with smaller least common multiple.\n\nEliminate the variable that creates the least arithmetic.","Sign Care":"Signs determine the operation.\n\n- `+3y` and `-3y`: add.\n- `+3y` and `+3y`: subtract.\n- `-3y` and `-3y`: subtract.\n\nAfter combining, keep the sign of the remaining terms carefully.","Fractions and Decimals":"If a fraction appears, clear it first when helpful.\n\nExample:\n\n`(1/2)x+y=5`\n\n`x-y=1`\n\nMultiply the first equation by 2:\n\n`x+2y=10`\n\nSubtract `x-y=1`:\n\n`3y=9`\n\n`y=3`\n\nThen `x=4`.","Special Cases":"If elimination removes both variables:\n\n- False statement, such as `0=4`: no solution.\n- True statement, such as `0=0`: infinitely many solutions.\n\nThese are not single ordered pairs.","Modeling With Elimination":"Elimination works naturally when two context equations share one count.\n\nExample:\n\n`3p+2m=13`\n\n`5p+2m=19`\n\nBoth have `2m`. Subtract:\n\n`2p=6`\n\n`p=3`\n\nThen maps cost `$2`.","Graph Connection":"The solution of a linear system is the graph intersection.\n\nElimination finds the same intersection without drawing the graph. For `x+y=7` and `x-y=1`, adding gives `2x=8`, so the intersection has `x=4`. Then `y=3`, so the intersection is `(4,3)`.","Equation Battle Elimination":"Legal elimination moves preserve the solution set:\n\n- Multiply an entire equation by a nonzero number.\n- Add or subtract entire equations.\n- Replace one equation with the result of a legal combination.\n\nIllegal moves include changing only one term, adding only left sides, or forgetting to combine constants.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Adding matching coefficients.\n  Correction: Matching terms cancel by subtraction.\n- Mistake: Subtracting opposite coefficients.\n  Correction: Opposite terms cancel by addition.\n- Mistake: Multiplying only one term.\n  Correction: Multiply the entire equation.\n- Mistake: Forgetting the constant when scaling.\n  Correction: Scale both sides.\n- Mistake: Stopping after `x=...`.\n  Correction: Back-substitute for the other variable.\n- Mistake: Calling `0=0` the point `(0,0)`.\n  Correction: `0=0` means infinitely many solutions.","Guided Practice":"1. Solve `x+y=9` and `x-y=3`.\n   - Add: `2x=12`.\n   - `x=6`.\n   - `y=3`.\n   - Solution: `(6,3)`.\n\n2. Solve `2x+3y=16` and `4x-3y=14`.\n   - Add: `6x=30`.\n   - `x=5`.\n   - `y=2`.\n   - Solution: `(5,2)`.\n\n3. Classify `2x+y=5` and `4x+2y=14`.\n   - Multiply first by `-2`: `-4x-2y=-10`.\n   - Add: `0=4`.\n   - No solution.","Independent Practice":"1. `x+y=8`, `x-y=2`; answer `(5,3)`.\n2. `2x+y=12`, `3x-y=8`; answer `(4,4)`.\n3. `4x+2y=20`, `x+2y=8`; answer `(4,2)`.\n4. `2x+3y=14`, `4x-3y=10`; answer `(4,2)`.\n5. `2x+y=6`, `4x+2y=12`; infinitely many solutions.\n6. `2x+y=6`, `4x+2y=13`; no solution.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Identify whether to add, subtract, or scale.\n2. Multiply entire equations correctly.\n3. Eliminate a chosen variable.\n4. Back-substitute and write `(x,y)`.\n5. Check both original equations.\n6. Classify contradiction and identity cases.\n7. Use elimination in a context problem.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `3x+y=13`, `x-y=3`; solution `(4,1)`.\n2. `2x+5y=21`, `2x-y=3`; solution `(3,3)`.\n3. `x+2y=10`, `3x+2y=18`; solution `(4,3)`.\n4. `2x+y=5`, `4x+2y=10`; infinitely many solutions.\n5. `2x+y=5`, `4x+2y=11`; no solution.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player adds when coefficients match: \"Matching terms cancel by subtraction; opposite terms cancel by addition.\"\n- If the player subtracts when coefficients are opposites: \"Opposite signs are ready to add to zero.\"\n- If scaling is partial: \"Scale the entire equation, including every term and the constant.\"\n- If the player stops after one variable: \"Back-substitute to complete the ordered pair.\"\n- If signs slip: \"Rewrite the combined equation slowly, term by term.\"\n- If special cases are confused: \"When both variables disappear, judge the remaining statement as true or false.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready to compare elimination with graphing systems.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P012\n- Estimated duration: 6 minutes\n- Target player state: knows substitution systems and linear equation solving\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 012 question\n- Remediation trigger: two wrong-operation errors, two partial-scaling errors, one special-case classification error, or repeated incomplete ordered-pair answers\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed elimination systems, including one scaling-both-equations item, one model, and one special-case classification","tutorialId":"Tut-P012","phaseId":"P012","title":"Systems by elimination"},"Tut-P013":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to solve linear systems by graphing: graph both equations on the same coordinate plane and interpret the intersection.","Why It Matters":"Graphing turns a system into a picture. It helps players see whether two rules meet once, never meet, or are actually the same rule. It is especially useful for modeling comparisons and for checking algebraic answers from substitution or elimination.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. What does the point `(2,5)` mean? Expected: `x=2`, `y=5`.\n2. What are the slope and y-intercept of `y=2x+1`? Expected: slope 2, y-intercept 1.\n3. What kind of line is `x=4`? Expected: vertical.\n4. What kind of line is `y=-3`? Expected: horizontal.\n5. What does it mean if two lines never cross? Expected: no shared solution.","Core Concept":"Each equation in a two-variable system graphs as a set of points.\n\nThe system solution is any point shared by both graphs.\n\nFor two nonparallel lines, the shared point is the intersection.\n\nExample:\n\n`y=x+1`\n\n`y=-x+5`\n\nThe lines cross at `(2,3)`, so the solution is `(2,3)`.","Reading Intersections":"To read an intersection:\n\n1. Locate the crossing point.\n2. Read the horizontal coordinate first.\n3. Read the vertical coordinate second.\n4. Write `(x,y)`.\n\nDo not reverse the order.","Graphing Slope-Intercept Systems":"For `y=mx+b`:\n\n1. Plot the y-intercept `b`.\n2. Use the slope `m` to plot another point.\n3. Draw the line.\n4. Repeat for the second equation.\n5. Read the intersection.\n\nExample:\n\n`y=2x+1`\n\n`y=-x+7`\n\nThe lines intersect at `(2,5)`.","Standard Form Graphing":"For `Ax+By=C`, either:\n\n- Solve for `y`, then graph with slope and intercept.\n- Find intercepts by setting `x=0` and `y=0`.\n\nExample:\n\n`x+y=6` becomes `y=6-x`.\n\n`x-y=2` becomes `y=x-2`.\n\nThe intersection is `(4,2)`.","Vertical and Horizontal Lines":"- `x=a` is vertical. Every point has x-coordinate `a`.\n- `y=b` is horizontal. Every point has y-coordinate `b`.\n\nThe system `x=3` and `y=-2` has solution `(3,-2)`.","Parallel and Overlapping Lines":"Two distinct parallel lines never intersect:\n\n`y=2x+1`\n\n`y=2x-4`\n\nNo solution.\n\nTwo identical lines overlap completely:\n\n`y=3x-2`\n\n`2y=6x-4`\n\nInfinitely many solutions.","Solution Types From Graphs":"- One intersection point: one solution.\n- Parallel distinct lines: no solution.\n- Same line: infinitely many solutions.\n\nThe number of drawn lines is not the number of solutions. Count shared points.","Approximate and Exact Solutions":"Some intersections do not land on grid points. A graph can estimate the solution.\n\nExample:\n\n`y=(1/2)x+1`\n\n`y=-x+5`\n\nThe graph suggests about `(2.67,2.33)`. Algebra gives the exact solution `(8/3,7/3)`.","Tables and Graphs":"Tables can help build a graph. Use the same x-values for both equations.\n\nFor:\n\n`y=x+2`\n\n`y=-2x+8`\n\nat `x=2`, both give `y=4`, so `(2,4)` is the intersection.","Window and Scale":"A graph window must show the intersection.\n\nFor:\n\n`y=10x+5`\n\n`y=-5x+50`\n\nthe intersection is `(3,35)`. A window with `0\u003c=y\u003c=10` hides it. Choose a window like `0\u003c=x\u003c=5`, `0\u003c=y\u003c=55`.","Checking Graphical Solutions":"After reading a point from a graph, check both equations.\n\nFor `(1,4)` in:\n\n`y=2x+2`\n\n`y=-x+5`\n\ncheck:\n\n`4=2(1)+2`\n\n`4=-1+5`\n\nBoth are true.","Modeling With Graphs":"Graphing shows when two quantities become equal.\n\nExample:\n\nPlan A: `y=10+2x`\n\nPlan B: `y=4+5x`\n\nThe intersection is `(2,14)`, meaning after 2 levels both plans cost 14 coins.","Matching Graphs and Equations":"To match a system to a graph:\n\n1. Compare y-intercepts.\n2. Compare slope direction.\n3. Estimate the intersection.\n4. Check that the displayed graph matches both equations.","Reverse Construction":"To create two lines through a target point:\n\n1. Pick two different slopes.\n2. Use the target point to find intercepts.\n3. Check the point in both equations.\n\nFor `(3,1)`, `y=x-2` and `y=-2x+7` both work.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Reversing coordinates.\n  Correction: Read horizontal first, vertical second.\n- Mistake: Reporting an intercept as the solution.\n  Correction: The solution is where both lines meet.\n- Mistake: Calling parallel lines two solutions.\n  Correction: Parallel distinct lines have no shared point.\n- Mistake: Calling overlapping lines one solution.\n  Correction: Overlapping lines share infinitely many points.\n- Mistake: Graphing `x=a` as horizontal.\n  Correction: `x=a` is vertical.\n- Mistake: Using a window that hides the crossing.\n  Correction: Estimate or solve first, then choose a fitting window.","Guided Practice":"1. Solve by graphing `y=x+1` and `y=-x+5`.\n   - Graph both lines.\n   - The crossing is `(2,3)`.\n\n2. Classify `y=2x+1` and `y=2x-4`.\n   - Same slope, different intercepts.\n   - No solution.\n\n3. Solve `x=4` and `y=2x-1`.\n   - The vertical line fixes `x=4`.\n   - The sloped line gives `y=7`.\n   - Solution `(4,7)`.","Independent Practice":"1. `y=x+2`, `y=-x+8`; answer `(3,5)`.\n2. `x+y=9`, `x-y=1`; answer `(5,4)`.\n3. `x=-2`, `y=3x+1`; answer `(-2,-5)`.\n4. `y=4`, `y=x-1`; answer `(5,4)`.\n5. `y=3x+1`, `y=3x-2`; no solution.\n6. `y=-x+6`, `2y=-2x+12`; infinitely many solutions.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Graph two slope-intercept lines.\n2. Graph standard-form lines by conversion or intercepts.\n3. Read intersections in `(x,y)` order.\n4. Classify crossing, parallel, and overlapping lines.\n5. Handle vertical and horizontal lines.\n6. Estimate non-grid intersections.\n7. Verify graph-read solutions algebraically.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `y=2x+1`, `y=-x+7`; solution `(2,5)`.\n2. `x+y=6`, `x-y=2`; solution `(4,2)`.\n3. `x=3`, `y=-2`; solution `(3,-2)`.\n4. `y=2x+1`, `y=2x-4`; no solution.\n5. `y=x-3`, `2y=2x-6`; infinitely many solutions.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player reverses coordinates: \"Read the graph left-right first for `x`, then up-down for `y`.\"\n- If the player chooses an intercept: \"The solution must be on both lines, not just where one line touches an axis.\"\n- If parallel lines are misclassified: \"Parallel distinct lines have no shared point.\"\n- If overlapping lines are called one solution: \"The lines share every point on the line, so there are infinitely many solutions.\"\n- If the graph window hides the answer: \"Estimate or solve roughly first, then resize the graph to include the intersection.\"\n- If the player relies on an approximate point: \"Use algebra to verify or refine the exact coordinates.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready to connect systems to function notation and graph-based domain/range thinking.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P013\n- Estimated duration: 6 minutes\n- Target player state: knows linear graphing and systems by substitution or elimination\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 013 question\n- Remediation trigger: two coordinate reversal errors, two intercept-as-solution errors, one solution-type classification error, or one hidden-window error\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on graphing systems including a standard-form graph, a vertical or horizontal line, a special-case classification, and an exact check","tutorialId":"Tut-P013","phaseId":"P013","title":"Systems by graphing"},"Tut-P014":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to read, evaluate, solve, and interpret function notation across formulas, tables, graphs, and contexts.","Why It Matters":"Function notation is the language of input and output. It lets a game, graph, table, or formula all say the same thing clearly: when this input goes in, that output comes out. Later topics such as domain, range, transformations, composition, inverses, and modeling all depend on this notation.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. In the point `(4,11)`, which coordinate is the input? Expected: 4.\n2. Evaluate `2(4)+3`. Expected: 11.\n3. What is `(-3)^2`? Expected: 9.\n4. What does a graph point `(-1,4)` say about input and output? Expected: input -1, output 4.\n5. Solve `2x+3=11`. Expected: `x=4`.","What f(x) Means":"`f(x)` is read \"f of x.\"\n\nIt does not mean `f` times `x`.\n\nThe letter `f` names the function. The value inside parentheses is the input. The function returns an output.\n\n`f(4)=11` means:\n\n- Function name: `f`\n- Input: `4`\n- Output: `11`\n- Graph point: `(4,11)`","Formula Evaluation":"To evaluate a formula:\n\n1. Identify the input.\n2. Replace every `x` with that input.\n3. Use parentheses when needed.\n4. Simplify.\n\nExample:\n\n`f(x)=2x+3`\n\n`f(4)=2(4)+3=11`","Parentheses and Signs":"Negative inputs need parentheses.\n\nFor `f(x)=x^2-2x`:\n\n`f(-3)=(-3)^2-2(-3)=9+6=15`\n\nWithout parentheses, the square and negative sign can be interpreted incorrectly.","Replace Every Input":"If `x` appears more than once, every `x` must be replaced.\n\nFor `f(x)=x^2+3x-4`:\n\n`f(2)=2^2+3(2)-4=6`\n\nReplacing only one `x` changes the rule.","Expression Inputs":"The input can be an expression.\n\nFor `f(x)=3x-5`:\n\n`f(t+2)=3(t+2)-5=3t+1`\n\nThe entire expression `t+2` replaces `x`.","Solving f(x)=k":"`f(x)=k` asks for input values that produce output `k`.\n\nFor `f(x)=2x+3`, solving `f(x)=11` means:\n\n`2x+3=11`\n\n`x=4`\n\nFor `f(x)=x^2`, solving `f(x)=9` gives `x=-3` or `x=3`.","Tables and Ordered Pairs":"A table lists input-output pairs.\n\nIf a table row says `x=3` and `f(x)=11`, then:\n\n`f(3)=11`\n\nand the graph point is `(3,11)`.\n\nTo solve from a table, look for the output. If `f(x)=1` appears at inputs 0 and 2, then both inputs are solutions.","Graphs and Function Values":"On a graph, the x-coordinate is the input and the y-coordinate is the output.\n\nIf the graph contains `(-1,4)`, then `f(-1)=4`.\n\nTo solve `f(x)=4`, look for all points on the graph with y-coordinate 4.","Comparing Function Values":"To compare `f(3)` and `g(3)`:\n\n1. Evaluate `f` at 3.\n2. Evaluate `g` at 3.\n3. Compare the outputs.\n\nThe input must be the same for both functions.","Expression Inputs and Rates":"Function notation can describe changes.\n\nFor `f(x)=2x+3`:\n\n`f(x+h)=2(x+h)+3=2x+2h+3`\n\nThe difference quotient:\n\n`(f(x+h)-f(x))/h`\n\nsimplifies to 2 when `h` is not 0.","Context Meaning":"In context, function notation carries units.\n\nIf `h(t)` gives height in meters after `t` seconds, then `h(3)=45` means:\n\nAfter 3 seconds, the height is 45 meters.\n\nThe input unit is seconds. The output unit is meters.","Reverse Construction":"To create a function with `f(2)=7`, choose a rule that outputs 7 at input 2.\n\nExample:\n\n`f(x)=3x+1`\n\nCheck:\n\n`f(2)=3(2)+1=7`","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Reading `f(3)` as multiplication.\n  Correction: `f` is the function name; 3 is the input.\n- Mistake: Replacing only one `x`.\n  Correction: Replace every occurrence of the input variable.\n- Mistake: Missing parentheses for negative inputs.\n  Correction: Write `(-2)^2` when the input is `-2`.\n- Mistake: Solving `f(x)=11` by evaluating `f(11)`.\n  Correction: Set the formula equal to 11 and solve for the input.\n- Mistake: Reversing graph coordinates.\n  Correction: Function notation `f(a)=b` corresponds to `(a,b)`.","Guided Practice":"1. If `f(x)=4x-1`, find `f(3)`.\n   - `4(3)-1=11`.\n\n2. If `f(x)=x^2+2`, find `f(-2)`.\n   - `(-2)^2+2=6`.\n\n3. If `f(x)=5x+1`, solve `f(x)=16`.\n   - `5x+1=16`.\n   - `x=3`.\n\n4. If `f(6)=-2`, write the ordered pair.\n   - `(6,-2)`.","Independent Practice":"1. `f(x)=3x+2`; find `f(5)`. Answer: `17`.\n2. `g(x)=x^2-1`; find `g(-4)`. Answer: `15`.\n3. `h(x)=2x-7`; solve `h(x)=9`. Answer: `x=8`.\n4. Table has `x: 1,2,3` and `p(x): 4,6,8`; find `p(2)`. Answer: `6`.\n5. Graph contains `(5,-1)`; find `q(5)`. Answer: `-1`.\n6. Write `r(-3)=10` as a point. Answer: `(-3,10)`.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Identify function name, input, and output.\n2. Evaluate formulas at numbers and expressions.\n3. Use parentheses for negative inputs.\n4. Solve `f(x)=k`.\n5. Read function values from tables and graphs.\n6. Convert between `f(a)=b` and `(a,b)`.\n7. Interpret function notation with units.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `f(x)=2x+5`; `f(6)=17`.\n2. `g(x)=x^2+3x`; `g(-2)=-2`.\n3. `h(x)=4x-1`; solve `h(x)=15`; answer `x=4`.\n4. If `p(7)=12`, the graph point is `(7,12)`.\n5. If `A(t)` is area in square meters after `t` seconds, `A(5)=20` means area is 20 square meters after 5 seconds.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player treats `f(3)` as multiplication: \"The letter names the function; the number inside parentheses is the input.\"\n- If only one `x` is replaced: \"Every occurrence of the input variable receives the same input.\"\n- If a negative input is mishandled: \"Wrap the input in parentheses before applying powers or multiplication.\"\n- If `f(x)=k` is evaluated as `f(k)`: \"The output is already known; solve for the input.\"\n- If a table or graph value is reversed: \"Function notation follows `(input, output)`.\"\n- If context units are missing: \"Name what the input measures and what the output measures.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready for domain restrictions, where not every input is allowed.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P014\n- Estimated duration: 6 minutes\n- Target player state: knows substitution, linear equations, and coordinate points\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 014 question\n- Remediation trigger: two multiplication-notation errors, two negative-input errors, two graph/table reversal errors, or one repeated `f(x)=k` solving confusion\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed formula, table, graph, context, and solve-output function notation tasks","tutorialId":"Tut-P014","phaseId":"P014","title":"Function notation"},"Tut-P015":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to find the domain of a function from its formula by identifying which real inputs are allowed and which inputs break the rule.","Why It Matters":"Domain is the set of legal inputs. In a game, it prevents impossible moves. In math, it prevents division by zero, square roots of negative values, and context errors such as negative lengths. Later graph, range, rational, radical, logarithmic, and inverse-function work depends on reading domains correctly.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. Why is `1/0` undefined? Expected: division by zero is not allowed.\n2. Is `sqrt(0)` defined? Expected: yes.\n3. Is `sqrt(-4)` real? Expected: no.\n4. Solve `x-3=0`. Expected: `x=3`.\n5. Solve `x-5 \u003e= 0`. Expected: `x \u003e= 5`.","Core Concept":"Start with all real numbers. Then remove or restrict inputs that make the formula impossible.\n\nMain restrictions:\n\n1. Denominators cannot be zero.\n2. Even-root radicands must be nonnegative.\n3. Even roots in denominators must be positive.\n4. Context may restrict inputs beyond algebra.","No-Restriction Formulas":"Polynomials and absolute value expressions usually have all real numbers as their algebraic domain.\n\nExamples:\n\n`f(x)=3x^2-5x+1`\n\nDomain: `(-infinity, infinity)`.\n\n`g(x)=|2x-7|+4`\n\nDomain: `(-infinity, infinity)`.","Denominator Restrictions":"For rational formulas, set the denominator not equal to zero.\n\nExample:\n\n`f(x)=5/(x-3)`\n\nDenominator: `x-3`\n\nForbidden: `x-3=0`, so `x=3`\n\nDomain: `(-infinity,3) union (3,infinity)`.","Factored Denominators":"If the denominator factors, exclude every zero of every factor.\n\nExample:\n\n`4/(x^2-9)`\n\n`x^2-9=(x-3)(x+3)`\n\nExclude `x=3` and `x=-3`.","Denominators That Never Vanish":"Some denominators are never zero for real inputs.\n\nExample:\n\n`1/(x^2+4)`\n\nSince `x^2 \u003e= 0`, `x^2+4 \u003e= 4`. The denominator never equals zero.\n\nDomain: all real numbers.","Square-Root Restrictions":"For square roots, require the radicand to be nonnegative.\n\nExample:\n\n`sqrt(x-5)`\n\n`x-5 \u003e= 0`\n\n`x \u003e= 5`\n\nDomain: `[5,infinity)`.\n\nFor `sqrt(7-x)`:\n\n`7-x \u003e= 0`\n\n`x \u003c= 7`\n\nDomain: `(-infinity,7]`.","Roots in Denominators":"If a square root is in the denominator, the radicand must be positive.\n\nExample:\n\n`1/sqrt(x-2)`\n\nNeed `x-2 \u003e 0`.\n\nDomain: `(2,infinity)`.\n\nThe endpoint is excluded because it makes the denominator zero.","Quadratic Radicands":"For `sqrt(x^2-9)`, require:\n\n`x^2-9 \u003e= 0`\n\n`(x-3)(x+3) \u003e= 0`\n\nThe product is nonnegative outside the roots.\n\nDomain: `(-infinity,-3] union [3,infinity)`.","Odd Roots":"Odd roots, such as cube roots, allow negative radicands.\n\n`cuberoot(x-4)` has domain `(-infinity, infinity)`.\n\nDo not apply square-root rules to odd roots.","Combining Restrictions":"When a formula has more than one restriction, keep only inputs that satisfy all restrictions.\n\nExample:\n\n`sqrt(x+1)/(x-3)`\n\nSquare root: `x \u003e= -1`\n\nDenominator: `x != 3`\n\nDomain: `[-1,3) union (3,infinity)`.","Original Formula Restrictions":"Simplifying a rational expression does not erase original restrictions.\n\nExample:\n\n`(x^2-9)/(x-3)`\n\nThis simplifies to `x+3`, but the original formula had denominator zero at `x=3`.\n\nDomain: `x != 3`.","Context Domains":"Context can restrict inputs.\n\n`A(s)=s^2` algebraically accepts all real numbers. But if `s` is side length, then `s \u003e= 0`.\n\nAlways ask what the input represents.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Excluding numerator zeros.\n  Correction: Numerator zeros are allowed unless another rule forbids them.\n- Mistake: Allowing denominator zero after cancellation.\n  Correction: Record original restrictions first.\n- Mistake: Using `\u003e` for square-root radicands in the numerator.\n  Correction: Use `\u003e=`; square root of 0 is allowed.\n- Mistake: Using `\u003e=` for square roots in denominators.\n  Correction: Use `\u003e`; denominator cannot be zero.\n- Mistake: Combining restrictions with union instead of overlap.\n  Correction: The domain must satisfy every restriction at once.\n- Mistake: Ignoring context.\n  Correction: Algebraic domain and context domain can differ.","Guided Practice":"1. Find the domain of `f(x)=1/(x+4)`.\n   - Denominator `x+4 != 0`.\n   - `x != -4`.\n   - Domain: `(-infinity,-4) union (-4,infinity)`.\n\n2. Find the domain of `g(x)=sqrt(x+2)`.\n   - `x+2 \u003e= 0`.\n   - `x \u003e= -2`.\n   - Domain: `[-2,infinity)`.\n\n3. Find the domain of `h(x)=sqrt(x-1)/(x+3)`.\n   - `x \u003e= 1`.\n   - `x != -3`.\n   - Since `-3` is outside `x \u003e= 1`, domain is `[1,infinity)`.","Independent Practice":"1. `f(x)=x^3-2x`; answer `(-infinity, infinity)`.\n2. `g(x)=1/(x+5)`; answer `x != -5`.\n3. `h(x)=sqrt(x-4)`; answer `[4,infinity)`.\n4. `p(x)=sqrt(10-x)`; answer `(-infinity,10]`.\n5. `q(x)=1/sqrt(x+1)`; answer `(-1,infinity)`.\n6. `r(x)=(x+2)/(x^2-16)`; answer `x != -4, 4`.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Identify denominator restrictions.\n2. Identify square-root restrictions.\n3. Distinguish numerator roots from denominator roots.\n4. Preserve original restrictions after cancellation.\n5. Combine multiple restrictions by intersection.\n6. Write domains in interval notation.\n7. Apply context restrictions.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `f(x)=4x^2+1`; domain `(-infinity, infinity)`.\n2. `g(x)=3/(x-8)`; domain `x != 8`.\n3. `h(x)=sqrt(2x-10)`; domain `[5,infinity)`.\n4. `p(x)=1/sqrt(6-x)`; domain `(-infinity,6)`.\n5. `q(x)=sqrt(x+2)/(x-1)`; domain `[-2,1) union (1,infinity)`.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player excludes numerator zeros: \"Zeros are allowed in the numerator; they only matter in the denominator or under an even root.\"\n- If denominator restrictions are missed: \"Ask what would make the denominator equal zero.\"\n- If square-root endpoints are excluded incorrectly: \"Square root of 0 is defined unless the root is in the denominator.\"\n- If a square-root denominator endpoint is included: \"That would make the denominator zero, so it must be excluded.\"\n- If restrictions are not combined: \"The domain must pass every restriction at the same time.\"\n- If cancellation erases a restriction: \"Record original denominator restrictions before simplifying.\"\n- If context is ignored: \"What does the input represent in the real situation?\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P015\n- Estimated duration: 7 minutes\n- Target player state: knows function notation, inequalities, interval notation, and basic factoring\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 015 question\n- Remediation trigger: two denominator-zero errors, two square-root endpoint errors, one lost-canceled-restriction error, or one repeated interval-notation error\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed polynomial, rational, radical, combined, simplified, and context domain tasks","tutorialId":"Tut-P015","phaseId":"P015","title":"Domain from formulas"},"Tut-P016":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to find the domain of a function from its graph by reading where the graph exists along the x-axis.","Why It Matters":"Graphs reveal allowed inputs visually. Endpoints, holes, arrows, and gaps show the same domain ideas that formulas express with algebra. This helps players connect symbolic restrictions to visual behavior and prepares them for range, transformations, piecewise functions, rational holes, and asymptotes.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. Does domain use x-values or y-values? Expected: x-values.\n2. What does a closed dot mean? Expected: included.\n3. What does an open dot mean? Expected: excluded.\n4. What does an arrow mean on a graph? Expected: the graph continues.\n5. What does union mean? Expected: combine separate pieces.","Core Concept":"The domain of a graph is the set of all x-values where the graph has at least one point.\n\nTo find it:\n\n1. Scan left to right.\n2. Identify where the graph starts and stops.\n3. Check endpoint types.\n4. Look for arrows, holes, gaps, and isolated points.\n5. Write the x-values in interval or set notation.","Endpoints":"Closed dots include endpoint x-values.\n\nOpen dots exclude endpoint x-values.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Closed segment from `x=-2` to `x=5`: `[-2,5]`\n- Open segment from `x=-2` to `x=5`: `(-2,5)`\n- Closed at `-3`, open at `4`: `[-3,4)`","Arrows and Infinity":"An arrow means the graph continues forever in that direction.\n\n- Closed dot at `x=1`, arrow right: `[1,infinity)`\n- Open dot at `x=4`, arrow left: `(-infinity,4)`\n- Arrows on both ends: `(-infinity, infinity)`\n\nDo not use the edge of the graph window as an endpoint when arrows are shown.","Domain Versus Range":"Domain uses x-values.\n\nRange uses y-values.\n\nA parabola with arrows on both arms may have a lowest y-value, but its domain is still all real numbers because it continues left and right.","Radical-Style Graphs":"A square-root-style graph often begins at a starting x-value and continues right.\n\nIf it starts with a closed dot at `x=2` and continues right, the domain is `[2,infinity)`.","Holes and Asymptotes":"A hole removes a point if no filled point exists at the same x-value.\n\nA vertical asymptote often removes an x-value.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Hole at `x=1`: domain excludes 1.\n- Vertical asymptote at `x=3`: domain excludes 3.","Holes and Filled Points":"If a graph has a hole at x=2 but also has a filled point at x=2, then x=2 is in the domain.\n\nDomain asks whether any point exists at that x-value, not whether every visual branch has a point there.","Multiple Pieces":"Separate graph pieces require union notation.\n\nExample:\n\nClosed segment from `x=-4` to `x=-1` and closed segment from `x=2` to `x=6`:\n\n`[-4,-1] union [2,6]`\n\nDo not fill the gap between pieces.","Isolated Points":"Discrete points contribute their x-values.\n\nFor points `(-3,2)`, `(-1,5)`, `(2,0)`, and `(4,6)`, the domain is:\n\n`{-3,-1,2,4}`","Context Graphs":"In context, the x-axis usually has units.\n\nIf a height-time graph starts at `t=0` and ends at `t=12`, the domain is `[0,12]` seconds.\n\nInclude units when helpful.","Window Versus Domain":"A graph may be displayed only inside a viewing window.\n\nIf arrows show the graph continues beyond the visible window, the domain extends beyond what is currently shown.\n\nWindow edges are not endpoints unless the graph actually stops there.","Matching Graphs and Domains":"To match a graph to interval notation:\n\n1. Identify leftmost and rightmost x-values.\n2. Check open or closed endpoint symbols.\n3. Check for arrows.\n4. Check for holes and gaps.\n5. Use union if there are separate pieces.","Reverse Construction":"To draw a graph with domain `[-1,4) union {6}`:\n\n1. Draw any segment or curve from a closed point at x=-1 to an open point at x=4.\n2. Leave a gap.\n3. Add a filled point at x=6.\n\nThe y-values can vary; the domain controls only x-values.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Reading y-values.\n  Correction: Project graph points onto the x-axis.\n- Mistake: Using graph window edges as endpoints.\n  Correction: Look for arrows.\n- Mistake: Filling gaps between pieces.\n  Correction: Use union notation.\n- Mistake: Ignoring isolated points.\n  Correction: Add their x-values with braces.\n- Mistake: Excluding a hole x-value even when a filled point exists there.\n  Correction: Domain includes any x-value with at least one filled point.\n- Mistake: Reversing endpoint symbols.\n  Correction: Closed means bracket; open means parenthesis.","Guided Practice":"1. A graph runs from a closed point at `x=-1` to an open point at `x=4`.\n   - Domain: `[-1,4)`.\n\n2. A graph has a vertical asymptote at `x=2` and branches on both sides.\n   - Domain: `(-infinity,2) union (2,infinity)`.\n\n3. A graph has points `(-2,5)`, `(0,1)`, and `(3,4)`.\n   - Domain: `{-2,0,3}`.","Independent Practice":"1. Closed segment from `x=0` to `x=7`; answer `[0,7]`.\n2. Open segment from `x=-3` to `x=2`; answer `(-3,2)`.\n3. Closed dot at `x=-4`, arrow right; answer `[-4,infinity)`.\n4. Arrows both directions with a hole at `x=6`; answer `(-infinity,6) union (6,infinity)`.\n5. Points at x-values `-1`, `2`, and `5`; answer `{-1,2,5}`.\n6. Pieces `[-5,-2)` and `(1,4]`; answer `[-5,-2) union (1,4]`.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Use x-values only for domain.\n2. Interpret open and closed endpoints.\n3. Use arrows to include infinity.\n4. Exclude holes or asymptotes correctly.\n5. Include filled points and isolated points.\n6. Use union for separate pieces.\n7. Avoid graph-window traps.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. Closed segment from `x=-3` to `x=2`; domain `[-3,2]`.\n2. Open left endpoint at `x=1`, arrow right; domain `(1,infinity)`.\n3. Branches on both sides of asymptote `x=-4`; domain `(-infinity,-4) union (-4,infinity)`.\n4. Discrete points with x-values `0`, `3`, `8`; domain `{0,3,8}`.\n5. Piece `[-2,0)` plus ray `[5,infinity)`; domain `[-2,0) union [5,infinity)`.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player gives y-values: \"Domain is the graph\u0027s x-shadow, not its height.\"\n- If endpoint symbols are wrong: \"Open dots use parentheses; closed dots use brackets.\"\n- If arrows are ignored: \"The graph continues beyond the window in the arrow direction.\"\n- If a gap is filled: \"The graph has no points over that gap, so use separate intervals.\"\n- If isolated points are missed: \"A single filled point still adds its x-value to the domain.\"\n- If holes are over-removed: \"Check whether another filled point exists at the same x-value.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready to read range from graphs using the same visual logic vertically.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P016\n- Estimated duration: 6 minutes\n- Target player state: knows formula domain, graphing basics, and interval notation\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 016 question\n- Remediation trigger: two domain-range reversals, two endpoint-symbol errors, one graph-window error, or one repeated gap/hole error\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed endpoint, arrow, hole, asymptote, discrete, context, and multi-piece graph domains","tutorialId":"Tut-P016","phaseId":"P016","title":"Domain from graphs"},"Tut-P017":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to find the range of a function from its graph by reading where the graph exists vertically along the y-axis.","Why It Matters":"Range tells which outputs are possible. In games and models, range answers questions like \"What scores can happen?\", \"What heights are reached?\", and \"What values are impossible?\" It is the vertical partner of domain.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. Does range use x-values or y-values? Expected: y-values.\n2. What does a closed endpoint mean? Expected: included.\n3. What does an open endpoint mean? Expected: excluded unless another filled point has that y-value.\n4. What is the range of a horizontal line at y=3? Expected: `{3}`.\n5. What does union mean? Expected: combine separate pieces.","Core Concept":"The range of a graph is the set of all y-values where the graph has at least one point.\n\nTo find it:\n\n1. Scan bottom to top.\n2. Identify the lowest and highest output values.\n3. Check whether endpoints are open or closed.\n4. Look for arrows, holes, gaps, and isolated points.\n5. Write the y-values in interval or set notation.","Reading Range":"Project the graph onto the y-axis.\n\nClosed lowest y=-2 and closed highest y=5 gives:\n\n`[-2,5]`\n\nOpen lowest y=-2 and open highest y=5 gives:\n\n`(-2,5)`","Endpoint Inclusion":"Use brackets when the graph includes the endpoint y-value.\n\nUse parentheses when the graph approaches but does not include the endpoint y-value.\n\nIf another filled point exists at the same y-value, that y-value is included.","Arrows and Unbounded Range":"An upward arrow means y-values continue toward `infinity`.\n\nA downward arrow means y-values continue toward `-infinity`.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Closed minimum y=1 and arrow upward: `[1,infinity)`\n- Open maximum y=4 and arrow downward: `(-infinity,4)`","Common Graph Families":"- Nonhorizontal line with arrows: range `(-infinity, infinity)`.\n- Horizontal line at y=3: range `{3}`.\n- Upward parabola with vertex y=-4: `[-4,infinity)`.\n- Downward parabola with vertex y=6: `(-infinity,6]`.\n- Square-root-style graph starting at y=0 and rising: `[0,infinity)`.","Holes and Asymptotes in Range":"A missing point may remove a y-value only if no other point on the graph has that y-value.\n\nA horizontal asymptote can mark a y-value the graph approaches but never reaches.\n\nFor a reciprocal-style graph approaching `y=0` without touching it:\n\n`(-infinity,0) union (0,infinity)`","Holes With Replacement Values":"If there is a hole at y=2 but a filled point elsewhere with y=2, then y=2 is in the range.\n\nRange asks whether at least one point has that output.","Discrete Graphs":"For discrete points, range is the set of y-coordinates.\n\nFor points `(-3,2)`, `(-1,5)`, `(2,0)`, `(4,6)`:\n\nRange: `{0,2,5,6}`","Multiple Range Bands":"Separate vertical output bands require union notation.\n\nIf outputs are from -4 to -1 and from 2 to 6:\n\n`[-4,-1] union [2,6]`\n\nDo not fill the vertical gap.","Context Ranges":"In context, include units.\n\nIf a height-time graph has minimum height 0 meters and maximum height 30 meters, the range is:\n\n`[0,30]` meters.","Matching Graphs and Ranges":"To match a graph to range notation:\n\n1. Find lowest and highest y-values.\n2. Check endpoint inclusion.\n3. Check arrows and asymptotes.\n4. Check holes and isolated points.\n5. Use union for separated vertical bands.","Reverse Construction":"To draw a graph with range `[-1,4) union {6}`:\n\n1. Make any graph piece whose y-values cover -1 included through 4 excluded.\n2. Add a filled point at any x-value with y=6.\n3. Leave y-values between 4 and 6 missing.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Giving x-values.\n  Correction: Range is the graph\u0027s vertical shadow.\n- Mistake: Giving all real range for a horizontal line.\n  Correction: A horizontal line has one y-value.\n- Mistake: Filling vertical gaps.\n  Correction: Use union notation.\n- Mistake: Ignoring a horizontal asymptote.\n  Correction: Exclude y-values the graph never reaches.\n- Mistake: Removing a y-value despite a filled replacement point.\n  Correction: A single filled point restores that y-value.\n- Mistake: Confusing graph window with range.\n  Correction: Follow arrows.","Guided Practice":"1. A graph has closed lowest y=-1 and open highest y=4.\n   - Range: `[-1,4)`.\n\n2. A downward parabola has vertex y=10.\n   - Range: `(-infinity,10]`.\n\n3. A graph has discrete points with y-values 1, 3, and 3.\n   - Range: `{1,3}`.","Independent Practice":"1. Closed vertical range from y=0 to y=7; answer `[0,7]`.\n2. Open low y=-3 and closed high y=2; answer `(-3,2]`.\n3. Upward parabola with vertex y=-5; answer `[-5,infinity)`.\n4. Horizontal line y=-4; answer `{-4}`.\n5. Reciprocal graph with horizontal asymptote y=2 and branches above and below; answer `(-infinity,2) union (2,infinity)`.\n6. Output bands `[-2,0]` and `[4,9)`; answer `[-2,0] union [4,9)`.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Use y-values only for range.\n2. Interpret open and closed y-endpoints.\n3. Use arrows to identify unbounded outputs.\n4. Read ranges of common graph families.\n5. Handle holes, asymptotes, and filled replacement points.\n6. Use union notation for separate output bands.\n7. Include context units.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. Closed range from y=-3 to y=2; answer `[-3,2]`.\n2. Open maximum y=5 with arrow downward; answer `(-infinity,5)`.\n3. Upward parabola vertex y=1; answer `[1,infinity)`.\n4. Discrete points with y-values 0, 3, 8; answer `{0,3,8}`.\n5. Bands `[-2,0)` and `[5,infinity)`; answer `[-2,0) union [5,infinity)`.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player gives x-values: \"Range is vertical; use y-values.\"\n- If endpoint symbols are wrong: \"Closed output values use brackets; open output values use parentheses.\"\n- If a horizontal line is called all real: \"A horizontal line has one output, even though it has many inputs.\"\n- If the player gives domain of a parabola: \"A parabola may use all x-values, but range depends on its vertex and opening direction.\"\n- If a y-gap is filled: \"No graph points reach those output values, so use separate intervals.\"\n- If a hole is over-removed: \"Check whether another filled point has the same y-value.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready to use range ideas in transformations and graph analysis.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P017\n- Estimated duration: 6 minutes\n- Target player state: knows domain from graphs, function notation, and interval notation\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 017 question\n- Remediation trigger: two domain-range reversals, two endpoint-symbol errors, one horizontal-line range error, or one repeated hole/asymptote error\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed endpoint, arrow, parabola, horizontal-line, hole, discrete, context, and multi-band range tasks","tutorialId":"Tut-P017","phaseId":"P017","title":"Range from graphs"},"Tut-P018":{"Learning Goal":"Learn how formulas like `f(x-3)+2`, `-f(x)`, and `2f(x)` move, flip, stretch, and compress function graphs.","Why It Matters":"Transformations let players build complex graphs from familiar parent functions. They are the bridge from function notation to graph design, modeling, inverse functions, piecewise functions, quadratics, and trigonometric graphs.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. Does `f(x)+3` change inputs or outputs? Expected: outputs.\n2. Does `f(x-3)` change inputs or outputs? Expected: inputs.\n3. What point results from shifting `(2,5)` right 4? Expected: `(6,5)`.\n4. What point results from reflecting `(2,5)` over the x-axis? Expected: `(2,-5)`.\n5. What point results from reflecting `(2,5)` over the y-axis? Expected: `(-2,5)`.","Core Concept":"Transformations change a graph by changing inputs or outputs.\n\nOutside changes affect y-values directly.\n\nInside changes affect x-values, often in the opposite-looking direction.","Vertical Shifts":"`g(x)=f(x)+k` shifts the graph up `k`.\n\n`g(x)=f(x)-k` shifts the graph down `k`.\n\nPoint rule:\n\n`(x,y) -\u003e (x,y+k)`","Horizontal Shifts":"`g(x)=f(x-h)` shifts the graph right `h`.\n\n`g(x)=f(x+h)` shifts the graph left `h`.\n\nPoint rule:\n\nRight `h`: `(x,y) -\u003e (x+h,y)`\n\nLeft `h`: `(x,y) -\u003e (x-h,y)`","Reflections":"`g(x)=-f(x)` reflects over the x-axis.\n\nPoint rule:\n\n`(x,y) -\u003e (x,-y)`\n\n`g(x)=f(-x)` reflects over the y-axis.\n\nPoint rule:\n\n`(x,y) -\u003e (-x,y)`","Vertical Scaling":"`g(x)=a f(x)` multiplies y-values by `a`.\n\n- If `a\u003e1`, vertical stretch.\n- If `0\u003ca\u003c1`, vertical compression.\n- If `a\u003c0`, reflect over x-axis and scale by `|a|`.\n\nPoint rule:\n\n`(x,y) -\u003e (x,ay)`","Horizontal Scaling":"Inside scaling affects x-values by a reciprocal.\n\n`g(x)=f(bx)` compresses horizontally by factor `1/b` when `b\u003e1`.\n\nPoint rule:\n\n`(x,y) -\u003e (x/b,y)`\n\n`g(x)=f(x/b)` stretches horizontally by factor `b`.\n\nPoint rule:\n\n`(x,y) -\u003e (bx,y)`","Combined Transformations":"For `g(x)=2f(x-3)+1`:\n\n- `x-3`: shift right 3.\n- `2f(...)`: vertical stretch by 2.\n- `+1`: shift up 1.\n\nPoint `(4,5)` becomes `(7,11)`.","Full Transformation Form":"A common form is:\n\n`g(x)=a f(b(x-h))+k`\n\n- `h` controls horizontal shift.\n- `b` controls horizontal scale by reciprocal `1/b`.\n- `a` controls vertical scale and possible x-axis reflection.\n- `k` controls vertical shift.\n\nFor this phase, emphasize meaning over memorizing a rigid order.","Point Mapping":"Point mapping is often the safest method.\n\nIf `(2,5)` is on `f` and `g(x)=f(x-4)+3`, then:\n\n- x shifts right 4: `2 -\u003e 6`\n- y shifts up 3: `5 -\u003e 8`\n\nNew point: `(6,8)`.","Domain and Range Effects":"Horizontal transformations affect domain.\n\nVertical transformations affect range.\n\nExample:\n\nIf domain of `f` is `[-2,5]` and `g(x)=f(x-3)+4`, domain shifts right 3:\n\n`[1,8]`\n\nIf range of `f` is `[1,7]`, range shifts up 4:\n\n`[5,11]`\n\nIf a vertical scale is negative, transform endpoints and reorder them from least to greatest.","Matching Graphs and Formulas":"To match a transformed graph:\n\n1. Look for horizontal movement.\n2. Look for vertical movement.\n3. Look for reflection.\n4. Look for stretch or compression.\n5. Translate those movements into inside and outside notation.","Parent Function Transformations":"Quadratic parent:\n\n`y=(x-h)^2+k`\n\nVertex: `(h,k)`.\n\nAbsolute value parent:\n\n`y=a|x-h|+k`\n\nVertex: `(h,k)`, with vertical scale `a` and reflection if `a\u003c0`.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: `f(x-3)` means left 3.\n  Correction: Inside subtraction means right 3.\n- Mistake: `f(x+3)` means right 3.\n  Correction: Inside addition means left 3.\n- Mistake: `-f(x)` reflects over y-axis.\n  Correction: Outside negation reflects over x-axis.\n- Mistake: `f(-x)` reflects over x-axis.\n  Correction: Inside negation reflects over y-axis.\n- Mistake: `f(2x)` stretches horizontally by 2.\n  Correction: It compresses horizontally by `1/2`.\n- Mistake: Negative vertical scale gives range endpoints in the written order.\n  Correction: Transform endpoints, then reorder least to greatest.","Guided Practice":"1. Describe `g(x)=f(x+4)-2`.\n   - `x+4`: left 4.\n   - `-2`: down 2.\n\n2. If `(1,3)` is on `f`, find the transformed point for `g(x)=-f(x)+5`.\n   - x unchanged.\n   - y becomes `-3+5=2`.\n   - New point `(1,2)`.\n\n3. If `f` has range `[0,6]`, find the range of `g(x)=-2f(x)+1`.\n   - Transform endpoints: `0 -\u003e 1`, `6 -\u003e -11`.\n   - Reorder: `[-11,1]`.","Independent Practice":"1. `g(x)=f(x)+7`; answer: up 7.\n2. `g(x)=f(x-2)`; answer: right 2.\n3. `g(x)=f(x+5)`; answer: left 5.\n4. `g(x)=-f(x)`; answer: reflect over x-axis.\n5. `g(x)=f(-x)`; answer: reflect over y-axis.\n6. If `(3,-1)` is on `f`, then for `g(x)=2f(x-4)+3`, transformed point is `(7,1)`.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Distinguish inside and outside transformations.\n2. Identify horizontal and vertical shifts.\n3. Identify reflections and scaling.\n4. Map points from `f` to `g`.\n5. Update domain and range after transformations.\n6. Match transformed graphs to formulas.\n7. Correct sign and reciprocal-scale mistakes.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `g(x)=f(x-6)+2`; right 6, up 2.\n2. `g(x)=-3f(x)`; reflect over x-axis and vertical stretch by 3.\n3. `g(x)=f(4x)`; horizontal compression by `1/4`.\n4. `(2,5)` under `g(x)=f(x+1)-4` becomes `(1,1)`.\n5. If `f` has range `[-2,3]`, `g(x)=-f(x)+1` has range `[-2,3]`? Corrected: endpoints transform to `3` and `-2`, so range `[-2,3]`; this example is symmetric but still requires endpoint transformation.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If horizontal signs are reversed: \"Inside shifts feel opposite: `x-h` means right, `x+h` means left.\"\n- If reflections are confused: \"Outside negative flips y-values across the x-axis; inside negative flips x-values across the y-axis.\"\n- If scaling is mistaken for shifting: \"Multiplication stretches or compresses; addition shifts.\"\n- If horizontal scaling is wrong: \"Inside scaling uses reciprocal movement in x-values.\"\n- If point mapping is wrong: \"Transform x-values for inside changes and y-values for outside changes.\"\n- If range endpoints are reversed after negative scaling: \"Transform both endpoints, then write from least to greatest.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready to combine functions and transformations in composition.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P018\n- Estimated duration: 7 minutes\n- Target player state: knows function notation, graph domain/range, and coordinate points\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 018 question\n- Remediation trigger: two horizontal sign errors, two axis-reflection errors, one reciprocal-scale error, or repeated point-mapping errors\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed transformations including shifts, reflections, scaling, point mapping, graph matching, and domain/range transfer","tutorialId":"Tut-P018","phaseId":"P018","title":"Function transformations"},"Tut-P019":{"Learning Goal":"Learn to compose functions by feeding the output of one function into another, then evaluate, simplify, interpret, and find domains of compositions.","Why It Matters":"Composition models chained systems: one process transforms an input, and another process uses that result. It appears in game logic, unit conversion, transformations, inverse functions, piecewise rules, and advanced modeling.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. What does `f(3)` mean? Expected: output of `f` at input 3.\n2. If `g(3)=9`, what is the input and output? Expected: input 3, output 9.\n3. Evaluate `f(9)` if `f(x)=2x+1`. Expected: 19.\n4. What domain rule applies to `sqrt(x-2)`? Expected: `x \u003e= 2`.\n5. Is `f(g(x))` usually the same as `g(f(x))`? Expected: no.","Core Concept":"Composition means function chaining.\n\n`(f o g)(x)=f(g(x))`\n\nThe rightmost function acts first.\n\nFor `f(x)=2x+1` and `g(x)=x^2`:\n\n`f(g(3))`\n\nFirst `g(3)=9`.\n\nThen `f(9)=19`.","Numeric Composition":"Steps:\n\n1. Evaluate the inner function.\n2. Use that output as the input to the outer function.\n3. Continue until the final output is reached.\n\nExample:\n\n`g(f(3))`\n\n`f(3)=7`\n\n`g(7)=49`","Order Matters":"Using the same functions:\n\n`f(g(3))=19`\n\n`g(f(3))=49`\n\nThe order changes the result because the function chain changes.","Formula Composition":"To find `f(g(x))`, replace every input variable in `f` with the entire expression `g(x)`.\n\nExample:\n\n`f(x)=2x+3`\n\n`g(x)=x-5`\n\n`f(g(x))=2(x-5)+3=2x-7`\n\nUse parentheses around the whole inner expression.","Tables and Graphs":"For table or graph composition, follow the chain:\n\n`f(g(2))`\n\nIf `g(2)=4`, then find `f(4)`.\n\nIf `f(4)=7`, then `f(g(2))=7`.\n\nIf the needed outer input is missing, the composition is undefined.","Undefined Compositions":"A composition can fail in two ways:\n\n1. The original input is not allowed by the inner function.\n2. The inner output is not allowed by the outer function.\n\nExample:\n\nIf `g(3)=5` but `f` is not defined at 5, then `f(g(3))` is undefined.","Domain of Compositions":"To find the domain of `f(g(x))`:\n\n1. Make sure `x` is allowed in `g`.\n2. Make sure `g(x)` is allowed in `f`.\n\nExample:\n\n`f(x)=sqrt(x)`, `g(x)=x-2`\n\n`f(g(x))=sqrt(x-2)`\n\nNeed `x-2 \u003e= 0`, so domain `[2,infinity)`.\n\nExample:\n\n`f(x)=1/x`, `g(x)=x-4`\n\n`f(g(x))=1/(x-4)`\n\nNeed `x != 4`.","Decomposition":"Decomposition reverses the task: write a function as a composition.\n\nExample:\n\n`h(x)=(2x+1)^2`\n\nInner: `g(x)=2x+1`\n\nOuter: `f(u)=u^2`\n\nThen `h(x)=f(g(x))`.","Context Composition":"In context, the inner output unit must match the outer input unit.\n\nExample:\n\n`L(t)=2t+1` gives level after `t` hours.\n\n`S(L)=100L+50` gives score at level `L`.\n\n`S(L(3))` means time goes into level, then level goes into score.","Nested Composition":"For three functions, still work inside out.\n\n`f(g(h(2)))`\n\nEvaluate `h(2)`, then `g(...)`, then `f(...)`.","Solving Composition Equations":"To solve `f(g(x))=k`:\n\n1. Compose and simplify.\n2. Set the expression equal to `k`.\n3. Solve.\n4. Check domain restrictions.\n\nExample:\n\n`f(x)=2x+1`, `g(x)=x-3`\n\n`f(g(x))=2x-5`\n\nSolve `2x-5=7`, so `x=6`.","Identity and Undoing":"Some functions undo each other.\n\nIf `f(x)=x+5` and `g(x)=x-5`, then:\n\n`f(g(x))=x`\n\n`g(f(x))=x`\n\nThis prepares for inverse functions.","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Evaluating the leftmost function first.\n  Correction: The innermost/rightmost function acts first.\n- Mistake: Treating composition as multiplication.\n  Correction: Feed one output into the next function.\n- Mistake: Assuming order does not matter.\n  Correction: Calculate both orders separately.\n- Mistake: Forgetting parentheses around the inner expression.\n  Correction: Substitute the whole inner formula.\n- Mistake: Ignoring domain.\n  Correction: Check both the inner function and outer function requirements.","Guided Practice":"1. `f(x)=x+4`, `g(x)=2x`; find `f(g(3))`.\n   - `g(3)=6`.\n   - `f(6)=10`.\n\n2. `f(x)=x^2`, `g(x)=x-1`; find `f(g(x))`.\n   - `(x-1)^2`.\n\n3. `f(x)=sqrt(x)`, `g(x)=x+5`; find domain of `f(g(x))`.\n   - `sqrt(x+5)` requires `x \u003e= -5`.","Independent Practice":"1. `f(x)=3x`, `g(x)=x+2`; find `f(g(4))`. Answer: `18`.\n2. `f(x)=x^2`, `g(x)=x+1`; find `f(g(x))`. Answer: `(x+1)^2`.\n3. `f(x)=x-7`, `g(x)=2x`; find `g(f(x))`. Answer: `2x-14`.\n4. Table has `g(1)=5`, `f(5)=9`; find `f(g(1))`. Answer: `9`.\n5. `f(x)=1/x`, `g(x)=x+3`; domain of `f(g(x))`: `x != -3`.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Interpret `(f o g)(x)` as `f(g(x))`.\n2. Evaluate numerical compositions.\n3. Simplify symbolic compositions.\n4. Use tables and graphs for composition.\n5. Identify undefined compositions.\n6. Find domains of compositions.\n7. Explain why order matters.\n8. Decompose nested expressions.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `f(x)=2x+5`, `g(x)=x^2`; `f(g(2))=13`.\n2. Same functions: `g(f(2))=81`.\n3. `f(x)=3x-1`, `g(x)=x+4`; `f(g(x))=3x+11`.\n4. `f(x)=sqrt(x)`, `g(x)=2x-6`; domain of `f(g(x))` is `[3,infinity)`.\n5. `h(x)=(x-7)^3`; one decomposition is `g(x)=x-7`, `f(u)=u^3`.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player starts with the wrong function: \"Composition works inside out. Start with the function closest to the input.\"\n- If the player multiplies function values: \"The circle means feed output into input, not multiply.\"\n- If order is assumed equal: \"Run both chains separately; their intermediate values may differ.\"\n- If symbolic substitution is partial: \"The entire inner expression replaces the variable in the outer function.\"\n- If a table chain fails: \"Check whether the inner output is listed as an input for the outer function.\"\n- If domain is ignored: \"A composition must satisfy both the inner and outer function restrictions.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready for inverse functions, where compositions can undo each other.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P019\n- Estimated duration: 7 minutes\n- Target player state: knows function notation, formula domains, transformations, and table/graph reading\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 019 question\n- Remediation trigger: two order errors, two product-confusion errors, two missing-parentheses errors, or one repeated composition-domain error\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed numeric, symbolic, table, graph, domain, decomposition, context, and boss composition tasks","tutorialId":"Tut-P019","phaseId":"P019","title":"Function composition"},"Tut-P020":{"Learning Goal":"Learn inverse functions as undoing rules that reverse inputs and outputs, then find, verify, and interpret inverse functions across formulas, tables, graphs, and contexts.","Why It Matters":"Inverse functions let players reverse a rule: score back to level, distance back to time, transformed value back to original value. This is the foundation for logarithms, inverse trigonometric functions, solving equations, and undoing transformations.","Prerequisite Check":"Ask the player:\n\n1. If `f(4)=9`, what input produced output 9? Expected: 4.\n2. What point comes from swapping `(2,7)`? Expected: `(7,2)`.\n3. What line reflects inverse graphs? Expected: `y=x`.\n4. Solve `x=2y+3` for `y`. Expected: `y=(x-3)/2`.\n5. Does every function pass the horizontal line test? Expected: no.","What Inverses Mean":"An inverse reverses a function\u0027s input-output relationship.\n\nIf:\n\n`f(4)=9`\n\nthen:\n\n`f^-1(9)=4`\n\nThe inverse does not mean reciprocal.","Ordered Pairs and Tables":"If `(a,b)` is on `f`, then `(b,a)` is on `f^-1`.\n\nTables work the same way. If a table shows `f(3)=8`, then `f^-1(8)=3`.","Inverse Graphs":"The graph of an inverse is the reflection of the original graph across `y=x`.\n\nPoint rule:\n\n`(x,y) -\u003e (y,x)`","One-to-One Functions":"A function has an inverse function only if each output comes from one input.\n\nUse the horizontal line test:\n\n- If every horizontal line hits at most once, the inverse is a function.\n- If a horizontal line hits more than once, the inverse is not a function unless the domain is restricted.","Finding Algebraic Inverses":"Steps:\n\n1. Write `y=f(x)`.\n2. Swap `x` and `y`.\n3. Solve for `y`.\n4. Rename `y` as `f^-1(x)`.\n5. State domain and range restrictions when needed.\n\nExample:\n\n`f(x)=2x+3`\n\n`y=2x+3`\n\n`x=2y+3`\n\n`y=(x-3)/2`\n\nSo `f^-1(x)=(x-3)/2`.","Evaluating Inverses":"To find `f^-1(11)`, ask which input gives output 11.\n\nIf `f(x)=4x-1`:\n\n`4x-1=11`\n\n`x=3`\n\nSo `f^-1(11)=3`.","Verifying Inverses":"Two functions are inverses when both compositions return the original input:\n\n`f(g(x))=x`\n\nand\n\n`g(f(x))=x`\n\nExample:\n\n`f(x)=2x+3`\n\n`g(x)=(x-3)/2`\n\nBoth compositions simplify to `x`.","Domain and Range Swap":"Domain and range swap under inverses.\n\nIf `f` has domain `[1,6]` and range `[-2,8]`, then:\n\n- `f^-1` has domain `[-2,8]`.\n- `f^-1` has range `[1,6]`.","Restricted Domains":"Some functions need restricted domains before their inverse is a function.\n\n`f(x)=x^2` fails the horizontal line test on all real numbers.\n\nRestrict to `x\u003e=0`, and the inverse is:\n\n`f^-1(x)=sqrt(x)`","Radical Inverses":"For `f(x)=sqrt(x-2)+1`:\n\n`y=sqrt(x-2)+1`\n\nSwap:\n\n`x=sqrt(y-2)+1`\n\nSolve:\n\n`y=(x-1)^2+2`\n\nThe inverse domain is `x\u003e=1` because the original range is `y\u003e=1`.","Algebraic Inverses Beyond Linear":"Cubic example:\n\n`f(x)=x^3+2`\n\nInverse:\n\n`f^-1(x)=cuberoot(x-2)`\n\nShifted reciprocal example:\n\n`f(x)=1/(x-2)+3`\n\nInverse:\n\n`f^-1(x)=2+1/(x-3)`","Context Inverses":"If `S(l)=100l+50` gives score from level, then `S^-1(score)` gives level from score.\n\n`S^-1(750)=7` means:\n\nA score of 750 corresponds to level 7.","Reverse Construction":"If you are given an inverse rule and need the original, invert it again.\n\nIf `f^-1(x)=3x-4`, then:\n\n`f(x)=(x+4)/3`","Common Mistakes":"- Mistake: Treating `f^-1(x)` as `1/f(x)`.\n  Correction: Inverse means undoing, not reciprocal.\n- Mistake: Not swapping `x` and `y`.\n  Correction: Swap input and output first.\n- Mistake: Swapping but not solving for `y`.\n  Correction: Finish isolating the new output.\n- Mistake: Ignoring one-to-one behavior.\n  Correction: Use the horizontal line test.\n- Mistake: Forgetting domain/range restrictions.\n  Correction: Domain and range swap.","Guided Practice":"1. If `f(2)=7`, find `f^-1(7)`.\n   - Answer: `2`.\n\n2. Find inverse of `f(x)=5x-10`.\n   - `y=5x-10`.\n   - `x=5y-10`.\n   - `y=(x+10)/5`.\n\n3. Check whether `f(x)=x^2` on all real numbers has inverse function.\n   - No; it fails horizontal line test.","Independent Practice":"1. If `f(6)=11`, find `f^-1(11)`. Answer: 6.\n2. Point `(4,-3)` on `f`; inverse point `(-3,4)`.\n3. Inverse of `f(x)=3x+2`; answer `(x-2)/3`.\n4. Inverse of `f(x)=(x+1)/4`; answer `4x-1`.\n5. If `f` domain `[0,9]`, range `[1,4]`, inverse domain `[1,4]`, range `[0,9]`.","Mastery Check":"The player is ready to advance when they can:\n\n1. Interpret inverse notation.\n2. Swap ordered pairs and table inputs/outputs.\n3. Use graph reflection across `y=x`.\n4. Apply the horizontal line test.\n5. Find linear and nonlinear inverse formulas.\n6. Verify inverses with composition.\n7. Swap domain and range.\n8. Interpret inverse functions in context.\n\nMastery check set:\n\n1. `f(5)=12`; `f^-1(12)=5`.\n2. Point `(8,-1)` on `f`; point `(-1,8)` on `f^-1`.\n3. `f(x)=7x-4`; `f^-1(x)=(x+4)/7`.\n4. `f(x)=sqrt(x-3)`; inverse `f^-1(x)=x^2+3`, domain `x\u003e=0`.\n5. `f(x)=x^2` needs restriction such as `x\u003e=0` to have inverse function.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If inverse is treated as reciprocal: \"Inverse means undo the function\u0027s input-output relationship, not take `1/f(x)`.\"\n- If coordinates are not swapped: \"Inverse graphs swap input and output, so swap coordinates.\"\n- If algebra starts without swapping: \"Write `y=...`, then swap `x` and `y` before solving.\"\n- If a non-one-to-one function is inverted without restriction: \"Use the horizontal line test; repeated outputs need a domain restriction.\"\n- If domain/range are unchanged: \"The inverse swaps domain and range.\"\n- If verification stops after one direction: \"Check both compositions when possible, and respect domains.\"\n- If the player succeeds quickly: \"You are ready for piecewise functions and later logarithms as inverses of exponentials.\"","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P020\n- Estimated duration: 7 minutes\n- Target player state: knows function notation, composition, domain/range, and algebraic solving\n- Unlock condition: available from any Phase 020 question\n- Remediation trigger: two reciprocal-confusion errors, two no-swap algebra errors, one horizontal-line-test error, or repeated domain/range swap errors\n- Advancement trigger: 80 percent accuracy on mixed notation, table, graph, algebraic inverse, verification, domain/range, restricted-domain, and context tasks","tutorialId":"Tut-P020","phaseId":"P020","title":"Inverse functions"},"Tut-P021":{"Learning Goal":"Master piecewise functions by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A piecewise function uses different formulas on different parts of the domain.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The input value decides which branch applies.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Closed endpoints are included; open endpoints are not included.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Function notation still means replace the input after choosing the correct branch.","Core Concept":"- Choose the branch by checking the input against the condition first.\n- Only evaluate the formula from the branch that contains the input.\n- At a boundary, endpoint symbols decide which formula gives the function value.\n- Piecewise graphs are built one interval at a time.\n- Continuity at a join requires matching output values from both sides.\n- Context piecewise models often represent tiered prices, rates, or rules.","Worked Example":"- For f(x) = {2x+1 if x \u003c 3; x^2 if x \u003e= 3}, find f(2). Answer: 5 Reason: Because 2 \u003c 3, use 2x+1. Then f(2)=2(2)+1=5.\n- For f(x) = {x+4 if x \u003c 1; 3x if x \u003e= 1}, find f(1). Answer: 3 Reason: The condition x \u003e= 1 includes 1, so use 3x. Thus f(1)=3.\n- For g(x) = {-x if x \u003c 0; x+5 if x \u003e= 0}, find g(-6). Answer: 6 Reason: Since -6 \u003c 0, use -x. Then g(-6)=-(-6)=6.\n- For h(x) = {4 if x = 0; x^2+1 if x != 0}, find h(0). Answer: 4 Reason: The special branch x=0 applies, so h(0)=4.","Common Mistakes":"- Evaluating every branch and choosing the nicest answer.\n- Ignoring whether an endpoint uses \u003c or \u003c=.\n- Using the y-value to choose a branch instead of the x-value.\n- Assuming a jump graph cannot still be a function.\n- Forgetting to restrict each graph segment to its interval.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: For p(x) = {x+2 if x \u003c -1; 5 if -1 \u003c= x \u003c 4; 2x-3 if x \u003e= 4}, find p(3). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 5.\n- Prompt: For p(x) = {x+2 if x \u003c -1; 5 if -1 \u003c= x \u003c 4; 2x-3 if x \u003e= 4}, find p(4). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 5.\n- Prompt: For f(x) = {x^2 if x \u003c 2; x+3 if x \u003e= 2}, make the outputs for x=1,2,3. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 1, 5, 6.\n- Prompt: A graph has y=x+1 for x\u003c2 with an open circle at (2,3), and y=5 for x\u003e=2 with a closed circle at (2,5). What is f(2)? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 5.\n- Prompt: What is the domain of f(x) = {x+1 if x\u003c0; x^2 if 0\u003c=x\u003c=3; 7 if x\u003e3}? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: all real numbers.\n- Prompt: Find the range of f(x) = {2 if x\u003c0; x+1 if 0\u003c=x\u003c=2}. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: {2} union [1,3].","Independent Practice":"- Continuity check: Is f(x) = {x+2 if x\u003c1; 3x if x\u003e=1} continuous at x=1? Expected answer: yes.\n- Jump discontinuity: Is f(x) = {2x if x\u003c1; x+4 if x\u003e=1} continuous at x=1? Expected answer: no.\n- Solve within a branch: For f(x) = {x+4 if x\u003c2; 2x-1 if x\u003e=2}, solve f(x)=7. Expected answer: x=4.\n- Absolute value as piecewise: Write |x-3| as a piecewise function. Expected answer: {3-x if x\u003c3; x-3 if x\u003e=3}.\n- Context tiered pricing: A parking garage charges $5 for the first hour and $3 per additional hour. Write the cost for h hours when 0\u003ch\u003c=3. Expected answer: C(h) = {5 if 0\u003ch\u003c=1; 5+3(h-1) if 1\u003ch\u003c=3}.\n- Build from graph description: A function is -2 for x\u003c0, equals x for 0\u003c=x\u003c4, and equals 6 for x\u003e=4. Write f(x). Expected answer: f(x) = {-2 if x\u003c0; x if 0\u003c=x\u003c4; 6 if x\u003e=4}.","Mastery Check":"- Find a so f(x) = {2x+a if x\u003c3; x^2 if x\u003e=3} is continuous at x=3. Mastery answer: a=3.\n- For f(x) = {ax+1 if x\u003c2; 9 if x\u003e=2}, find a if f(1)=6. Mastery answer: a=5.\n- Let f(x) = {x+1 if x\u003c0; 2x if x\u003e=0}. Find f(f(-2)). Mastery answer: -1.\n- For f(x) = {x^2-1 if x\u003c-1; 4 if -1\u003c=x\u003c2; 3x-2 if x\u003e=2}, find f(-2)+f(-1)+f(2). Mastery answer: 11.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P021\n- Phase: 021\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P021","phaseId":"P021","title":"Piecewise functions"},"Tut-P022":{"Learning Goal":"Master quadratic factoring by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A quadratic in standard form has terms ax^2+bx+c.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Factoring rewrites a sum as a product.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The zero product property says if AB=0, then A=0 or B=0.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A greatest common factor should be removed before trinomial factoring.","Core Concept":"- For x^2+bx+c, find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b.\n- For x^2-bx+c, both factor signs may be negative.\n- If c is negative, the factor signs are opposite.\n- For ax^2+bx+c, use grouping or another reliable factoring method.\n- After factoring an equation equal to zero, set each factor equal to zero.\n- Not every quadratic factors over integers.","Worked Example":"- Factor x^2+7x+12. Answer: (x+3)(x+4) Reason: The numbers 3 and 4 multiply to 12 and add to 7.\n- Factor x^2-9x+20. Answer: (x-4)(x-5) Reason: The numbers -4 and -5 multiply to 20 and add to -9.\n- Factor x^2+x-12. Answer: (x+4)(x-3) Reason: The numbers 4 and -3 multiply to -12 and add to 1.\n- Factor x^2+10x+25. Answer: (x+5)^2 Reason: Both factors are x+5 because 5 and 5 multiply to 25 and add to 10.","Common Mistakes":"- Factoring only the first and last terms.\n- Using numbers that add to c instead of multiply to c.\n- Solving factors before the equation equals zero.\n- Dropping a negative sign during grouping.\n- Assuming every quadratic has integer factors.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Factor x^2-49. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (x-7)(x+7).\n- Prompt: Factor 3x^2+12x. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 3x(x+4).\n- Prompt: Factor 2x^2+10x+12 completely. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 2(x+2)(x+3).\n- Prompt: Factor 2x^2+7x+3. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (2x+1)(x+3).\n- Prompt: Factor 3x^2+5x-2. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (3x-1)(x+2).\n- Prompt: Factor 6x^2+11x+3. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (3x+1)(2x+3).","Independent Practice":"- Solve by factoring simple: Solve x^2+5x+6=0. Expected answer: x=-2 or x=-3.\n- Solve with negative constant: Solve x^2-4x-21=0. Expected answer: x=7 or x=-3.\n- Solve with leading coefficient: Solve 2x^2-5x-3=0. Expected answer: x=3 or x=-1/2.\n- Equation not yet zero: Solve x^2+3x=10 by factoring. Expected answer: x=2 or x=-5.\n- Find zeros from factored form: What are the zeros of y=(x-4)(x+6)? Expected answer: x=4 and x=-6.\n- Build quadratic from roots: Write a monic quadratic with roots -2 and 5. Expected answer: x^2-3x-10.","Mastery Check":"- Does x^2+x+1 factor over the integers? Mastery answer: no.\n- A rectangle has area x^2+8x+15. What binomial side lengths fit? Mastery answer: x+3 and x+5.\n- A student factors x^2-2x-15 as (x-3)(x-5). What is wrong? Mastery answer: The signs do not multiply to -15; the correct factorization is (x-5)(x+3)..\n- Solve 4x^2-25=0 and state the factors used. Mastery answer: x=5/2 or x=-5/2; factors (2x-5)(2x+5).","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P022\n- Phase: 022\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P022","phaseId":"P022","title":"Quadratic factoring"},"Tut-P023":{"Learning Goal":"Master completing the square by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A perfect square trinomial has the form x^2+2kx+k^2.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Half of the x-coefficient, squared, completes x^2+bx.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Equations stay balanced only when the same value is added to both sides.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Vertex form is a(x-h)^2+k.","Core Concept":"- Complete the square by adding (b/2)^2 to x^2+bx.\n- A completed square reveals the vertex of a parabola.\n- For equations, move constants first, complete the square, then take square roots.\n- When a is not 1, factor a from the quadratic and linear terms first.\n- Completing the square works even when a quadratic is not factorable.\n- The process connects algebraic form to graph features.","Worked Example":"- What number completes the square for x^2+8x? Answer: 16 Reason: Half of 8 is 4, and 4^2=16.\n- What number completes the square for x^2-6x? Answer: 9 Reason: Half of -6 is -3, and (-3)^2=9.\n- Rewrite x^2+10x+25 as a squared binomial. Answer: (x+5)^2 Reason: Half of 10 is 5, so the trinomial is (x+5)^2.\n- Complete the square: x^2+4x+7. Answer: (x+2)^2+3 Reason: Add and subtract 4: x^2+4x+4+3=(x+2)^2+3.","Common Mistakes":"- Squaring b instead of half of b.\n- Adding a value inside an equation without balancing the other side.\n- Taking only the positive square root.\n- Forgetting to factor the leading coefficient before completing the square.\n- Reading the vertex sign backward in (x-h)^2+k.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Write y=x^2-12x+40 in vertex form. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: y=(x-6)^2+4.\n- Prompt: What is the vertex of y=(x+3)^2-8? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (-3,-8).\n- Prompt: Solve x^2+6x+5=0 by completing the square. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=-1 or x=-5.\n- Prompt: Solve x^2+4x-1=0 by completing the square. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=-2+-sqrt(5).\n- Prompt: What number completes the square for x^2+5x? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 25/4.\n- Prompt: Solve x^2+5x+1=0 by completing the square. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=(-5+-sqrt(21))/2.","Independent Practice":"- Leading coefficient factor: Write y=2x^2+8x+3 in vertex form. Expected answer: y=2(x+2)^2-5.\n- Leading coefficient negative: Write y=-x^2+6x-2 in vertex form. Expected answer: y=-(x-3)^2+7.\n- Max or min from vertex: Does y=3(x-1)^2+5 have a minimum or maximum, and what is its value? Expected answer: minimum value 5.\n- Vertex from standard form: Find the vertex of y=x^2+2x-8 by completing the square. Expected answer: (-1,-9).\n- Solve with leading coefficient: Solve 2x^2+8x+1=0 by completing the square. Expected answer: x=-2+-sqrt(14)/2.\n- Derive quadratic formula step: After completing the square on ax^2+bx+c=0, what squared expression appears? Expected answer: (x+b/(2a))^2.","Mastery Check":"- A student changes x^2+8x=3 into (x+4)^2=3. What is missing? Mastery answer: They added 16 to the left but not the right; it should be (x+4)^2=19..\n- Expand y=(x-4)^2+6 into standard form. Mastery answer: y=x^2-8x+22.\n- A ball has height h(t)=-16(t-2)^2+80. What is its maximum height? Mastery answer: 80.\n- Write y=-2x^2+12x-11 in vertex form and give the vertex. Mastery answer: y=-2(x-3)^2+7; vertex (3,7).","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P023\n- Phase: 023\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P023","phaseId":"P023","title":"Completing the square"},"Tut-P024":{"Learning Goal":"Master quadratic formula by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A quadratic equation in standard form is ax^2+bx+c=0.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The quadratic formula is x=(-b+-sqrt(b^2-4ac))/(2a).\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The values a, b, and c come from standard form.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The expression b^2-4ac is the discriminant.","Core Concept":"- Use the quadratic formula when factoring is hard or impossible.\n- Move all terms to one side before identifying a, b, and c.\n- Substitute coefficients carefully, especially when b or c is negative.\n- Simplify the discriminant before simplifying the final radical.\n- The denominator is the whole quantity 2a.\n- Check solutions by substitution when possible.","Worked Example":"- For 3x^2-5x+2=0, identify a, b, and c. Answer: a=3, b=-5, c=2 Reason: Read directly from ax^2+bx+c=0.\n- Solve x^2-5x+6=0 using the quadratic formula. Answer: x=2 or x=3 Reason: Here a=1,b=-5,c=6. The formula gives (5+-sqrt(25-24))/2=(5+-1)/2.\n- Solve x^2+4x-5=0 using the quadratic formula. Answer: x=1 or x=-5 Reason: a=1,b=4,c=-5. The formula gives (-4+-sqrt(16+20))/2=(-4+-6)/2.\n- Solve 2x^2+3x-2=0. Answer: x=1/2 or x=-2 Reason: a=2,b=3,c=-2. The discriminant is 25, so x=(-3+-5)/4.","Common Mistakes":"- Using b instead of -b in the numerator.\n- Forgetting that b^2 is positive even when b is negative.\n- Leaving the equation outside standard form before reading coefficients.\n- Dividing only the radical by 2a instead of the entire numerator.\n- Rounding too early when exact form is requested.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Solve x^2+2x-2=0. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=-1+-sqrt(3).\n- Prompt: Solve x^2-6x+1=0. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=3+-2sqrt(2).\n- Prompt: Solve x^2=3x+10 using the quadratic formula. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=5 or x=-2.\n- Prompt: Solve 4x^2-9=0 using the quadratic formula. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=3/2 or x=-3/2.\n- Prompt: Solve 5x^2-20x=0 using the quadratic formula. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=0 or x=4.\n- Prompt: Solve x^2-8x+16=0 using the quadratic formula. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=4.","Independent Practice":"- Two irrational roots: Solve 3x^2-2x-1=0. Expected answer: x=1 or x=-1/3.\n- Exact radical with a\u003e1: Solve 2x^2+4x-3=0. Expected answer: x=(-2+-sqrt(10))/2.\n- Decimal approximation: Solve x^2+x-1=0 to the nearest hundredth. Expected answer: x=0.62 or x=-1.62.\n- Check a candidate: Is x=2 a solution of 3x^2-7x+2=0? Expected answer: yes.\n- Formula setup only: Set up the quadratic formula for 5x^2+x-4=0 without simplifying. Expected answer: x=(-1+-sqrt(1-4(5)(-4)))/(10).\n- Error analysis denominator: A student solves 2x^2+3x-2=0 and writes x=(-3+-5)/2. What is wrong? Expected answer: The denominator should be 2a=4, not 2..","Mastery Check":"- For x^2-6x+8=0, a student uses -6+-sqrt(36-32) over 2. What is wrong? Mastery answer: They used b instead of -b; the numerator should start with 6..\n- A height model is h(t)=-16t^2+32t+48. When is h(t)=0? Mastery answer: t=3 seconds or t=-1 second; physical time is 3 seconds.\n- Why might the quadratic formula be better than factoring for x^2+4x+1=0? Mastery answer: It does not factor over integers and the formula gives x=-2+-sqrt(3)..\n- Solve 6x^2-7x-3=0 exactly. Mastery answer: x=3/2 or x=-1/3.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P024\n- Phase: 024\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P024","phaseId":"P024","title":"Quadratic formula"},"Tut-P025":{"Learning Goal":"Master discriminant by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A quadratic in standard form is ax^2+bx+c=0.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The discriminant is b^2-4ac.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The discriminant is the expression under the radical in the quadratic formula.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A positive perfect-square discriminant gives two rational real solutions.","Core Concept":"- The sign of the discriminant tells how many real solutions a quadratic has.\n- The square status of a positive discriminant tells whether real solutions are rational or irrational.\n- The discriminant links algebraic roots to graph x-intercepts.\n- Parameter problems use inequalities or equations involving b^2-4ac.\n- The discriminant classifies roots before solving the quadratic.\n- The discriminant is not itself the solution set.","Worked Example":"- Find the discriminant of x^2-5x+6=0. Answer: 1 Reason: Here a=1, b=-5, c=6. The discriminant is (-5)^2-4(1)(6)=25-24=1.\n- Find the discriminant of x^2+4x+4=0. Answer: 0 Reason: Here a=1, b=4, c=4. The discriminant is 16-16=0.\n- Find the discriminant of x^2+2x+5=0. Answer: -16 Reason: Here a=1, b=2, c=5. The discriminant is 4-20=-16.\n- Use the discriminant to classify the roots of 2x^2-3x-2=0. Answer: two rational real roots Reason: The discriminant is (-3)^2-4(2)(-2)=9+16=25, a positive perfect square.","Common Mistakes":"- Treating the discriminant as x.\n- Using b^2+4ac instead of b^2-4ac.\n- Forgetting that a negative b still has positive b^2.\n- Saying D=0 means no solution instead of one repeated real solution.\n- Forgetting to put the equation in standard form first.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Use the discriminant to classify the roots of 3x^2+2x+1=0. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: two complex roots and no real roots.\n- Prompt: Use the discriminant to classify the roots of 4x^2-12x+9=0. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: one repeated real root.\n- Prompt: Use the discriminant to classify x^2-2x-7=0. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: two irrational real roots.\n- Prompt: How many x-intercepts does y=x^2+6x+10 have? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 0.\n- Prompt: How many x-intercepts does y=x^2-8x+16 have? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 1.\n- Prompt: How many x-intercepts does y=-x^2+3x+4 have? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 2.","Independent Practice":"- Discriminant from roots type: A quadratic has discriminant 18. What type of roots does it have? Expected answer: two irrational real roots.\n- Discriminant from formula: For a quadratic, the formula gives x=(7+-sqrt(13))/4. What is the discriminant? Expected answer: 13.\n- Parameter for one root: Find k so x^2+kx+9=0 has one repeated real root. Expected answer: k=6 or k=-6.\n- Parameter for no real roots: For x^2+6x+c=0, what values of c give no real roots? Expected answer: c\u003e9.\n- Parameter for two real roots: For x^2-4x+m=0, what values of m give two real roots? Expected answer: m\u003c4.\n- Leading coefficient parameter: Find p so 2x^2+px+8=0 has exactly one real solution. Expected answer: p=8 or p=-8.","Mastery Check":"- A student says the discriminant of x^2-3x-10=0 is 9-40=-31. What is the error? Mastery answer: The c value is -10, so -4ac becomes +40; the discriminant is 49..\n- Use the discriminant to classify x^2+2x=8. Mastery answer: two rational real roots.\n- Why does a negative discriminant produce no real roots? Mastery answer: Because the quadratic formula would require the square root of a negative number for real x-values..\n- Classify the roots of 5x^2-2x+1=0 and say how many x-intercepts the graph has. Mastery answer: two complex roots; 0 x-intercepts.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P025\n- Phase: 025\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P025","phaseId":"P025","title":"Discriminant"},"Tut-P026":{"Learning Goal":"Master quadratic graphs by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A quadratic graph is a parabola.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Vertex form y=a(x-h)^2+k shows vertex (h,k).\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Factored form y=a(x-r1)(x-r2) shows x-intercepts.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Standard form y=ax^2+bx+c shows the y-intercept c.","Core Concept":"- The vertex is the turning point of a parabola.\n- The axis of symmetry is the vertical line through the vertex.\n- The sign and size of a control opening direction and width.\n- x-intercepts are real zeros of the quadratic.\n- The y-intercept occurs when x=0.\n- A quadratic graph can be described using vertex, intercepts, and opening direction.","Worked Example":"- For y=2(x-3)^2-5, identify the vertex. Answer: (3,-5) Reason: Vertex form y=a(x-h)^2+k gives vertex (h,k), so h=3 and k=-5.\n- Find the axis of symmetry of y=(x+4)^2+1. Answer: x=-4 Reason: The vertex is (-4,1), so the axis is x=-4.\n- Does y=-3(x-1)^2+7 open up or down? Answer: down Reason: The coefficient a=-3 is negative, so the parabola opens down.\n- Which is narrower: y=x^2 or y=4x^2? Answer: y=4x^2 Reason: A larger absolute value of a makes the parabola vertically steeper and narrower.","Common Mistakes":"- Reading the vertex sign backward from vertex form.\n- Confusing x-intercepts with the y-intercept.\n- Forgetting that a negative leading coefficient opens down.\n- Using all real numbers as a context domain when time or length is restricted.\n- Assuming the graph scale does not affect interpretation.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Find the y-intercept of y=2x^2-5x+8. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (0,8).\n- Prompt: Find the x-intercepts of y=(x-2)(x+5). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (2,0) and (-5,0).\n- Prompt: Find the axis of symmetry of y=(x-1)(x-7). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=4.\n- Prompt: Find the vertex of y=x^2-6x+11. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (3,2).\n- Prompt: Does y=-2(x+1)^2+9 have a maximum or minimum value, and what is it? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: maximum value 9.\n- Prompt: Find the range of y=3(x-2)^2-4. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: y\u003e=-4.","Independent Practice":"- Graph from three features: Sketch description: vertex (1,-3), opens up, passes through (0,-1). What is a in y=a(x-1)^2-3? Expected answer: a=2.\n- Number of x-intercepts: How many x-intercepts does y=x^2+2x+5 have? Expected answer: 0.\n- Convert vertex to standard: Expand y=(x+2)^2-6. Expected answer: y=x^2+4x-2.\n- Convert standard to vertex: Write y=x^2+8x+10 in vertex form. Expected answer: y=(x+4)^2-6.\n- Match graph description: Which formula has vertex (-2,5) and opens down: y=-(x+2)^2+5 or y=(x-2)^2+5? Expected answer: y=-(x+2)^2+5.\n- Intercept and vertex consistency: A parabola has roots -1 and 5. What is its axis of symmetry? Expected answer: x=2.","Mastery Check":"- A height is h(t)=-16(t-2)^2+64. What is the maximum height? Mastery answer: 64.\n- For h(t)=-5(t-3)^2+45, when does the object reach maximum height? Mastery answer: t=3.\n- A student says y=(x-6)^2+4 has vertex (-6,4). What is wrong? Mastery answer: The x-coordinate is 6, not -6..\n- Summarize y=-x^2+4x+5 by giving vertex, axis, y-intercept, and x-intercepts. Mastery answer: vertex (2,9), axis x=2, y-intercept (0,5), x-intercepts (-1,0) and (5,0).","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P026\n- Phase: 026\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P026","phaseId":"P026","title":"Quadratic graphs"},"Tut-P027":{"Learning Goal":"Master quadratic modeling by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A quadratic graph is a parabola.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Vertex form y=a(x-h)^2+k shows vertex (h,k).\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Factored form y=a(x-r1)(x-r2) shows x-intercepts.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Standard form y=ax^2+bx+c shows the y-intercept c.","Core Concept":"- Quadratic models often describe area, height, revenue, and symmetrical optimization.\n- The vertex often represents a maximum or minimum in context.\n- Zeros can represent times, break-even values, or dimensions where output is zero.\n- The domain must fit the situation, not just the algebra.\n- Units should be included in interpreted answers.\n- Model parameters can be found from points or constraints.","Worked Example":"- A ball\u0027s height is h(t)=-16(t-2)^2+80. What is the maximum height? Answer: 80 feet Reason: The vertex gives the maximum because the coefficient is negative.\n- For h(t)=-16(t-3)^2+144, when is the ball highest? Answer: 3 seconds Reason: The vertex occurs at t=3.\n- For h(t)=-16t^2+64t, when does the ball hit the ground after launch? Answer: 4 seconds Reason: Set h=0: -16t(t-4)=0, so t=0 or t=4; after launch is 4.\n- A rectangle has width x and length 20-x. Write the area function. Answer: A(x)=x(20-x)=-x^2+20x Reason: Area is width times length.","Common Mistakes":"- Reading the vertex sign backward from vertex form.\n- Confusing x-intercepts with the y-intercept.\n- Forgetting that a negative leading coefficient opens down.\n- Using all real numbers as a context domain when time or length is restricted.\n- Assuming the graph scale does not affect interpretation.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: For A(x)=-x^2+20x, what width gives maximum area? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=10.\n- Prompt: Revenue is R(p)=p(100-5p). What price maximizes revenue? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: p=10.\n- Prompt: Profit is P(x)=-(x-2)(x-8). What production levels break even? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=2 and x=8.\n- Prompt: For C(x)=2x^2+5x+40, what does C(0)=40 represent? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: fixed cost of 40 units of money.\n- Prompt: A profit model has zeros at 3 and 11 and opens down with a= -2. Write P(x). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: P(x)=-2(x-3)(x-11).\n- Prompt: A downward parabola has zeros at 4 and 12. At what x is its maximum? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=8.","Independent Practice":"- Fence model: A farmer uses 60 m of fence for three sides against a wall. If width is x, write area. Expected answer: A(x)=x(60-2x).\n- Fence maximum: For A(x)=x(60-2x), what width maximizes area? Expected answer: 15 m.\n- Context domain: For A(x)=x(60-2x), what is the practical domain? Expected answer: 0\u003cx\u003c30.\n- Dropped object: A dropped object\u0027s height is h(t)=-16t^2+100. When is it at height 36? Expected answer: 2 seconds.\n- Bridge arch: An arch is y=-0.25(x-8)^2+16. What is the maximum height? Expected answer: 16.\n- Symmetric points: If a projectile is at the same height at t=1 and t=5, when is the maximum? Expected answer: t=3.","Mastery Check":"- A model h(t)=a(t-2)^2+50 passes through (0,18). Find a. Mastery answer: a=-8.\n- A student keeps t=-1 as a time solution for h(t)=0. What should they do? Mastery answer: Reject t=-1 if time after launch is required..\n- Which has greater maximum: A(x)=-(x-4)^2+30 or B(x)=-2(x-5)^2+28? Mastery answer: A.\n- Demand is q=80-4p and revenue is R=pq. Find the price and maximum revenue. Mastery answer: price 10; maximum revenue 400.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P027\n- Phase: 027\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P027","phaseId":"P027","title":"Quadratic modeling"},"Tut-P028":{"Learning Goal":"Master polynomial end behavior by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A polynomial is a sum of powers of x with real coefficients.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The degree is the highest exponent with a nonzero coefficient.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Zeros are x-values where the polynomial equals zero.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Factored form shows zeros and multiplicities.","Core Concept":"- End behavior is controlled by the leading term.\n- Even degree ends go the same direction.\n- Odd degree ends go opposite directions.\n- Positive leading coefficients rise to the right.\n- Negative leading coefficients fall to the right.\n- Lower-degree terms do not control far-left or far-right behavior.","Worked Example":"- Describe the end behavior of f(x)=2x^4-3x+1. Answer: left up, right up Reason: Even degree with positive leading coefficient means both ends rise.\n- Describe the end behavior of f(x)=-5x^6+x^2. Answer: left down, right down Reason: Even degree with negative leading coefficient means both ends fall.\n- Describe the end behavior of f(x)=x^3-4x. Answer: left down, right up Reason: Odd degree with positive leading coefficient falls left and rises right.\n- Describe the end behavior of f(x)=-2x^5+7x. Answer: left up, right down Reason: Odd degree with negative leading coefficient rises left and falls right.","Common Mistakes":"- Using the constant term to decide end behavior.\n- Counting a repeated factor only once when multiplicity matters.\n- Dropping zero coefficient entries during polynomial division.\n- Confusing a remainder value with an x-intercept.\n- Forgetting to test the original polynomial after factoring.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: What is the leading term of 4x-7x^3+2x^5-1? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 2x^5.\n- Prompt: What is the degree of -3x^7+2x^4-9? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 7.\n- Prompt: As x-\u003einfinity, what happens to f(x)=-x^4+3x? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: f(x)-\u003e-infinity.\n- Prompt: As x-\u003e-infinity, what happens to f(x)=3x^5-x^2? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: f(x)-\u003e-infinity.\n- Prompt: Which polynomial could have both ends up: x^3+1, -x^4+2, or x^4-x? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x^4-x.\n- Prompt: A graph falls left and rises right. Is the degree even or odd? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: odd.","Independent Practice":"- Match leading sign: A graph falls left and rises right. Is the leading coefficient positive or negative? Expected answer: positive.\n- Lower terms ignored: Does +1000 in f(x)=x^5+1000 change the end behavior? Expected answer: no.\n- Table evidence: A polynomial\u0027s values become very negative for large negative x and very positive for large positive x. What end behavior is shown? Expected answer: left down, right up.\n- Minimum possible degree: What is the least possible degree for a polynomial with left up and right down? Expected answer: 1.\n- Degree and turns: Can a degree 4 polynomial have 5 turning points? Expected answer: no.\n- End behavior notation: Write end behavior for f(x)=-x^3+2 using limits. Expected answer: as x-\u003e-infinity, f(x)-\u003einfinity; as x-\u003einfinity, f(x)-\u003e-infinity.","Mastery Check":"- Which has the same end behavior as -3x^4+2x: 5x^4-1 or -x^6+x? Mastery answer: -x^6+x.\n- A student says f(x)=x^6-x has odd end behavior because of -x. What is wrong? Mastery answer: The leading term x^6 controls end behavior..\n- Give a polynomial with left down and right down. Mastery answer: -x^2.\n- Classify degree parity and leading sign for a graph that rises left and falls right. Mastery answer: odd degree, negative leading coefficient.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P028\n- Phase: 028\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P028","phaseId":"P028","title":"Polynomial end behavior"},"Tut-P029":{"Learning Goal":"Master polynomial multiplicity by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A polynomial is a sum of powers of x with real coefficients.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The degree is the highest exponent with a nonzero coefficient.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Zeros are x-values where the polynomial equals zero.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Factored form shows zeros and multiplicities.","Core Concept":"- Multiplicity is the exponent on a repeated factor.\n- Odd multiplicity usually crosses the x-axis.\n- Even multiplicity usually touches and turns around.\n- Higher multiplicity makes the graph flatter near the zero.\n- The sum of multiplicities equals the degree when all factors are shown.\n- Multiplicity helps sketch a polynomial graph from factored form.","Worked Example":"- In f(x)=(x-2)^3(x+1), what is the multiplicity of x=2? Answer: 3 Reason: The factor x-2 has exponent 3.\n- List zeros and multiplicities for f(x)=(x+4)^2(x-1)^5. Answer: x=-4 mult 2; x=1 mult 5 Reason: Each zero comes from setting a factor to zero; the exponent is multiplicity.\n- Does f(x)=(x-3)^3 cross or bounce at x=3? Answer: cross Reason: Odd multiplicity zeros cross the x-axis.\n- Does f(x)=(x+2)^4 cross or bounce at x=-2? Answer: bounce Reason: Even multiplicity zeros touch and turn around.","Common Mistakes":"- Using the constant term to decide end behavior.\n- Counting a repeated factor only once when multiplicity matters.\n- Dropping zero coefficient entries during polynomial division.\n- Confusing a remainder value with an x-intercept.\n- Forgetting to test the original polynomial after factoring.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: What is the degree of f(x)=2(x-1)^2(x+3)^3? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 5.\n- Prompt: Describe end behavior of f(x)=-(x-1)^2(x+2). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: left up, right down.\n- Prompt: For f(x)=(x+1)^2(x-4), identify where the graph bounces and crosses. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: bounces at -1; crosses at 4.\n- Prompt: Which zero is flatter: multiplicity 1 or multiplicity 5? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: multiplicity 5.\n- Prompt: Write a polynomial with a bounce at x=2 and a cross at x=-3. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (x-2)^2(x+3).\n- Prompt: What is the least degree for zeros -1 with bounce and 4 with cross? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 3.","Independent Practice":"- Sign through simple root: If f(x) has factor (x-5), does the sign usually change at x=5? Expected answer: yes.\n- Sign through double root: If f(x) has factor (x+6)^2, does the sign usually change at x=-6? Expected answer: no.\n- Repeated root equation: Solve (x-2)^2(x+1)=0 and include multiplicities. Expected answer: x=2 mult 2; x=-1 mult 1.\n- Match graph: A graph touches at x=-2 and crosses at x=3. Which factored form fits: (x+2)^2(x-3) or (x+2)(x-3)^2? Expected answer: (x+2)^2(x-3).\n- Multiplicity from expanded clue: If f(x)=(x-1)(x-1)(x+4), what is the multiplicity of x=1? Expected answer: 2.\n- Error analysis: A student says (x+2)^2 crosses at -2. What is wrong? Expected answer: Even multiplicity means the graph bounces at -2..","Mastery Check":"- Find the y-intercept of f(x)=(x-2)^2(x+1). Mastery answer: (0,4).\n- For f(x)=-2(x+1)^2(x-3)^2, describe end behavior. Mastery answer: left down, right down.\n- Write a degree 5 polynomial with zeros 0 mult 3 and 2 mult 2. Mastery answer: x^3(x-2)^2.\n- Summarize f(x)=(x+2)^2(x-1)^3: zeros, behavior, degree, end behavior. Mastery answer: zero -2 bounce; zero 1 cross; degree 5; left down right up.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P029\n- Phase: 029\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P029","phaseId":"P029","title":"Polynomial multiplicity"},"Tut-P030":{"Learning Goal":"Master polynomial division by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A polynomial is a sum of powers of x with real coefficients.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The degree is the highest exponent with a nonzero coefficient.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Zeros are x-values where the polynomial equals zero.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Factored form shows zeros and multiplicities.","Core Concept":"- Polynomial division rewrites dividend divided by divisor as quotient plus remainder over divisor.\n- Long division works for any polynomial divisor.\n- Synthetic division is a shortcut for divisors of the form x-c.\n- Missing powers need zero coefficient entries.\n- The remainder has degree lower than the divisor.\n- A zero remainder means the divisor is a factor.","Worked Example":"- Divide x^2+5x+6 by x+2. Answer: x+3 remainder 0 Reason: Use c=-2. Synthetic division gives quotient x+3 and remainder 0.\n- Divide x^2+3x+5 by x+1. Answer: x+2 remainder 3 Reason: Use c=-1. The quotient is x+2 and the remainder is 3.\n- For division by x-4, what number is used in synthetic division? Answer: 4 Reason: The divisor x-c uses c=4.\n- For division by x+7, what number is used in synthetic division? Answer: -7 Reason: x+7=x-(-7), so c=-7.","Common Mistakes":"- Using the constant term to decide end behavior.\n- Counting a repeated factor only once when multiplicity matters.\n- Dropping zero coefficient entries during polynomial division.\n- Confusing a remainder value with an x-intercept.\n- Forgetting to test the original polynomial after factoring.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Divide x^3-2x^2-5x+6 by x-3. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x^2+x-2 remainder 0.\n- Prompt: Divide x^3+2x+1 by x+1. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x^2-x+3 remainder -2.\n- Prompt: When dividing 2x^3+3x^2 by x, what is the first quotient term? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 2x^2.\n- Prompt: Divide x^2+5x+4 by x+1. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x+4 remainder 0.\n- Prompt: Write (x^2+1)/(x-1) as quotient plus remainder over divisor. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x+1+2/(x-1).\n- Prompt: Check that x^2+3x+2 divided by x+1 equals x+2. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (x+1)(x+2)=x^2+3x+2.","Independent Practice":"- Quartic synthetic: Divide x^4-1 by x-1. Expected answer: x^3+x^2+x+1 remainder 0.\n- Negative synthetic value: Divide 2x^3+x^2-8x-4 by x+2. Expected answer: 2x^2-3x-2 remainder 0.\n- Nonzero remainder meaning: If division by x-5 has remainder 7, is x-5 a factor? Expected answer: no.\n- Degree of quotient: If a degree 5 polynomial is divided by a degree 1 polynomial, what is the quotient degree? Expected answer: 4.\n- Divide by quadratic: Divide x^3+2x^2-x-2 by x+2. Expected answer: x^2-1 remainder 0.\n- Long division by quadratic: Divide x^3+3x^2+2x+4 by x^2+1. Expected answer: x+3 remainder x+1.","Mastery Check":"- A student divides x^3+4x+1 by x-2 using coefficients 1,4,1. What is wrong? Mastery answer: They omitted the 0x^2 coefficient entry..\n- If quotient is x^2+2, divisor is x-3, and remainder is 5, what is the dividend? Mastery answer: x^3-3x^2+2x-1.\n- If x^3-4x^2+x+6 divided by x-2 has remainder 0, what factor is confirmed? Mastery answer: x-2.\n- Divide 2x^4-3x^3+0x^2+5x-1 by x-1. Mastery answer: 2x^3-x^2-x+4 remainder 3.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P030\n- Phase: 030\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P030","phaseId":"P030","title":"Polynomial division"},"Tut-P031":{"Learning Goal":"Master remainder theorem by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A polynomial is a sum of powers of x with real coefficients.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The degree is the highest exponent with a nonzero coefficient.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Zeros are x-values where the polynomial equals zero.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Factored form shows zeros and multiplicities.","Core Concept":"- When f(x) is divided by x-c, the remainder is f(c).\n- The theorem finds remainders without full division.\n- The sign of c comes from rewriting the divisor as x-c.\n- If f(c)=0, then x-c is a factor.\n- Remainders can be positive, negative, zero, or fractional.\n- Evaluation and synthetic division must agree.","Worked Example":"- Find the remainder when f(x)=x^2+3x+5 is divided by x-2. Answer: 15 Reason: By the remainder theorem, the remainder is f(2)=4+6+5=15.\n- Find the remainder when x^3+2x+4 is divided by x+1. Answer: 1 Reason: Use c=-1. f(-1)=-1-2+4=1.\n- Find the remainder when x^2-5x+6 is divided by x-2. Answer: 0 Reason: f(2)=4-10+6=0.\n- Find the remainder when f(x)=2x^2-3x+1 is divided by x-3. Answer: 10 Reason: f(3)=18-9+1=10.","Common Mistakes":"- Using the constant term to decide end behavior.\n- Counting a repeated factor only once when multiplicity matters.\n- Dropping zero coefficient entries during polynomial division.\n- Confusing a remainder value with an x-intercept.\n- Forgetting to test the original polynomial after factoring.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Find the remainder when f(x)=x^3-4x is divided by x+2. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 0.\n- Prompt: For divisor x-7, what input is used in the remainder theorem? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 7.\n- Prompt: For divisor x+5, what input is used in the remainder theorem? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: -5.\n- Prompt: If synthetic division of f by x-4 ends with 9, what is f(4)? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 9.\n- Prompt: Find k so f(x)=x^2+kx+3 has remainder 0 when divided by x-1. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: k=-4.\n- Prompt: Find a so f(x)=ax^2+2x-5 has remainder 7 when divided by x-2. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: a=2.","Independent Practice":"- Remainder from table: If f(3)=-8, what is the remainder when f(x) is divided by x-3? Expected answer: -8.\n- Nonlinear divisor caution: Can the remainder theorem in the form f(c) be used directly for division by x^2+1? Expected answer: no.\n- Remainder value interpretation: If f(5)=0, what is the remainder when dividing by x-5? Expected answer: 0.\n- Evaluate carefully: Find the remainder when f(x)=-x^3+x^2+2 is divided by x+1. Expected answer: 4.\n- Standard notation: For P(x)=3x^3-x+6, find the remainder when divided by x-1. Expected answer: 8.\n- Error analysis sign: A student divides by x+2 and evaluates f(2). What is wrong? Expected answer: They should evaluate f(-2)..","Mastery Check":"- If f(-4)=0, name a factor of f(x). Mastery answer: x+4.\n- Find the remainder when x^4-3x^2+x is divided by x-2. Mastery answer: 6.\n- Find m so x^3+mx+10 has remainder 4 when divided by x+2. Mastery answer: m= -1.\n- For f(x)=2x^3-5x+1, find remainders for division by x-1 and x+1. Mastery answer: -2 and 4.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P031\n- Phase: 031\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P031","phaseId":"P031","title":"Remainder theorem"},"Tut-P032":{"Learning Goal":"Master factor theorem by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A polynomial is a sum of powers of x with real coefficients.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The degree is the highest exponent with a nonzero coefficient.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Zeros are x-values where the polynomial equals zero.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Factored form shows zeros and multiplicities.","Core Concept":"- x-c is a factor of f(x) exactly when f(c)=0.\n- A zero of a polynomial corresponds to a linear factor.\n- Synthetic division can confirm and reduce a polynomial after a factor is found.\n- Known factors help factor higher-degree polynomials.\n- The sign in the factor x-c is opposite the zero c.\n- Factor theorem connects equations, graphs, and division.","Worked Example":"- Is x-2 a factor of f(x)=x^2-5x+6? Answer: yes Reason: Evaluate f(2)=4-10+6=0.\n- Is x+1 a factor of f(x)=x^2+3x+5? Answer: no Reason: Evaluate f(-1)=1-3+5=3, not 0.\n- If f(4)=0, name a factor of f(x). Answer: x-4 Reason: A zero c gives factor x-c.\n- If x+7 is a factor, what zero does the polynomial have? Answer: -7 Reason: Set x+7=0 to get x=-7.","Common Mistakes":"- Using the constant term to decide end behavior.\n- Counting a repeated factor only once when multiplicity matters.\n- Dropping zero coefficient entries during polynomial division.\n- Confusing a remainder value with an x-intercept.\n- Forgetting to test the original polynomial after factoring.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Given f(x)=x^3-6x^2+11x-6 and factor x-1, find the remaining quadratic factor. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x^2-5x+6.\n- Prompt: Factor x^3-6x^2+11x-6 completely. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (x-1)(x-2)(x-3).\n- Prompt: Find k so x-3 is a factor of f(x)=x^2+kx+6. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: k=-5.\n- Prompt: Find a so x+2 is a factor of f(x)=ax^2+3x-2. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: a=2.\n- Prompt: If x-5 is a factor, what x-intercept is on the graph? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (5,0).\n- Prompt: A table shows f(-3)=0. Name a factor. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x+3.","Independent Practice":"- Check multiple candidates: Which is a factor of x^3+x^2-4x-4: x-2 or x+2? Expected answer: x-2 and x+2.\n- Divide after factor: If x+1 is a factor of x^3+4x^2+x-6, find the quotient. Expected answer: x^2+3x-2.\n- Error analysis sign: A student says f(3)=0 means x+3 is a factor. What is wrong? Expected answer: The factor should be x-3..\n- Repeated factor: If f(x)=(x-2)^2(x+1), is x-2 a factor? Expected answer: yes.\n- Least polynomial: Write a monic quadratic with factors x-4 and x+1. Expected answer: x^2-3x-4.\n- Find missing constant: Find c so x-5 is a factor of x^2-2x+c. Expected answer: c=-15.","Mastery Check":"- Why does f(2)=0 help factor a cubic? Mastery answer: It confirms x-2 is a factor, so division reduces the cubic to a quadratic..\n- Is x+3 a factor of -x^2-5x-6? Mastery answer: yes.\n- Given x-2 is a factor of x^3-4x^2+x+6, find all roots. Mastery answer: x=2, 3, -1.\n- Find a so x+4 is a factor of f(x)=2x^3+ax+8. Mastery answer: a=-15.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P032\n- Phase: 032\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P032","phaseId":"P032","title":"Factor theorem"},"Tut-P033":{"Learning Goal":"Master rational expression simplification by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A rational expression is a ratio of polynomials.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Values that make the original denominator zero are excluded.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A common factor can create a hole after cancellation.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A non-cancelled denominator factor creates a vertical asymptote.","Core Concept":"- Factor numerator and denominator before cancelling.\n- Only common factors may be cancelled.\n- Restrictions come from the original denominator.\n- A simplified expression may hide excluded values.\n- Signs can be factored out to reveal common binomials.\n- Simplification should be checked by multiplication.","Worked Example":"- Simplify 6x^2/(3x). Answer: 2x, x != 0 Reason: Cancel common factor 3x; x=0 is excluded from the original denominator.\n- Simplify (x^2-9)/(x-3). Answer: x+3, x != 3 Reason: Factor numerator as (x-3)(x+3), then cancel x-3.\n- Simplify (x^2+5x+6)/(x+2). Answer: x+3, x != -2 Reason: Factor numerator as (x+2)(x+3).\n- Simplify (x^2-4)/(x^2+x-6). Answer: (x+2)/(x+3), x != 2,-3 Reason: Factor to (x-2)(x+2)/((x+3)(x-2)) and cancel x-2.","Common Mistakes":"- Cancelling terms that are not common factors.\n- Forgetting restrictions from the original denominator.\n- Calling every excluded value an asymptote.\n- Ignoring holes after simplifying.\n- Using y-values instead of x-values for denominator restrictions.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Simplify (4x^2+8x)/(2x). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 2x+4, x != 0.\n- Prompt: Simplify (3x^2-12x)/(6x). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (x-4)/2, x != 0.\n- Prompt: Simplify (x^2-16)/(x^2-8x+16). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (x+4)/(x-4), x != 4.\n- Prompt: Simplify (x-5)/(5-x). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: -1, x != 5.\n- Prompt: Can (x+3)/(x+5) be simplified by cancelling x? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: no.\n- Prompt: Simplify (2-x)/(x-2). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: -1, x != 2.","Independent Practice":"- Factor by grouping: Simplify (x^2+3x)/(x^2+5x+6). Expected answer: x/(x+2), x != -2,-3.\n- Complex trinomial: Simplify (2x^2+7x+3)/(2x+1). Expected answer: x+3, x != -1/2.\n- Restrictions after cancellation: Simplify (x^2-1)/(x-1) and state restrictions. Expected answer: x+1, x != 1.\n- Multiple restrictions: Simplify (x^2+x-6)/(x^2-4). Expected answer: (x+3)/(x+2), x != -2,2.\n- Rational expression multiplication: Simplify [(x^2-4)/(x+3)] * [(x+3)/(x-2)]. Expected answer: x+2, x != -3,2.\n- Rational expression division: Simplify [(x^2-9)/(x+1)] divided by [(x+3)/(x+1)]. Expected answer: x-3, x != -1,-3.","Mastery Check":"- A student simplifies (x+4)/(x+7) to 4/7. What is wrong? Mastery answer: They cancelled terms, not common factors..\n- Is (x^2-25)/(x+5) equivalent to x-5 for all x? Mastery answer: no; x=-5 is excluded.\n- Create a rational expression that simplifies to x+2 but excludes x=3. Mastery answer: ((x+2)(x-3))/(x-3).\n- Simplify (x^2-6x+9)/(9-x^2) with restrictions. Mastery answer: (x-3)/(3+x) times -1 or -(x-3)/(x+3), x != 3,-3.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P033\n- Phase: 033\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P033","phaseId":"P033","title":"Rational expression simplification"},"Tut-P034":{"Learning Goal":"Master rational restrictions and holes by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A rational expression is a ratio of polynomials.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Values that make the original denominator zero are excluded.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A common factor can create a hole after cancellation.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A non-cancelled denominator factor creates a vertical asymptote.","Core Concept":"- Restrictions come from zeros of the original denominator.\n- A cancelled denominator factor creates a hole.\n- A non-cancelled denominator factor creates a vertical asymptote.\n- The x-coordinate of a hole is the cancelled restriction.\n- The y-coordinate of a hole is found from the simplified function.\n- Domain excludes both holes and vertical asymptotes.","Worked Example":"- Find restrictions for f(x)=1/(x-4). Answer: x != 4 Reason: The denominator is zero at x=4.\n- Find restrictions for f(x)=1/((x-2)(x+3)). Answer: x != 2,-3 Reason: Both denominator factors can be zero.\n- For f(x)=(x^2-9)/(x-3), identify the hole x-value. Answer: x=3 Reason: The factor x-3 cancels, so the excluded value becomes a hole.\n- Find the hole of f(x)=(x^2-9)/(x-3). Answer: (3,6) Reason: Simplify to x+3, then evaluate at x=3 to get y=6.","Common Mistakes":"- Cancelling terms that are not common factors.\n- Forgetting restrictions from the original denominator.\n- Calling every excluded value an asymptote.\n- Ignoring holes after simplifying.\n- Using y-values instead of x-values for denominator restrictions.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Find the vertical asymptote of f(x)=1/(x+5). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x=-5.\n- Prompt: For f(x)=((x-1)(x+2))/((x-1)(x-3)), identify the hole and vertical asymptote. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: hole at x=1; vertical asymptote x=3.\n- Prompt: Find the hole coordinate for f(x)=((x-1)(x+2))/((x-1)(x-3)). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (1,-3/2).\n- Prompt: Find the domain of f(x)=((x+4)(x-2))/((x-2)(x+1)). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: all real x except x=2 and x=-1.\n- Prompt: Does f(x)=(x+1)/(x-2) have a hole? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: no.\n- Prompt: For f(x)=(x+5)/(x+5), what is the domain and graph feature? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x != -5; hole at (-5,1).","Independent Practice":"- Factor first: Find holes of f(x)=(x^2+x-6)/(x-2). Expected answer: hole at x=2.\n- Hole coordinate after factoring: Find the hole coordinate of f(x)=(x^2+x-6)/(x-2). Expected answer: (2,5).\n- Asymptote after factoring: Find the vertical asymptote of f(x)=(x^2-4)/(x^2-5x+6). Expected answer: x=3.\n- Hole after factoring: Find the hole of f(x)=(x^2-4)/(x^2-5x+6). Expected answer: (2,-4).\n- Domain after simplification: Simplify f(x)=(x^2-4)/(x^2-5x+6) and state the domain restrictions. Expected answer: (x+2)/(x-3), x != 2,3.\n- Error analysis all asymptotes: A student says f(x)=((x-2)(x+1))/((x-2)(x-5)) has asymptotes x=2 and x=5. What is wrong? Expected answer: x=2 is a hole, not an asymptote..","Mastery Check":"- For f(x)=(x^2-1)/(x-1), can x=1 be used after simplifying? Mastery answer: no.\n- Build a rational function with a hole at x=4 and vertical asymptote x=-2. Mastery answer: (x-4)/((x-4)(x+2)).\n- A graph follows y=x+1 except for an open circle at (2,3). What rational feature is shown? Mastery answer: a hole at (2,3).\n- For f(x)=((x+3)(x-1))/((x+3)(x-4)), give domain restrictions, hole, and vertical asymptote. Mastery answer: x != -3,4; hole (-3,4/7); vertical asymptote x=4.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P034\n- Phase: 034\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P034","phaseId":"P034","title":"Rational restrictions and holes"},"Tut-P035":{"Learning Goal":"Master rational asymptotes by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Factor numerators and denominators before deciding what cancels.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Values that make the original denominator zero are restrictions.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A canceled factor creates a hole; an uncanceled denominator factor can create a vertical asymptote.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Compare polynomial degrees to predict end behavior of rational functions.","Core Concept":"- Vertical asymptotes come from uncanceled denominator zeros.\n- Horizontal asymptotes depend on the degree comparison after the rational expression is simplified.\n- If numerator and denominator have the same degree, the horizontal asymptote is the ratio of leading coefficients.\n- If the numerator degree is smaller, the horizontal asymptote is y = 0.\n- If the numerator degree is exactly one more, a slant asymptote comes from polynomial division.\n- Holes and asymptotes both come from restrictions, but they describe different graph behavior.","Worked Example":"- Find the vertical asymptote of f(x) = 5/(x - 3). Answer: x = 3 Reason: The denominator is zero when x - 3 = 0, so x = 3. No factor cancels, so the graph has a vertical asymptote at x = 3.\n- For f(x) = (x - 2)/((x - 2)(x + 5)), identify the hole and the vertical asymptote. Answer: Hole at x = 2; vertical asymptote x = -5 Reason: The factor x - 2 cancels, so x = 2 is a hole. The remaining denominator x + 5 is zero at x = -5, giving a vertical asymptote.\n- Find the horizontal asymptote of f(x) = (4x + 1)/(2x^2 - 7). Answer: y = 0 Reason: The numerator has degree 1 and the denominator has degree 2. Since the numerator degree is smaller, f(x) approaches 0 as x grows large.\n- Find the horizontal asymptote of f(x) = (6x^2 - 5)/(3x^2 + x + 9). Answer: y = 2 Reason: The degrees are equal, so use the ratio of leading coefficients: 6/3 = 2. The horizontal asymptote is y = 2.","Common Mistakes":"- Treating every excluded x-value as a vertical asymptote.\n- Finding horizontal asymptotes from constant terms instead of leading terms.\n- Forgetting to simplify before classifying holes and vertical asymptotes.\n- Claiming a horizontal asymptote whenever the numerator has larger degree.\n- Using the quotient from division but forgetting to drop the remainder for a slant asymptote.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Does f(x) = (x^3 + 2)/(x^2 + 1) have a horizontal asymptote? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: No horizontal asymptote.\n- Prompt: Find the slant asymptote of f(x) = (x^2 + 3x + 1)/(x + 1). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: y = x + 2.\n- Prompt: Find the vertical asymptotes of f(x) = (x + 1)/(x^2 - 9). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = 3 and x = -3.\n- Prompt: For f(x) = (x^2 - 4)/(x^2 - 5x + 6), find the vertical asymptote and hole. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Vertical asymptote x = 3; hole at x = 2.\n- Prompt: Find the horizontal asymptote of f(x) = (2x^2 + 2x)/(x^2 - 1). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: y = 2.\n- Prompt: Find the hole of f(x) = (x^2 - 1)/(x - 1). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Hole at (1, 2).","Independent Practice":"- two vertical asymptotes and one horizontal asymptote: Find the vertical and horizontal asymptotes of f(x) = (3x^2 + 1)/(x^2 - 4). Expected answer: Vertical asymptotes x = 2 and x = -2; horizontal asymptote y = 3.\n- slant asymptote with negative leading term: Find the slant asymptote of f(x) = (2x^2 - x + 4)/(x - 2). Expected answer: y = 2x + 3.\n- asymptote from transformed reciprocal: Find the vertical and horizontal asymptotes of f(x) = 4/(x + 2) - 5. Expected answer: Vertical asymptote x = -2; horizontal asymptote y = -5.\n- domain restriction that becomes a hole: For f(x) = (x^2 + 5x + 6)/(x + 2), find the removable restriction and the simplified graph. Expected answer: Removable restriction x = -2; simplified graph y = x + 3.\n- classifying all asymptote types: Classify the end behavior and vertical asymptote of f(x) = (x^2 + 1)/(x - 4). Expected answer: Vertical asymptote x = 4; slant asymptote y = x + 4.\n- build a rational function from asymptotes: Give one rational function with vertical asymptote x = 1 and horizontal asymptote y = 3. Expected answer: One answer is f(x) = 3 + 2/(x - 1).","Mastery Check":"- A player says f(x) = (x - 4)/(x^2 - 16) has vertical asymptotes x = 4 and x = -4. What is the correction? Mastery answer: x = 4 is a hole; x = -4 is the only vertical asymptote.\n- A rational function has values 101, 201, and 1001 near x = 2 from the right, while values far left and far right approach 4. Name the likely asymptotes. Mastery answer: Vertical asymptote x = 2; horizontal asymptote y = 4.\n- Find the slant asymptote of f(x) = (x^2 - 9)/(x - 1). Mastery answer: y = x + 1.\n- For f(x) = (2x^2 - 8)/(x^2 - x - 6), identify holes, vertical asymptotes, and horizontal asymptote. Mastery answer: Hole at x = -2; vertical asymptote x = 3; horizontal asymptote y = 2.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P035\n- Phase: 035\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P035","phaseId":"P035","title":"Rational asymptotes"},"Tut-P036":{"Learning Goal":"Master exponential growth by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A percent increase of r becomes a multiplier of 1 + r.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Repeated multiplication creates exponential change.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Function notation such as f(3) means evaluate when the input is 3.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Powers count repeated factors, not repeated addition.","Core Concept":"- An exponential growth model has the form A = a b^t with a \u003e 0 and b \u003e 1.\n- The initial value is the output when t = 0.\n- The growth factor b is the multiplier for each equal time step.\n- The percent growth rate is b - 1, written as a percent.\n- Doubling models use a growth factor of 2 for each doubling interval.\n- Continuous growth uses A = P e^(rt), where r is the continuous growth rate.","Worked Example":"- If f(t) = 3(2^t), find f(4). Answer: 48 Reason: Substitute t = 4: f(4) = 3(2^4) = 3(16) = 48.\n- For A(t) = 80(1.25^t), name the initial value and growth factor. Answer: Initial value 80; growth factor 1.25 Reason: When t = 0, 1.25^0 = 1, so the starting amount is 80. The base 1.25 is the multiplier each period.\n- What growth factor represents a 7 percent increase each year? Answer: 1.07 Reason: A 7 percent increase means keep 100 percent and add 7 percent, so the multiplier is 1 + 0.07 = 1.07.\n- A town has 5000 people and grows by 4 percent per year. Write a model for the population after t years. Answer: P(t) = 5000(1.04^t) Reason: The initial value is 5000. A 4 percent increase gives growth factor 1.04, so the model is P(t) = 5000(1.04^t).","Common Mistakes":"- Adding the percent rate each period instead of multiplying by the growth factor.\n- Using 12 for a 12 percent increase instead of 1.12.\n- Mistaking the initial value for the growth factor.\n- Assuming every increasing table is exponential.\n- Rounding too early in a multi-step model.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: A video has 200 views and grows by 50 percent per day. How many views are expected after 3 days? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 675 views.\n- Prompt: A table shows 6, 18, 54, 162 for equal time steps. What is the growth factor? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 3.\n- Prompt: An exponential sequence has A(0) = 12 and A(1) = 18. Write A(t). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: A(t) = 12(1.5^t).\n- Prompt: A bacteria culture starts with 40 cells and doubles every hour. Write a model for t hours. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: B(t) = 40(2^t).\n- Prompt: If B(t) = 40(2^t), when will the culture reach 320 cells? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: t = 3 hours.\n- Prompt: Which table is exponential: A has outputs 5, 10, 15, 20; B has outputs 5, 10, 20, 40? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Table B.","Independent Practice":"- find percent growth from growth factor: A model has growth factor 1.18. What is the percent growth rate? Expected answer: 18 percent.\n- evaluate growth with fractional initial value: A plant is 2.5 cm tall and grows by 20 percent each week. What height is predicted after 2 weeks? Expected answer: 3.6 cm.\n- growth from graph intercept: An exponential growth graph crosses the y-axis at 9 and triples each step. Write the model. Expected answer: y = 9(3^x).\n- compare two growth models: Which is larger at t = 3: A(t) = 100(1.1^t) or B(t) = 80(1.2^t)? Expected answer: B(3) is larger.\n- correct a growth comparison: A player says A(t) = 100(1.1^t) is larger than B(t) = 80(1.2^t) at t = 3 because 100 \u003e 80. What is the correct comparison? Expected answer: B(3) is larger.\n- solve for starting value: An exponential model with growth factor 1.5 has value 54 at t = 2. What is the initial value? Expected answer: 24.","Mastery Check":"- A quantity follows A(t) = 300e^(0.02t). What is A(10) to the nearest whole number? Mastery answer: 366.\n- A quantity follows A(t) = 500e^(0.04t). What is A(3) to the nearest whole number? Mastery answer: 564.\n- A value grows from 20 to 45 in two equal steps. What is the growth factor per step? Mastery answer: 1.5.\n- A game item worth 30 coins increases by 10 percent per level. How much is it worth after 5 levels, to the nearest coin? Mastery answer: 48 coins.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P036\n- Phase: 036\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P036","phaseId":"P036","title":"Exponential growth"},"Tut-P037":{"Learning Goal":"Master exponential decay by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A percent decrease of r becomes a multiplier of 1 - r.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? An exponential model uses repeated multiplication over equal intervals.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A base between 0 and 1 creates decay when the initial value is positive.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Half-life means multiplying by 1/2 for each half-life interval.","Core Concept":"- An exponential decay model has the form A = a b^t with a \u003e 0 and 0 \u003c b \u003c 1.\n- The decay factor b is the amount kept each period.\n- The percent decay rate is 1 - b, written as a percent.\n- Half-life models can be written A = A0(1/2)^(t/h), where h is the half-life.\n- Exponential decay approaches zero for positive initial values but does not reach zero in the basic model.\n- Continuous decay uses A = P e^(-rt), where r is positive.","Worked Example":"- For A(t) = 120(0.8^t), what is the decay factor? Answer: 0.8 Reason: The base of the exponential expression is the per-period multiplier, so the decay factor is 0.8.\n- What decay factor represents a 12 percent decrease each month? Answer: 0.88 Reason: A 12 percent decrease means 88 percent remains, so the multiplier is 1 - 0.12 = 0.88.\n- If f(t) = 50(0.5^t), find f(3). Answer: 6.25 Reason: Substitute t = 3: f(3) = 50(0.5^3) = 50(0.125) = 6.25.\n- A car is worth 18000 dollars and loses 10 percent of its value each year. Write V(t). Answer: V(t) = 18000(0.90^t) Reason: The initial value is 18000. Losing 10 percent means keeping 90 percent, so the decay factor is 0.90.","Common Mistakes":"- Using 0.15 instead of 0.85 for a 15 percent decrease.\n- Subtracting the same amount each period instead of multiplying by the same factor.\n- Making the base negative because the situation is decreasing.\n- Confusing half-life with subtracting half of the original amount each interval.\n- Rounding the decay factor before solving when an exact fraction is available.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: A bike worth 600 dollars loses 20 percent of its value each year. What is it worth after 2 years? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 384 dollars.\n- Prompt: A model has decay factor 0.73. What percent decrease does this represent? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 27 percent.\n- Prompt: A table shows 160, 80, 40, 20 for equal time steps. What exponential model starts at t = 0? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: A(t) = 160(0.5^t).\n- Prompt: A sample starts with 96 grams and has half-life 5 hours. Write a model for t hours. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: A(t) = 96(1/2)^(t/5).\n- Prompt: Using A(t) = 96(1/2)^(t/5), how much remains after 15 hours? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 12 grams.\n- Prompt: If A(t) = 200(0.5^t), when is A(t) = 25? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: t = 3.","Independent Practice":"- distinguish linear decrease from exponential decay: Which table is exponential decay: A has outputs 100, 90, 80, 70; B has outputs 100, 80, 64, 51.2? Expected answer: Table B.\n- initial value from later value: A model has decay factor 0.5 and A(4) = 10. What is A(0)? Expected answer: 160.\n- continuous decay evaluation: A medicine amount is M(t) = 80e^(-0.25t). How much remains after 4 hours, to the nearest tenth? Expected answer: 29.4.\n- compare two decay models: Which is larger at t = 2: A(t) = 100(0.7^t) or B(t) = 80(0.8^t)? Expected answer: B(2) is larger.\n- decay graph interpretation: A decay graph has y-intercept 300 and each step keeps 60 percent. Write the equation. Expected answer: y = 300(0.6^x).\n- reverse engineer a decay factor: A value drops from 500 to 320 in two equal steps. What is the decay factor per step? Expected answer: 0.8.","Mastery Check":"- A player models a 30 percent decrease from 90 as 90(0.30^t). What should the model be? Mastery answer: A(t) = 90(0.70^t).\n- For A(t) = 12(0.25^t), what value does A(t) approach as t becomes very large? Mastery answer: 0.\n- A 72 gram sample has half-life 3 days. How much remains after 12 days? Mastery answer: 4.5 grams.\n- A phone battery has 90 percent charge and loses 18 percent of its charge each hour. Estimate the charge after 3 hours to the nearest percent. Mastery answer: 50 percent.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P037\n- Phase: 037\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P037","phaseId":"P037","title":"Exponential decay"},"Tut-P038":{"Learning Goal":"Master compound interest by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Convert percent rates to decimals before using a formula.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Periodic compounding uses A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt).\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Continuous compounding uses A = Pe^(rt).\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The principal P is the starting amount.","Core Concept":"- More frequent compounding changes both the periodic rate and the number of periods.\n- The annual rate r is divided by n before it is applied each period.\n- The exponent nt multiplies years by periods per year.\n- Interest earned is final amount minus principal.\n- Continuous compounding is the limiting case A = Pe^(rt).\n- Solving for principal means dividing the future amount by the growth factor.","Worked Example":"- Invest 1000 dollars at 6 percent compounded annually for 3 years. What is the amount? Answer: $1191.02 Reason: Use A = 1000(1.06)^3. Since 1.06^3 = 1.191016, the amount is $1191.02.\n- In A = 2500(1 + 0.04/4)^(4t), what are P, r, and n? Answer: P = 2500, r = 0.04, n = 4 Reason: The form is P(1 + r/n)^(nt). Here the principal is 2500, the annual rate is 0.04, and quarterly compounding means n = 4.\n- Find the amount from 2000 dollars at 8 percent compounded quarterly for 1 year. Answer: $2164.86 Reason: Use A = 2000(1 + 0.08/4)^4 = 2000(1.02)^4. This equals about $2164.86.\n- For 500 dollars at 12 percent compounded monthly for 2 years, what expression gives the amount? Answer: 500(1.01)^24 Reason: The monthly rate is 0.12/12 = 0.01 and the number of months is 12(2) = 24.","Common Mistakes":"- Using r = 5 instead of r = 0.05 for 5 percent.\n- Putting n only in the exponent and not in the periodic rate.\n- Using simple interest when the problem says compounded.\n- Forgetting that monthly compounding has 12 periods per year.\n- Rounding inside the exponent before evaluating the final amount.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Evaluate 500 dollars at 12 percent compounded monthly for 2 years. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: $634.87.\n- Prompt: If 1500 dollars grows to 1686.23 dollars, how much interest was earned? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: $186.23.\n- Prompt: Invest 800 dollars at 5 percent compounded semiannually for 4 years. Find the amount. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: $974.72.\n- Prompt: Write, but do not evaluate, the amount for 1200 dollars at 3 percent compounded daily for 5 years using 365 days per year. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: A = 1200(1 + 0.03/365)^1825.\n- Prompt: Write the continuous compounding model for 700 dollars at 4.5 percent for t years. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: A(t) = 700e^(0.045t).\n- Prompt: Find the amount for 1000 dollars at 5 percent compounded continuously for 2 years. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: $1105.17.","Independent Practice":"- solve for principal: How much principal is needed to have 1210 dollars after 2 years at 10 percent compounded annually? Expected answer: $1000.\n- compare compounding frequencies: Which is larger after 1 year at 6 percent on 1000 dollars: annual compounding or monthly compounding? Expected answer: Monthly compounding.\n- find total periods: An investment is compounded weekly for 3 years. How many compounding periods are used? Expected answer: 156 periods.\n- rate conversion: What decimal rate should be used for 7.25 percent interest? Expected answer: 0.0725.\n- choose correct compound expression: Which expression models 900 dollars at 6 percent compounded quarterly for 3 years: 900(1.06)^3 or 900(1.015)^12? Expected answer: 900(1.015)^12.\n- future value target by inspection: At 100 percent annual interest compounded annually, how long does 300 dollars take to become 2400 dollars? Expected answer: 3 years.","Mastery Check":"- Find the amount for 250 dollars at 4 percent compounded annually for 6 years, rounded to cents. Mastery answer: $316.33.\n- A player writes 1000(1.08)^8 for 8 percent compounded quarterly for 2 years. What is the correct expression? Mastery answer: 1000(1.02)^8.\n- For 1000 dollars at 10 percent for 1 year, which is larger: annual compounding or continuous compounding? Mastery answer: Continuous compounding.\n- A savings account has 4000 dollars at 3.6 percent compounded monthly. Write the model and find the amount after 5 years. Mastery answer: A(t) = 4000(1.003)^(12t); A(5) is about $4787.58.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P038\n- Phase: 038\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P038","phaseId":"P038","title":"Compound interest"},"Tut-P039":{"Learning Goal":"Master logarithm definitions by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? An exponent tells how many times a base is used as a factor.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Exponential and logarithmic forms describe the same relationship.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The base of a logarithm must be positive and not equal to 1.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The argument of a logarithm must be positive.","Core Concept":"- log_b(a) = c means b^c = a.\n- A logarithm answers the question: what exponent goes on the base?\n- Logarithmic functions are inverses of exponential functions with the same base.\n- Logarithms of powers of the base evaluate cleanly.\n- The domain restriction comes from the argument being positive.\n- Exact log values often come from rewriting numbers as powers.","Worked Example":"- Rewrite log_2(8) = 3 in exponential form. Answer: 2^3 = 8 Reason: The base of the log is 2, the output is 3, and the argument is 8, so 2^3 = 8.\n- Rewrite 5^2 = 25 in logarithmic form. Answer: log_5(25) = 2 Reason: The base stays 5, the result 25 becomes the log argument, and the exponent 2 becomes the log value.\n- Evaluate log_3(81). Answer: 4 Reason: Since 3^4 = 81, log_3(81) = 4.\n- Evaluate log_5(1/25). Answer: -2 Reason: Since 5^-2 = 1/25, the logarithm equals -2.","Common Mistakes":"- Swapping the base and the argument when converting forms.\n- Treating log_b(a) as b times a.\n- Allowing zero or negative arguments in real logarithms.\n- Forgetting that log(x) means log base 10 in many precalculus contexts.\n- Assuming every logarithm has a whole-number value.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Evaluate log(1000). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 3.\n- Prompt: Evaluate ln(e^7). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 7.\n- Prompt: If log_4(x) = 3, find x. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 64.\n- Prompt: If log_2(1/8) = x, find x. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: -3.\n- Prompt: Find the domain of f(x) = log_3(x - 5). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x \u003e 5.\n- Prompt: Find the domain of g(x) = ln(2x + 6). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x \u003e -3.","Independent Practice":"- identify base and argument: In log_7(49) = 2, name the base, argument, and value. Expected answer: Base 7; argument 49; value 2.\n- evaluate log with fractional base: Evaluate log_(1/2)(8). Expected answer: -3.\n- rewrite exponential decay as logarithm: Rewrite (1/3)^4 = 1/81 in logarithmic form. Expected answer: log_(1/3)(1/81) = 4.\n- log equals zero: Evaluate log_9(1). Expected answer: 0.\n- log equals one: Evaluate log_11(11). Expected answer: 1.\n- error analysis base swap: A player rewrites log_4(64) = 3 as 64^3 = 4. What is the correct exponential form? Expected answer: 4^3 = 64.","Mastery Check":"- Is log_2(-8) a real number? Mastery answer: No real value.\n- If log_b(27) = 3 and b \u003e 0, find b. Mastery answer: 3.\n- Simplify 6^(log_6(20)). Mastery answer: 20.\n- Find x if log_2(x - 1) = 5. Mastery answer: 33.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P039\n- Phase: 039\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P039","phaseId":"P039","title":"Logarithm definitions"},"Tut-P040":{"Learning Goal":"Master logarithm properties by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Logarithms convert multiplication into addition.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Logarithms convert division into subtraction.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Exponents inside a logarithm can move to the front as coefficients.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Arguments of all logarithms must be positive.","Core Concept":"- Product property: log_b(MN) = log_b(M) + log_b(N).\n- Quotient property: log_b(M/N) = log_b(M) - log_b(N).\n- Power property: log_b(M^p) = p log_b(M).\n- Condensing reverses expansion and requires matching bases.\n- Change of base: log_b(a) = log(a)/log(b) = ln(a)/ln(b).\n- Expanded forms can introduce hidden domain conditions from each log argument.","Worked Example":"- Expand log_3(5x). Answer: log_3(5) + log_3(x) Reason: The product property changes multiplication inside one log into addition of two logs.\n- Expand log_2(x/7). Answer: log_2(x) - log_2(7) Reason: The quotient property changes division inside the log into subtraction.\n- Expand log_5(y^4). Answer: 4log_5(y) Reason: The power property moves the exponent 4 to the front as a coefficient.\n- Expand ln(3x^2/y). Answer: ln(3) + 2ln(x) - ln(y) Reason: Use product, power, and quotient properties: ln(3x^2/y) = ln(3) + ln(x^2) - ln(y) = ln(3) + 2ln(x) - ln(y).","Common Mistakes":"- Writing log(a + b) as log(a) + log(b).\n- Moving a coefficient into the log as multiplication instead of as a power.\n- Condensing logs with different bases.\n- Forgetting parentheses when applying quotient properties.\n- Ignoring positive-argument restrictions after expanding.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Condense log_4(2) + log_4(x). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: log_4(2x).\n- Prompt: Condense log_6(a) - log_6(b). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: log_6(a/b).\n- Prompt: Condense 3log_2(x). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: log_2(x^3).\n- Prompt: Condense 2ln(x) + ln(5) - ln(y). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: ln(5x^2/y).\n- Prompt: Rewrite log_7(20) using common logs. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: log(20)/log(7).\n- Prompt: Use change of base to evaluate log_2(32). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 5.","Independent Practice":"- domain after expansion: What restrictions are needed for ln(x - 1) + ln(x + 3)? Expected answer: x \u003e 1.\n- false sum rule: Is log_2(x + 5) equal to log_2(x) + log_2(5)? Expected answer: No.\n- expand square root: Expand log_3(sqrt(x)). Expected answer: (1/2)log_3(x).\n- condense with fractional coefficient: Condense (1/2)ln(x) + ln(4). Expected answer: ln(4sqrt(x)).\n- expand factored argument: Expand log_5((x - 1)(x + 2)). Expected answer: log_5(x - 1) + log_5(x + 2).\n- condense three logs: Condense log(x) + log(x - 1) - log(2). Expected answer: log(x(x - 1)/2).","Mastery Check":"- A player condenses 4log_3(x) as log_3(4x). What is the correct condensed form? Mastery answer: log_3(x^4).\n- Which is correct: log_5(12) = log(5)/log(12) or log(12)/log(5)? Mastery answer: log(12)/log(5).\n- Use properties to evaluate log_2(8) + log_2(4). Mastery answer: 5.\n- Expand ln((x^2 - 9)/(x + 1)) and state restrictions for the expanded logs. Mastery answer: ln(x - 3) + ln(x + 3) - ln(x + 1); restrictions x \u003e 3.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P040\n- Phase: 040\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P040","phaseId":"P040","title":"Logarithm properties"},"Tut-P041":{"Learning Goal":"Master logarithmic equations by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Convert log_b(A) = c into A = b^c.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Combine same-base logarithms before using one-to-one reasoning.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Every logarithm argument must be positive.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Solutions from algebra must be checked in the original equation.","Core Concept":"- A single logarithm equal to a number can be solved by rewriting in exponential form.\n- Same-base log equations can use the one-to-one property after domain checks.\n- Product and quotient properties can combine logs before solving.\n- Some algebraic candidates are extraneous because they make a log argument nonpositive.\n- Equations with logs on both sides often become equations between the arguments.\n- Approximate answers are reasonable when exact powers are not available.","Worked Example":"- Solve log_3(x) = 4. Answer: x = 81 Reason: Rewrite as x = 3^4. Since 3^4 = 81, x = 81.\n- Solve log_2(x - 1) = 5. Answer: x = 33 Reason: Rewrite as x - 1 = 2^5 = 32, so x = 33. The argument x - 1 is positive.\n- Solve ln(2x + 4) = 0. Answer: x = -3/2 Reason: Since ln(1) = 0, set 2x + 4 = 1. Then 2x = -3, so x = -3/2, which makes the argument positive.\n- Solve log_5(x + 7) = log_5(3x - 1). Answer: x = 4 Reason: With matching bases, set arguments equal: x + 7 = 3x - 1. Then 8 = 2x, so x = 4. Both arguments are positive.","Common Mistakes":"- Keeping an answer that makes a logarithm argument zero or negative.\n- Applying one-to-one reasoning before combining logs with the same base.\n- Dropping parentheses after converting from logarithmic form.\n- Solving log(x) + log(3) = log(12) as x + 3 = 12.\n- Using base 10 rules for natural logs without noticing that the base still matches on both sides.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Solve log_2(x) + log_2(3) = log_2(24). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = 8.\n- Prompt: Solve log_4(x) - log_4(2) = 3. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = 128.\n- Prompt: Solve log(x) = 2. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = 100.\n- Prompt: Solve ln(x) = 3. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = e^3.\n- Prompt: Solve 2log_3(x) = 4. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = 9.\n- Prompt: Solve log_2(x^2) = 6 for positive x. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = 8.","Independent Practice":"- extraneous solution check: Solve log_3(x - 2) + log_3(x) = log_3(3). Expected answer: x = 3.\n- equation with log on both sides: Solve ln(5x - 4) = ln(x + 8). Expected answer: x = 3.\n- solve after condensing quotient: Solve log_5(x + 4) - log_5(x) = 1. Expected answer: x = 1.\n- solve with base ten power: Solve log(2x - 6) = 1. Expected answer: x = 8.\n- solve with e and shift: Solve ln(x + 2) = ln(7). Expected answer: x = 5.\n- error analysis domain check: A player solves log_2(x - 3) = log_2(1 - x) and gets x = 2. What happens? Expected answer: There is no real solution.","Mastery Check":"- Solve log_2(x) + log_2(x - 2) = 3. Mastery answer: x = 4.\n- Solve log_2(x) = 5.5 exactly in exponential form. Mastery answer: x = 2^5.5.\n- Solve log_3(x) + log_3(x) = 4 for positive x. Mastery answer: x = 9.\n- Solve log_4(x - 1) + log_4(2) = log_4(10). Mastery answer: x = 6.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P041\n- Phase: 041\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P041","phaseId":"P041","title":"Logarithmic equations"},"Tut-P042":{"Learning Goal":"Master degree and radian measure by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A full circle is 360 degrees or 2pi radians.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A half circle is 180 degrees or pi radians.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? To convert degrees to radians, multiply by pi/180.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? To convert radians to degrees, multiply by 180/pi.","Core Concept":"- Degrees and radians are two units for measuring the same angle rotation.\n- Radians connect angle measure to arc length on a circle.\n- Coterminal angles differ by full rotations of 360 degrees or 2pi radians.\n- Positive angles rotate counterclockwise; negative angles rotate clockwise.\n- Special angles often convert to simple multiples of pi.\n- Angular speed can be measured in radians per unit time.","Worked Example":"- Convert 60 degrees to radians. Answer: pi/3 radians Reason: Multiply by pi/180: 60(pi/180) = pi/3.\n- Convert 3pi/4 radians to degrees. Answer: 135 degrees Reason: Multiply by 180/pi: (3pi/4)(180/pi) = 135 degrees.\n- Convert -45 degrees to radians. Answer: -pi/4 radians Reason: Multiply by pi/180: -45(pi/180) = -pi/4.\n- Convert -5pi/6 radians to degrees. Answer: -150 degrees Reason: Multiply by 180/pi: (-5pi/6)(180/pi) = -150 degrees.","Common Mistakes":"- Multiplying by 180/pi when converting degrees to radians.\n- Leaving degree symbols on radian answers or pi on degree answers.\n- Using s = r theta while theta is still in degrees.\n- Adding 180 degrees instead of 360 degrees for coterminal angles.\n- Treating pi radians as 90 degrees instead of 180 degrees.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: How many radians are in 360 degrees? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 2pi radians.\n- Prompt: Find a positive coterminal angle for -70 degrees. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 290 degrees.\n- Prompt: Find a positive coterminal angle for -pi/3. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 5pi/3.\n- Prompt: Find the angle between 0 degrees and 360 degrees that is coterminal with 765 degrees. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 45 degrees.\n- Prompt: Find the angle between 0 and 2pi that is coterminal with 17pi/6. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 5pi/6.\n- Prompt: Find the arc length when r = 6 and theta = pi/3 radians. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 2pi.","Independent Practice":"- arc length after degree conversion: Find the arc length for radius 10 and central angle 90 degrees. Expected answer: 5pi.\n- central angle from arc length: A circle has radius 8 and arc length 6. What is the central angle in radians? Expected answer: 3/4 radians.\n- sector rotation direction: Does -120 degrees rotate clockwise or counterclockwise from standard position? Expected answer: Clockwise.\n- convert common special angle: Convert 210 degrees to radians. Expected answer: 7pi/6 radians.\n- convert unusual radian angle: Convert 11pi/12 radians to degrees. Expected answer: 165 degrees.\n- angular speed: A wheel turns through 10pi radians in 5 seconds. What is its angular speed? Expected answer: 2pi radians per second.","Mastery Check":"- A point 4 cm from the center rotates at pi/2 radians per second. What is its linear speed? Mastery answer: 2pi cm per second.\n- A player converts 30 degrees to 30pi radians. What is the correct conversion? Mastery answer: pi/6 radians.\n- Find the angle between 0 and 2pi coterminal with 25pi/4. Mastery answer: pi/4.\n- A 135 degree central angle cuts an arc on a circle of radius 12. Find the arc length. Mastery answer: 9pi.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P042\n- Phase: 042\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P042","phaseId":"P042","title":"Degree and radian measure"},"Tut-P043":{"Learning Goal":"Master reference angles by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? A reference angle is always positive and acute, except for quadrantal angles where it is 0.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Quadrant I angles have reference angle equal to the angle.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Quadrant II reference angle is 180 degrees minus the angle.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Quadrant III reference angle is the angle minus 180 degrees.","Core Concept":"- Reference angles measure the acute angle from the terminal side to the x-axis.\n- For radian angles, use pi, 2pi, and their fractions the same way as 180 degrees and 360 degrees.\n- Negative or large angles should be made coterminal in the standard range before finding the reference angle.\n- Reference angles help transfer exact trig values across quadrants.\n- The reference angle is positive even when the original angle is negative.\n- Quadrantal angles land on an axis and have reference angle 0.","Worked Example":"- Find the reference angle for 35 degrees. Answer: 35 degrees Reason: The angle is already in Quadrant I, so its reference angle is 35 degrees.\n- Find the reference angle for 140 degrees. Answer: 40 degrees Reason: Quadrant II reference angle is 180 degrees minus the angle: 180 - 140 = 40 degrees.\n- Find the reference angle for 225 degrees. Answer: 45 degrees Reason: Quadrant III reference angle is angle minus 180 degrees: 225 - 180 = 45 degrees.\n- Find the reference angle for 315 degrees. Answer: 45 degrees Reason: Quadrant IV reference angle is 360 degrees minus the angle: 360 - 315 = 45 degrees.","Common Mistakes":"- Returning the original obtuse angle as the reference angle.\n- Keeping a negative reference angle for a negative rotation.\n- Using 90 degrees as the reference line instead of the x-axis.\n- Forgetting to reduce angles larger than 360 degrees.\n- Confusing the reference angle with the coterminal angle.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Find the reference angle for -30 degrees. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 30 degrees.\n- Prompt: Find the reference angle for 405 degrees. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 45 degrees.\n- Prompt: Find the reference angle for 180 degrees. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 0 degrees.\n- Prompt: Find the reference angle for pi/6. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: pi/6.\n- Prompt: Find the reference angle for 5pi/6. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: pi/6.\n- Prompt: Find the reference angle for 4pi/3. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: pi/3.","Independent Practice":"- radian quadrant IV: Find the reference angle for 7pi/4. Expected answer: pi/4.\n- negative radian reference: Find the reference angle for -2pi/3. Expected answer: pi/3.\n- large radian reference: Find the reference angle for 13pi/6. Expected answer: pi/6.\n- determine quadrant and reference angle: For 250 degrees, give the quadrant and reference angle. Expected answer: Quadrant III; reference angle 70 degrees.\n- determine radian quadrant and reference: For 11pi/6, give the quadrant and reference angle. Expected answer: Quadrant IV; reference angle pi/6.\n- build angles from reference angle: List the angles between 0 degrees and 360 degrees with reference angle 30 degrees. Expected answer: 30 degrees, 150 degrees, 210 degrees, 330 degrees.","Mastery Check":"- List the angles between 0 and 2pi with reference angle pi/4. Mastery answer: pi/4, 3pi/4, 5pi/4, 7pi/4.\n- A player says the reference angle for -150 degrees is -30 degrees. What is the correct reference angle? Mastery answer: 30 degrees.\n- Find the reference angle for 3pi/2. Mastery answer: 0.\n- Find the reference angle for -17pi/6. Mastery answer: pi/6.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P043\n- Phase: 043\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P043","phaseId":"P043","title":"Reference angles"},"Tut-P044":{"Learning Goal":"Master unit circle by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? On the unit circle, a point has coordinates (cos theta, sin theta).\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Quadrants determine the signs of sine and cosine.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? The special reference angles are 30 degrees, 45 degrees, and 60 degrees, or pi/6, pi/4, and pi/3.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Tangent equals sin theta divided by cos theta when cos theta is not zero.","Core Concept":"- The unit circle has radius 1 and center at the origin.\n- Cosine is the x-coordinate and sine is the y-coordinate.\n- Reference angles give the absolute values of special trig coordinates.\n- Quadrant signs turn reference values into exact unit-circle values.\n- Quadrantal angles have coordinates on the axes.\n- Solving simple trig equations on 0 to 2pi means finding all matching unit-circle angles.","Worked Example":"- What point on the unit circle corresponds to theta = 0? Answer: (1, 0) Reason: At angle 0, the terminal side points along the positive x-axis, so the point is (1, 0).\n- Give the unit-circle coordinates for theta = pi/6. Answer: (sqrt(3)/2, 1/2) Reason: The reference angle is 30 degrees in Quadrant I, so cosine is sqrt(3)/2 and sine is 1/2.\n- Give the unit-circle coordinates for theta = pi/4. Answer: (sqrt(2)/2, sqrt(2)/2) Reason: A 45 degree reference angle has equal positive coordinates on the unit circle.\n- Give the unit-circle coordinates for theta = pi/3. Answer: (1/2, sqrt(3)/2) Reason: The reference angle is 60 degrees in Quadrant I, so cosine is 1/2 and sine is sqrt(3)/2.","Common Mistakes":"- Swapping sine and cosine coordinates.\n- Using the right reference values but the wrong quadrant signs.\n- Giving only one angle when two unit-circle angles have the same sine or cosine.\n- Treating tangent as defined when cosine is zero.\n- Mixing degree and radian labels in the same answer.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Give the unit-circle coordinates for theta = 2pi/3. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (-1/2, sqrt(3)/2).\n- Prompt: Give the unit-circle coordinates for theta = 5pi/4. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (-sqrt(2)/2, -sqrt(2)/2).\n- Prompt: Give the unit-circle coordinates for theta = 11pi/6. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (sqrt(3)/2, -1/2).\n- Prompt: If the unit-circle point is (1/2, sqrt(3)/2), what is sin theta? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: sqrt(3)/2.\n- Prompt: If the unit-circle point is (-sqrt(3)/2, 1/2), what is cos theta? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: -sqrt(3)/2.\n- Prompt: Find tan(pi/4). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 1.","Independent Practice":"- tangent undefined: Find tan(pi/2). Expected answer: Undefined.\n- quadrantal sine and cosine: Find cos(pi) and sin(pi). Expected answer: cos(pi) = -1; sin(pi) = 0.\n- angle from point: Which angle between 0 and 2pi has unit-circle point (0, -1)? Expected answer: 3pi/2.\n- all angles with given sine: Find all theta in [0, 2pi) with sin theta = 1/2. Expected answer: theta = pi/6 and 5pi/6.\n- all angles with given cosine: Find all theta in [0, 2pi) with cos theta = -sqrt(2)/2. Expected answer: theta = 3pi/4 and 5pi/4.\n- coterminal unit-circle value: Find sin(13pi/6). Expected answer: 1/2.","Mastery Check":"- Give the unit-circle coordinates for 300 degrees. Mastery answer: (1/2, -sqrt(3)/2).\n- A player says the point for pi/6 is (1/2, sqrt(3)/2). What is the correct point? Mastery answer: (sqrt(3)/2, 1/2).\n- A Quadrant II unit-circle point has y-coordinate 3/5. What is its x-coordinate? Mastery answer: -4/5.\n- Find cos(7pi/6), sin(7pi/6), and tan(7pi/6). Mastery answer: cos(7pi/6) = -sqrt(3)/2; sin(7pi/6) = -1/2; tan(7pi/6) = sqrt(3)/3.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P044\n- Phase: 044\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P044","phaseId":"P044","title":"Unit circle"},"Tut-P045":{"Learning Goal":"Master exact trigonometric values by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Convert between degrees and radians for common angles such as 30 degrees, 45 degrees, 60 degrees, and 180 degrees.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use 30-60-90 and 45-45-90 triangles to recall sine, cosine, and tangent values.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Locate an angle on the unit circle and identify its reference angle.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Apply quadrant signs for sine, cosine, and tangent before writing the final value.","Core Concept":"- Exact trigonometric values come from unit-circle coordinates and special right triangles.\n- The cosine of an angle is the x-coordinate and the sine is the y-coordinate on the unit circle.\n- Tangent is sine divided by cosine, so its sign depends on both coordinates.\n- Reciprocal functions use csc x = 1/sin x, sec x = 1/cos x, and cot x = 1/tan x when defined.\n- Coterminal angles have the same exact trigonometric values.","Worked Example":"- Find the exact value of sin(pi/6). Answer: 1/2 Reason: The angle pi/6 is 30 degrees. On the unit circle its y-coordinate is 1/2, so sin(pi/6) = 1/2.\n- Find the exact value of cos(2pi/3). Answer: -1/2 Reason: The reference angle for 2pi/3 is pi/3. Cosine is negative in quadrant II, so cos(2pi/3) = -cos(pi/3) = -1/2.\n- Find the exact value of tan(5pi/4). Answer: 1 Reason: The reference angle is pi/4. In quadrant III, sine and cosine are both negative, so tangent is positive. Thus tan(5pi/4) = 1.\n- Find the exact value of csc(7pi/6). Answer: -2 Reason: Since 7pi/6 has reference angle pi/6 in quadrant III, sin(7pi/6) = -1/2. Therefore csc(7pi/6) = 1/(-1/2) = -2.","Common Mistakes":"- Using the reference angle value but forgetting the quadrant sign.\n- Treating secant, cosecant, and cotangent as inverse angle functions instead of reciprocals.\n- Giving decimal approximations when an exact value is requested.\n- Confusing the sine and cosine coordinates for 30 degree and 60 degree angles.\n- Forgetting that tangent is undefined when cosine is zero.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Find the exact value of sec(11pi/6). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 2sqrt(3)/3.\n- Prompt: Find the exact value of cot(3pi/4). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: -1.\n- Prompt: Find the exact value of sin(300 degrees). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: -sqrt(3)/2.\n- Prompt: Find the exact value of cos(-pi/3). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 1/2.\n- Prompt: Find the exact value of tan(-5pi/6). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: sqrt(3)/3.\n- Prompt: Find the exact value of sin(13pi/6). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 1/2.","Independent Practice":"- Exact expression with sine and cosine: Evaluate 2cos(pi/3) + sin(pi/2). Expected answer: 2.\n- Exact product using tangent and cosine: Evaluate tan(pi/3)cos(pi/6). Expected answer: 3/2.\n- Angle identification from signs and cosine: Find theta in [0, 2pi) if cos(theta) = -sqrt(2)/2 and sin(theta) \u003e 0. Expected answer: 3pi/4.\n- All three primary functions at one angle: Find sin(theta), cos(theta), and tan(theta) for theta = 7pi/4. Expected answer: sin(theta) = -sqrt(2)/2, cos(theta) = sqrt(2)/2, tan(theta) = -1.\n- Angle from a unit-circle point: A unit-circle point is (-1/2, -sqrt(3)/2). Find the angle theta in [0, 2pi). Expected answer: 4pi/3.\n- Reciprocal expression with cotangent: Evaluate sec(pi) - cot(5pi/4). Expected answer: -2.","Mastery Check":"- Compare sin(5pi/6) and cos(pi/3). Which is larger? Mastery answer: They are equal..\n- Evaluate (sin(4pi/3) + cos(5pi/6))/tan(7pi/6). Mastery answer: -3.\n- Give an angle theta in [0, 2pi) such that tan(theta) = -sqrt(3) and cos(theta) \u003c 0. Mastery answer: 2pi/3.\n- Starting at (1, 0), rotate counterclockwise by 5pi/3. What unit-circle point do you reach? Mastery answer: (1/2, -sqrt(3)/2).","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P045\n- Phase: 045\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P045","phaseId":"P045","title":"Exact trigonometric values"},"Tut-P046":{"Learning Goal":"Master trigonometric graphs by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Connect sine and cosine values to unit-circle coordinates.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Plot points in the coordinate plane using radians on the x-axis.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Identify x-intercepts, maximum values, minimum values, and increasing intervals from a graph.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Recognize that tangent has vertical asymptotes where cosine is zero.","Core Concept":"- The basic sine graph starts at (0, 0), rises to 1 at pi/2, returns to 0 at pi, reaches -1 at 3pi/2, and returns to 0 at 2pi.\n- The basic cosine graph starts at (0, 1), reaches 0 at pi/2, reaches -1 at pi, returns to 0 at 3pi/2, and returns to 1 at 2pi.\n- The tangent graph has period pi and vertical asymptotes at pi/2 + kpi.\n- Graph features can be read from key points, intercepts, symmetry, and asymptotes.\n- A graph can be identified by its starting value and first direction of motion.","Worked Example":"- On the graph of y = sin x over [0, 2pi], what is the maximum value and where does it first occur? Answer: The maximum value is 1 at x = pi/2. Reason: The basic sine graph reaches its highest point at the top of the unit circle. On [0, 2pi], that first happens at x = pi/2 with y = 1.\n- Find the x-intercepts of y = cos x on [0, 2pi]. Answer: x = pi/2 and x = 3pi/2 Reason: Cosine is the x-coordinate on the unit circle. It equals 0 at pi/2 and 3pi/2, so those are the x-intercepts.\n- Find the vertical asymptotes of y = tan x on (-pi, pi). Answer: x = -pi/2 and x = pi/2 Reason: tan x = sin x/cos x, so tangent has vertical asymptotes where cos x = 0. In (-pi, pi), that occurs at -pi/2 and pi/2.\n- What is the value of y = sin x at x = 3pi/2? Answer: -1 Reason: At 3pi/2 on the unit circle, the y-coordinate is -1. Therefore the sine graph has value -1 there.","Common Mistakes":"- Thinking sine and cosine have the same starting point.\n- Drawing tangent with a highest and lowest value like sine or cosine.\n- Forgetting the vertical asymptotes of tangent.\n- Counting endpoints incorrectly on interval questions.\n- Reading radians as if they were degrees on the horizontal axis.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: A basic trig graph starts at y = 1 when x = 0 and first reaches y = 0 at x = pi/2. Which function is it? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: y = cos x.\n- Prompt: List the x-intercepts of y = sin x on [0, 2pi]. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = 0, pi, and 2pi.\n- Prompt: On [0, 2pi], where is y = sin x increasing? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: From x = 0 to x = pi/2 and from x = 3pi/2 to x = 2pi.\n- Prompt: What is the range of y = 2 + sin x? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: [1, 3].\n- Prompt: The graph y = sin(2x) completes one full cycle from x = 0 to x = pi. What is its period? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: pi.\n- Prompt: A trig graph has y = -1 at x = 0 and y = 1 at x = pi. Which basic reflected function matches it? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: y = -cos x.","Independent Practice":"- Cosine midline crossing behavior: For y = cos x, what is the y-value at x = pi, and what happens at x = pi/2? Expected answer: At x = pi, y = -1. At x = pi/2, the graph crosses the midline y = 0 while decreasing..\n- Domain and range of tangent: State the domain and range of y = tan x. Expected answer: Domain: all real x except x = pi/2 + kpi. Range: all real numbers..\n- Intersection of sine and cosine: What is the first positive x-value where sin x = cos x? Expected answer: pi/4.\n- Counting cycles: How many full cycles does y = sin x complete from x = 0 to x = 4pi? Expected answer: 2 full cycles.\n- Identifying sine from key points: A graph passes through (0, 0), (pi/2, 1), (pi, 0), (3pi/2, -1), and (2pi, 0). Which basic trig function is it? Expected answer: y = sin x.\n- Identifying negative sine from direction: A basic trig graph crosses the origin going downward and reaches -1 at x = pi/2. Which function matches it? Expected answer: y = -sin x.","Mastery Check":"- What is the midline of the basic graph y = cos x? Mastery answer: y = 0.\n- Describe the key features of y = tan x on (-pi/2, pi/2). Mastery answer: It has vertical asymptotes at x = -pi/2 and x = pi/2, passes through (0, 0), and increases from negative values to positive values..\n- A repeating daylight model starts at its midline, rises to a maximum at x = pi/2, returns to the midline at x = pi, and reaches a minimum at x = 3pi/2. Which basic graph shape fits? Mastery answer: A sine graph, y = sin x, fits the shape..\n- A student says y = tan x has range [-1, 1] because sine and cosine do. Explain the error. Mastery answer: The range of y = tan x is all real numbers..","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P046\n- Phase: 046\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P046","phaseId":"P046","title":"Trigonometric graphs"},"Tut-P047":{"Learning Goal":"Master amplitude and period by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Identify the maximum, minimum, and midline of a graph.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Recall that sine and cosine have base period 2pi and tangent has base period pi.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use absolute value to interpret amplitude as a distance.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Recognize that horizontal scaling changes the period by a reciprocal factor.","Core Concept":"- For y = A sin(Bx) or y = A cos(Bx), the amplitude is |A|.\n- For y = A sin(Bx) or y = A cos(Bx), the period is 2pi/|B|.\n- For y = A tan(Bx), the period is pi/|B|.\n- A negative leading coefficient reflects the graph but does not make amplitude negative.\n- The range of an unshifted sine or cosine graph with amplitude A is [-|A|, |A|].","Worked Example":"- Find the amplitude of y = 3sin x. Answer: 3 Reason: For y = A sin x, amplitude is |A|. Here |3| = 3.\n- Find the amplitude of y = -4cos x. Answer: 4 Reason: Amplitude is a distance, so it is never negative. Since |-4| = 4, the amplitude is 4.\n- Find the period of y = sin(2x). Answer: pi Reason: For sine, period = 2pi/|B|. Here B = 2, so the period is 2pi/2 = pi.\n- Find the period of y = cos(x/2). Answer: 4pi Reason: The coefficient of x is B = 1/2. The period is 2pi/(1/2) = 4pi.","Common Mistakes":"- Reporting a negative amplitude when A is negative.\n- Multiplying by B instead of dividing the base period by |B|.\n- Using 2pi/|B| as the period for tangent.\n- Confusing amplitude with total height from minimum to maximum.\n- Changing the period when only the amplitude changes.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Find the amplitude and period of y = 5sin(3x). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Amplitude 5; period 2pi/3.\n- Prompt: Find the amplitude and period of y = -2cos(4x). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Amplitude 2; period pi/2.\n- Prompt: For y = sin(Bx), what positive value of B gives period pi/3? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: B = 6.\n- Prompt: An unshifted sine graph has range [-7, 7]. What is its amplitude? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 7.\n- Prompt: Find the amplitude and period of y = (1/2)sin(pi x). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Amplitude 1/2; period 2.\n- Prompt: Find the period of y = tan(3x). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: pi/3.","Independent Practice":"- Comparing amplitude and period changes: Compare y = 2sin x and y = sin(2x). Which changes amplitude, and which changes period? Expected answer: y = 2sin x changes amplitude; y = sin(2x) changes period..\n- Building an equation from amplitude and period: Write one sine equation with amplitude 3, period pi, and no shifts. Expected answer: y = 3sin(2x).\n- Range from amplitude: Find the range of y = -6cos(2x). Expected answer: [-6, 6].\n- Cycles on an interval: How many full cycles does y = sin(5x) complete on [0, 2pi]? Expected answer: 5 full cycles.\n- Period with a real-world scale: Find the amplitude and period of y = 4sin((pi/6)x). Expected answer: Amplitude 4; period 12.\n- Finding B from cycle count: A graph y = A sin(Bx) completes 2 full cycles on [0, 2pi], with B \u003e 0. Find B. Expected answer: B = 2.","Mastery Check":"- Write one sine equation with maximum 2, minimum -2, period pi, and no shifts. Mastery answer: y = 2sin(2x).\n- Find the vertical asymptotes of y = tan(2x). Mastery answer: x = pi/4 + kpi/2 for any integer k.\n- A tide height ranges from 2 meters to 8 meters and repeats every 12 hours. What are the amplitude and period? Mastery answer: Amplitude 3 meters; period 12 hours.\n- For y = -3cos((2pi/5)x), find the amplitude, period, and range. Mastery answer: Amplitude 3; period 5; range [-3, 3].","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P047\n- Phase: 047\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P047","phaseId":"P047","title":"Amplitude and period"},"Tut-P048":{"Learning Goal":"Master phase and vertical shifts by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Identify amplitude and period from equations of the form y = A sin(Bx) and y = A cos(Bx).\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use horizontal shift language: x - C means right C and x + C means left C.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Find a midline by averaging maximum and minimum values.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Factor the inside expression before reading a phase shift.","Core Concept":"- In y = A sin(B(x - C)) + D, the phase shift is right C and the vertical shift is D.\n- The midline of y = A sin(B(x - C)) + D or y = A cos(B(x - C)) + D is y = D.\n- The range of a shifted sine or cosine graph is [D - |A|, D + |A|].\n- When the inside is Bx - K, factor B first to get B(x - K/B).\n- A phase shift changes where the cycle begins but does not change amplitude.","Worked Example":"- For y = sin x + 2, identify the vertical shift and midline. Answer: Vertical shift up 2; midline y = 2 Reason: Adding 2 outside the sine function moves every y-value up by 2. The midline moves from y = 0 to y = 2.\n- For y = sin(x - pi/3), identify the phase shift. Answer: Right pi/3 Reason: The expression x - pi/3 means the input must be pi/3 larger to match the basic sine graph, so the graph shifts right pi/3.\n- For y = cos(x + pi/4), identify the phase shift. Answer: Left pi/4 Reason: The expression x + pi/4 is the same as x - (-pi/4). That represents a shift left pi/4.\n- For y = 3sin x - 1, identify the midline. Answer: y = -1 Reason: The -1 outside the sine function shifts the entire graph down 1. The midline is y = -1.","Common Mistakes":"- Reading x - C as a shift left instead of right.\n- Using K instead of K/B as the shift in Bx - K.\n- Treating the vertical shift as the amplitude.\n- Forgetting that reflection changes orientation but not midline.\n- Using maximum or minimum as the midline.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: For y = 2cos(x - pi/2) + 4, find the amplitude, period, phase shift, and midline. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Amplitude 2; period 2pi; shift right pi/2; midline y = 4.\n- Prompt: For y = -sin(x + pi) + 3, find the amplitude, phase shift, and midline. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Amplitude 1; shift left pi; midline y = 3.\n- Prompt: For y = sin(x - pi/6) - 2, where is the first midline crossing with the graph rising? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: At x = pi/6 on the midline y = -2.\n- Prompt: Find the range of y = 4sin(x - pi/2) + 1. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: [-3, 5].\n- Prompt: Write one sine equation with amplitude 3, midline y = 2, and phase shift right pi/4. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: y = 3sin(x - pi/4) + 2.\n- Prompt: A shifted sine graph has maximum 7 and minimum 1. Find its midline and amplitude. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Midline y = 4; amplitude 3.","Independent Practice":"- Phase shift after factoring: For y = cos(2(x + pi/6)), find the phase shift and period. Expected answer: Shift left pi/6; period pi.\n- Factoring the inside expression: For y = 2sin(3x - pi) + 5, find the phase shift, period, and midline. Expected answer: Shift right pi/3; period 2pi/3; midline y = 5.\n- Finding a shifted maximum: For y = cos(x - pi/3) - 2, where does the first maximum occur? Expected answer: At x = pi/3, with value -1.\n- Modeling a sinusoid from max, min, and period: A temperature model has maximum 10, minimum 4, period 24, and starts at the midline rising when t = 0. Write a sine model. Expected answer: y = 3sin((pi/12)t) + 7.\n- Evaluating a shifted sine expression: Evaluate y = 2sin(x - pi/6) + 1 at x = pi/2. Expected answer: 1 + sqrt(3).\n- Midline crossings after phase shift: Find the midline crossings of y = sin(x - pi/4) + 2 on [0, 2pi). Expected answer: x = pi/4 and x = 5pi/4.","Mastery Check":"- If y = cos(x - C) has its first maximum at x = pi/3, what is C? Mastery answer: C = pi/3.\n- Which basic function is equal to y = sin(x + pi/2)? Mastery answer: y = cos x.\n- For y = -4cos(2(x - pi/8)) - 1, find the amplitude, period, phase shift, midline, and range. Mastery answer: Amplitude 4; period pi; shift right pi/8; midline y = -1; range [-5, 3].\n- A student says y = sin(2x - pi) shifts left pi. What is the correct phase shift? Mastery answer: Right pi/2.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P048\n- Phase: 048\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P048","phaseId":"P048","title":"Phase and vertical shifts"},"Tut-P049":{"Learning Goal":"Master basic trigonometric identities by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use sine, cosine, and tangent values from the unit circle.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Simplify fractions and factor expressions carefully.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Recall that tangent is sine divided by cosine.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use quadrant signs when extracting a square root from a squared trig value.","Core Concept":"- Reciprocal identities include csc x = 1/sin x, sec x = 1/cos x, and cot x = 1/tan x.\n- Quotient identities include tan x = sin x/cos x and cot x = cos x/sin x.\n- The main Pythagorean identity is sin^2 x + cos^2 x = 1.\n- Related Pythagorean identities are 1 + tan^2 x = sec^2 x and 1 + cot^2 x = csc^2 x.\n- Identity work must respect where expressions are defined.","Worked Example":"- Rewrite tan x using sine and cosine. Answer: tan x = sin x/cos x Reason: The quotient identity defines tangent as the ratio of sine to cosine when cos x is not zero.\n- If sin x = 3/5 and cos x = 4/5, find tan x. Answer: 3/4 Reason: Use tan x = sin x/cos x. Then tan x = (3/5)/(4/5) = 3/4.\n- If sin x = -2/3, find csc x. Answer: -3/2 Reason: Cosecant is the reciprocal of sine, so csc x = 1/(-2/3) = -3/2.\n- Simplify sin^2 x + cos^2 x. Answer: 1 Reason: The Pythagorean identity states that sin^2 x + cos^2 x = 1 for every angle x.","Common Mistakes":"- Canceling terms across addition, such as reducing (sin x + 1)/sin x to 2.\n- Treating csc x as sin^-1 x instead of 1/sin x.\n- Forgetting domain restrictions when dividing by sine or cosine.\n- Taking a positive square root in every quadrant.\n- Trying to prove an identity by testing only one angle.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Simplify 1 + tan^2 x. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: sec^2 x.\n- Prompt: Simplify sec^2 x - tan^2 x. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 1.\n- Prompt: Simplify (sin x)(csc x), assuming the expression is defined. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 1.\n- Prompt: Simplify cos x tan x, assuming the expression is defined. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: sin x.\n- Prompt: Simplify sin x cot x, assuming the expression is defined. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: cos x.\n- Prompt: If cos x = 5/13 and x is in quadrant IV, find sin x and tan x. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: sin x = -12/13 and tan x = -12/5.","Independent Practice":"- Verifying by Pythagorean substitution: Simplify (1 - cos^2 x)/sin x, assuming sin x is not zero. Expected answer: sin x.\n- Multi-step identity simplification: Simplify (sec x - cos x)/tan x, assuming the expression is defined. Expected answer: sin x.\n- Using identity to collapse a product: Simplify (1 + tan^2 x)cos^2 x. Expected answer: 1.\n- Finding sine and cosine from tangent and quadrant: If tan x = 2 and x is in quadrant III, find sin x and cos x. Expected answer: sin x = -2sqrt(5)/5 and cos x = -sqrt(5)/5.\n- Using squared identity with quadrant: If sin^2 x = 7/16 and x is in quadrant II, find cos x. Expected answer: -3/4.\n- Testing a false identity: Is sin x + cos x = 1 an identity? Give a reason. Expected answer: No. For x = pi/4, sin x + cos x = sqrt(2), not 1..","Mastery Check":"- Simplify sec x cos x + tan x cot x, assuming all expressions are defined. Mastery answer: 2.\n- Simplify (tan x + cot x)sin x cos x, assuming all expressions are defined. Mastery answer: 1.\n- Simplify (sec^2 x - 1)/(1 - cos^2 x), assuming the expression is defined. Mastery answer: sec^2 x.\n- A student simplifies (sin x + 1)/sin x to 2. What is the correct simplification? Mastery answer: 1 + csc x.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P049\n- Phase: 049\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P049","phaseId":"P049","title":"Basic trigonometric identities"},"Tut-P050":{"Learning Goal":"Master trigonometric equations by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use unit-circle angles for common sine, cosine, and tangent values.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Factor simple algebraic expressions before solving.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Check every solution against the required interval.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Avoid dividing by a trig expression when it may equal zero.","Core Concept":"- Trigonometric equations often have more than one solution on a finite interval.\n- Solve by isolating the trig function, using identities, factoring, and then reading angles from the unit circle.\n- Squaring or dividing can create lost or extra solutions, so checking matters.\n- For equations involving 2x or x - C, solve the inner angle first and then translate back to x.\n- The interval determines which coterminal solutions are included.","Worked Example":"- Solve sin x = 1/2 on [0, 2pi). Answer: x = pi/6, 5pi/6 Reason: Sine is positive in quadrants I and II. The reference angle for 1/2 is pi/6, so the solutions are pi/6 and 5pi/6.\n- Solve cos x = -1/2 on [0, 2pi). Answer: x = 2pi/3, 4pi/3 Reason: Cosine is negative in quadrants II and III. The reference angle is pi/3, giving 2pi/3 and 4pi/3.\n- Solve tan x = 1 on [0, 2pi). Answer: x = pi/4, 5pi/4 Reason: Tangent is positive in quadrants I and III. The reference angle is pi/4, so the solutions are pi/4 and 5pi/4.\n- Solve 2sin x - 1 = 0 on [0, 2pi). Answer: x = pi/6, 5pi/6 Reason: First isolate sine: 2sin x = 1, so sin x = 1/2. On [0, 2pi), that gives x = pi/6 and 5pi/6.","Common Mistakes":"- Writing only the reference angle as the answer.\n- Forgetting a second quadrant or third quadrant solution with the same trig value.\n- Including 2pi when the interval is [0, 2pi).\n- Dividing by sin x or cos x and losing zero solutions.\n- Using degree answers when the problem is in radians.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Solve 2cos x + sqrt(2) = 0 on [0, 2pi). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = 3pi/4, 5pi/4.\n- Prompt: Solve tan(2x) = 0 on [0, 2pi). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = 0, pi/2, pi, 3pi/2.\n- Prompt: Solve sin^2 x = 1 on [0, 2pi). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = pi/2, 3pi/2.\n- Prompt: Solve cos^2 x = 1/4 on [0, 2pi). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = pi/3, 2pi/3, 4pi/3, 5pi/3.\n- Prompt: Solve 2sin^2 x - sin x = 0 on [0, 2pi). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = 0, pi/6, 5pi/6, pi.\n- Prompt: Solve tan^2 x = 3 on [0, 2pi). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = pi/3, 2pi/3, 4pi/3, 5pi/3.","Independent Practice":"- Product equal to zero: Solve 2cos x sin x = 0 on [0, 2pi). Expected answer: x = 0, pi/2, pi, 3pi/2.\n- Sine equals cosine: Solve sin x = cos x on [0, 2pi). Expected answer: x = pi/4, 5pi/4.\n- Factoring without losing solutions: Solve 2sin x cos x = sin x on [0, 2pi). Expected answer: x = 0, pi, pi/3, 5pi/3.\n- Shifted sine equation: Solve sin(x - pi/4) = 0 on [0, 2pi). Expected answer: x = pi/4, 5pi/4.\n- Tangent equation with coefficient: Solve 3tan x - sqrt(3) = 0 on [0, 2pi). Expected answer: x = pi/6, 7pi/6.\n- Secant equation: Solve sec x = 2 on [0, 2pi). Expected answer: x = pi/3, 5pi/3.","Mastery Check":"- Solve 4cos^2 x - 1 = 0 on [0, pi]. Mastery answer: x = pi/3, 2pi/3.\n- A height model is h = 3 + 2sin t. On [0, 2pi), when is h = 4? Mastery answer: t = pi/6, 5pi/6.\n- Solve 2sin^2 x + sin x - 1 = 0 on [0, 2pi). Mastery answer: x = pi/6, 5pi/6, 3pi/2.\n- A student solves sin x = 1/2 on [0, 2pi) and writes only x = pi/6. What solution is missing? Mastery answer: x = 5pi/6 is missing..","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P050\n- Phase: 050\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P050","phaseId":"P050","title":"Trigonometric equations"},"Tut-P051":{"Learning Goal":"Master law of sines by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use the triangle angle sum A + B + C = 180 degrees.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Match each side with its opposite angle: side a is opposite angle A.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Solve proportions accurately before rounding.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Remember that sine can have two angle solutions between 0 degrees and 180 degrees.","Core Concept":"- The Law of Sines states a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C for any triangle.\n- Use the Law of Sines when you know an angle-opposite side pair and another side or angle.\n- In SSA cases, inverse sine may create zero, one, or two possible triangles.\n- A triangle is impossible if the computed sine of an angle is greater than 1.\n- After finding an angle, always check that the angle sum stays below 180 degrees.","Worked Example":"- In triangle ABC, A = 30 degrees, B = 80 degrees, and a = 10. Find b to the nearest tenth. Answer: b = 19.7 Reason: Use b/sin 80 = 10/sin 30. Then b = 10sin80/sin30 = 20sin80, which is about 19.7.\n- In triangle ABC, A = 45 degrees, C = 60 degrees, and a = 12. Find c exactly. Answer: c = 6sqrt(6) Reason: Use c/sin60 = 12/sin45. Thus c = 12(sin60)/(sin45) = 12(sqrt(3)/2)/(sqrt(2)/2) = 6sqrt(6).\n- In triangle ABC, A = 40 degrees and B = 65 degrees. Find C. Answer: C = 75 degrees Reason: The angles of a triangle sum to 180 degrees, so C = 180 - 40 - 65 = 75 degrees.\n- In triangle ABC, A = 50 degrees, a = 8, and b = 10. Find the possible values of B to the nearest tenth. Answer: B = 73.3 degrees or 106.7 degrees Reason: Use sin B/10 = sin50/8, so sin B = 10sin50/8, about 0.9576. The sine values give B about 73.3 degrees or 106.7 degrees, and both keep the triangle angle sum below 180 degrees.","Common Mistakes":"- Pairing a side with an adjacent angle instead of its opposite angle.\n- Ignoring the second possible angle in an SSA case.\n- Accepting an angle set whose sum exceeds 180 degrees.\n- Rounding intermediate values so much that the final side is distorted.\n- Using Law of Sines when no angle-opposite side pair is known.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: In triangle ABC, A = 30 degrees, a = 5, and b = 12. How many triangles are possible? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: No triangle is possible..\n- Prompt: In triangle ABC, B = 70 degrees, b = 14, and A = 35 degrees. Find a to the nearest hundredth. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: a = 8.55.\n- Prompt: In triangle ABC, A = 32 degrees, B = 78 degrees, and a = 9. Find C, b, and c to the nearest hundredth. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: C = 70 degrees, b = 16.61, c = 15.96.\n- Prompt: If a/sin A = 18 and C = 40 degrees, find c to the nearest hundredth. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: c = 11.57.\n- Prompt: In triangle ABC, A = 42 degrees, C = 68 degrees, and c = 15. Find a to the nearest tenth. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: a = 10.8.\n- Prompt: In triangle ABC, A = 110 degrees, a = 20, and b = 12. Find B to the nearest tenth. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: B = 34.3 degrees.","Independent Practice":"- Ambiguous SSA with side lengths: In triangle ABC, A = 30 degrees, a = 10, and b = 15. Find the possible values of B to the nearest tenth. Expected answer: B = 48.6 degrees or 131.4 degrees.\n- Detecting impossible sine value: In triangle ABC, A = 85 degrees, a = 6, and b = 8. How many triangles are possible? Expected answer: No triangle is possible..\n- Finding two sides from ASA: In triangle ABC, A = 25 degrees, B = 100 degrees, and c = 18. Find a and b to the nearest hundredth. Expected answer: a = 9.29 and b = 21.65.\n- Another ambiguous angle case: In triangle ABC, A = 45 degrees, a = 7, and b = 9. Find the possible values of B to the nearest tenth. Expected answer: B = 65.4 degrees or 114.6 degrees.\n- Navigation distance with two angles: A triangle-shaped route has angles 52 degrees and 68 degrees at two landmarks. The side opposite the remaining angle is 30 km. Find the side opposite 52 degrees to the nearest tenth. Expected answer: 24.1 km.\n- Exact Law of Sines side: In triangle ABC, A = 30 degrees, B = 45 degrees, and a = 6. Find b exactly. Expected answer: b = 6sqrt(2).","Mastery Check":"- In triangle ABC, A = 36 degrees, B = 64 degrees, and b = 20. Find c to the nearest tenth. Mastery answer: c = 21.9.\n- Solve for x: x/sin40 degrees = 12/sin75 degrees. Round to the nearest hundredth. Mastery answer: x = 7.99.\n- In triangle ABC, A = 40 degrees, a = 10, and b = 12. Find the possible values of B and c to the nearest tenth. Mastery answer: B = 50.5 degrees with c = 15.6, or B = 129.5 degrees with c = 2.8.\n- A student uses 15/sin42 = a/sin68 for a triangle where A = 42 degrees, C = 68 degrees, and c = 15. Explain the error. Mastery answer: The side 15 is c, so it must pair with sin68, not sin42..","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P051\n- Phase: 051\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P051","phaseId":"P051","title":"Law of sines"},"Tut-P052":{"Learning Goal":"Master law of cosines by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Identify the side opposite a named angle in a triangle.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use the Pythagorean theorem as a special case when the included angle is 90 degrees.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Apply inverse cosine to find an angle from a cosine value.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Round only after the main computation is complete.","Core Concept":"- The Law of Cosines says c^2 = a^2 + b^2 - 2ab cos C, where C is the included angle between sides a and b.\n- Use Law of Cosines for SAS information to find the third side.\n- Use Law of Cosines for SSS information to find an angle.\n- The Pythagorean theorem appears when cos C = 0.\n- The sign of cos C helps identify whether an angle is acute, right, or obtuse.","Worked Example":"- Two sides of a triangle are 5 and 7 with included angle 60 degrees. Find the opposite side c exactly. Answer: c = sqrt(39) Reason: Use c^2 = 5^2 + 7^2 - 2(5)(7)cos60. Since cos60 = 1/2, c^2 = 25 + 49 - 35 = 39, so c = sqrt(39).\n- Two sides of a triangle are 8 and 10 with included angle 120 degrees. Find the opposite side c exactly. Answer: c = 2sqrt(61) Reason: Use c^2 = 8^2 + 10^2 - 2(8)(10)cos120. Since cos120 = -1/2, c^2 = 64 + 100 + 80 = 244, so c = 2sqrt(61).\n- A triangle has sides 3, 4, and 5. Find the angle opposite the side of length 5. Answer: 90 degrees Reason: Use cos C = (3^2 + 4^2 - 5^2)/(2(3)(4)) = (9 + 16 - 25)/24 = 0. Therefore C = 90 degrees.\n- A triangle has sides 6, 8, and 10. Find the angle opposite the side of length 10. Answer: 90 degrees Reason: cos C = (6^2 + 8^2 - 10^2)/(2(6)(8)) = (36 + 64 - 100)/96 = 0. Thus C = 90 degrees.","Common Mistakes":"- Using the non-included angle in the SAS formula.\n- Replacing cosine with sine in the formula.\n- Forgetting the minus sign before 2ab cos C.\n- Finding an angle but pairing it with the wrong opposite side.\n- Assuming every triangle with three sides is right.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: A triangle has sides 7, 9, and 11. Find the angle opposite the side of length 11 to the nearest tenth. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 85.9 degrees.\n- Prompt: Two sides of a triangle are 12 and 15 with included angle 40 degrees. Find the third side to the nearest hundredth. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 9.66.\n- Prompt: A triangle has sides 4, 6, and 8. Find the largest angle to the nearest tenth. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 104.5 degrees.\n- Prompt: Use side lengths 5, 6, and 7 to decide whether the triangle is acute, right, or obtuse. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Acute.\n- Prompt: Two paths of lengths 10 miles and 14 miles meet at an angle of 35 degrees. Find the distance between their endpoints to the nearest hundredth. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 8.16 miles.\n- Prompt: Two sides around an angle are 9 and 12, and the opposite side is 15. Find the included angle. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 90 degrees.","Independent Practice":"- Equilateral result from SAS: Two sides of a triangle are 4 and 4 with included angle 60 degrees. Find the opposite side. Expected answer: 4.\n- Exact angle from side lengths: A triangle has sides 1, 2, and sqrt(3). Find the angle opposite the side sqrt(3). Expected answer: 60 degrees.\n- Side length with included 120 degrees: Two sides of a triangle are 5 and 5 with included angle 120 degrees. Find the opposite side exactly. Expected answer: 5sqrt(3).\n- Choosing Law of Cosines: A triangle has sides 7, 8, and 9. Which law should you use first to find an angle, and why? Expected answer: Use the Law of Cosines first because all three sides are known..\n- Angle in a 13-14-15 triangle: A triangle has sides 13, 14, and 15. Find the angle opposite the side of length 15 to the nearest tenth. Expected answer: 67.4 degrees.\n- Obtuse triangle classification: Classify the triangle with side lengths 3, 4, and 6 as acute, right, or obtuse. Expected answer: Obtuse.","Mastery Check":"- A triangle has sides 5 and 7 with included angle 60 degrees. The side opposite the angle is x. Find x exactly. Mastery answer: x = sqrt(39).\n- Two ships leave the same port. One travels 18 km and the other travels 22 km, with an angle of 70 degrees between their paths. How far apart are they to the nearest hundredth? Mastery answer: 23.18 km.\n- In triangle ABC, a = 10, b = 8, and c = 6. Find angle A. Mastery answer: A = 90 degrees.\n- A student writes c^2 = a^2 + b^2 - 2ab sin C for Law of Cosines. What is wrong? Mastery answer: The formula uses cos C, not sin C..","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P052\n- Phase: 052\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P052","phaseId":"P052","title":"Law of cosines"},"Tut-P053":{"Learning Goal":"Master sequences by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Evaluate formulas by substituting a value for n.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Recognize constant differences as linear patterns.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Recognize constant ratios as exponential patterns.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Solve simple equations involving n.","Core Concept":"- A sequence is an ordered list of terms, often written a_1, a_2, a_3, and so on.\n- An arithmetic sequence has a constant difference d and explicit form a_n = a_1 + d(n - 1).\n- A geometric sequence has a constant ratio r and explicit form a_n = a_1 r^(n - 1).\n- A recursive rule defines a term using earlier term values.\n- The index n tells the position of a term, not always the term value itself.","Worked Example":"- Find the next two terms of 5, 8, 11, ... Answer: 14, 17 Reason: The common difference is 3. Add 3 to 11 to get 14, then add 3 again to get 17.\n- Find the common difference of 20, 15, 10, 5, ... Answer: -5 Reason: Each term decreases by 5. For example, 15 - 20 = -5 and 10 - 15 = -5.\n- Write an explicit rule for the arithmetic sequence with a_1 = 7 and d = 4. Answer: a_n = 7 + 4(n - 1), or a_n = 4n + 3 Reason: Use a_n = a_1 + d(n - 1). Substituting a_1 = 7 and d = 4 gives a_n = 7 + 4(n - 1), which simplifies to 4n + 3.\n- Find a_12 for the arithmetic sequence with a_1 = 3 and d = 5. Answer: 58 Reason: Use a_n = a_1 + d(n - 1). Then a_12 = 3 + 5(12 - 1) = 3 + 55 = 58.","Common Mistakes":"- Using n instead of n - 1 in explicit sequence formulas.\n- Confusing common difference with common ratio.\n- Calling any increasing sequence arithmetic.\n- Starting a recursive rule with the wrong first term.\n- Assuming geometric ratios must be positive.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: For the arithmetic sequence a_1 = 2 and d = 3, what value of n gives a_n = 29? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: n = 10.\n- Prompt: Find the next two terms of 2, 6, 18, ... Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 54, 162.\n- Prompt: Find the common ratio of 81, 27, 9, 3, ... Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 1/3.\n- Prompt: Write an explicit rule for the geometric sequence with a_1 = 5 and r = 2. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: a_n = 5(2)^(n - 1).\n- Prompt: Find a_6 for the geometric sequence with a_1 = 3 and r = -2. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: -96.\n- Prompt: Is 4, 9, 14, 19, ... arithmetic, geometric, or neither? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Arithmetic.","Independent Practice":"- Recursive arithmetic rule: Give a recursive rule for the sequence defined by a_n = 2n + 1 for n \u003e= 1. Expected answer: a_1 = 3, and a_n = a_(n - 1) + 2 for n \u003e= 2.\n- Recursive geometric rule: Give a recursive rule for 10, 5, 2.5, 1.25, ... Expected answer: a_1 = 10, and a_n = 0.5a_(n - 1) for n \u003e= 2.\n- Missing arithmetic term: Find the missing term in the arithmetic sequence 6, __, 18. Expected answer: 12.\n- Missing geometric term: Find the positive missing term in the geometric sequence 4, __, 36. Expected answer: 12.\n- Evaluating a nonlinear explicit rule: For the sequence a_n = n^2 + 1, find a_5. Expected answer: 26.\n- Recursive sequence evaluation: A sequence has a_1 = 1, a_2 = 3, and a_n = a_(n - 1) + a_(n - 2). Find a_5. Expected answer: 11.","Mastery Check":"- At n = 5, which is larger: a_n = 3n or b_n = 2^n? Mastery answer: b_n = 2^n is larger..\n- You start with $50 and add $15 each week. Write a formula for the balance after n weekly deposits. Mastery answer: a_n = 50 + 15n.\n- For a geometric sequence with a_1 = 4 and r = 3, which term equals 324? Mastery answer: a_5.\n- A student writes a_n = 5n + 7 for the sequence 7, 12, 17, 22, ... What is the correct explicit rule? Mastery answer: a_n = 5n + 2.","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P053\n- Phase: 053\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P053","phaseId":"P053","title":"Sequences"},"Tut-P054":{"Learning Goal":"Master series by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Find terms of arithmetic and geometric sequences.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Distinguish a term a_n from a sum S_n.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Evaluate powers and fractions accurately.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Interpret sigma notation by expanding the indicated terms.","Core Concept":"- A series is the sum of the terms of a sequence.\n- A finite arithmetic series can be summed with S_n = n(a_1 + a_n)/2.\n- A finite geometric series can be summed with S_n = a_1(1 - r^n)/(1 - r), when r is not 1.\n- An infinite geometric series has sum a_1/(1 - r) only when |r| \u003c 1.\n- Sigma notation names an index, lower bound, upper bound, and expression to sum.","Worked Example":"- Find the sum 2 + 4 + 6 + 8 + 10. Answer: 30 Reason: Add the five terms: 2 + 4 + 6 + 8 + 10 = 30. This is also an arithmetic series with average term 6 and 5 terms, so the sum is 5(6) = 30.\n- What formula gives the sum of the first n terms of an arithmetic series when a_1 and a_n are known? Answer: S_n = n(a_1 + a_n)/2 Reason: The sum equals the number of terms times the average of the first and last terms. The average is (a_1 + a_n)/2.\n- Find the sum of the first 10 terms of the arithmetic sequence with a_1 = 3 and d = 2. Answer: 120 Reason: First find a_10 = 3 + 2(9) = 21. Then S_10 = 10(3 + 21)/2 = 120.\n- Find the sum 3 + 6 + 12 + 24. Answer: 45 Reason: Add the terms directly: 3 + 6 + 12 + 24 = 45. The common ratio is 2, but direct addition is quick for four terms.","Common Mistakes":"- Finding the nth term when the question asks for the sum.\n- Using the infinite geometric formula when |r| is greater than or equal to 1.\n- Starting a sigma sum at the wrong index.\n- Forgetting that alternating geometric series can have negative ratios.\n- Mixing arithmetic and geometric sum formulas.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: What formula gives the sum of the first n terms of a geometric series with first term a_1 and ratio r, where r is not 1? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: S_n = a_1(1 - r^n)/(1 - r).\n- Prompt: Find the sum of the first 6 terms of the geometric series with a_1 = 2 and r = 3. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 728.\n- Prompt: Find the sum of the infinite geometric series with a_1 = 5 and r = 1/2. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 10.\n- Prompt: Find the sum 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 + ... Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 16.\n- Prompt: Does 3 + 6 + 12 + 24 + ... have a finite infinite sum? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: No..\n- Prompt: Evaluate sum from k = 1 to 4 of k^2. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 30.","Independent Practice":"- Sigma notation with powers: Evaluate sum from k = 0 to 3 of 2^k. Expected answer: 15.\n- Arithmetic series with unknown term count: Find the sum 7 + 10 + 13 + ... + 31. Expected answer: 171.\n- Alternating finite geometric sum: Find the sum 5 + (-10) + 20 + (-40). Expected answer: -25.\n- Alternating infinite geometric sum: Find the sum 12 - 6 + 3 - 1.5 + ... Expected answer: 8.\n- Finding number of terms from a sum: The sum of the first n positive odd numbers is 64. Find n. Expected answer: n = 8.\n- Telescoping series: Evaluate the sum from k = 1 to 4 of (1/k - 1/(k + 1)). Expected answer: 4/5.","Mastery Check":"- A theater has 15 rows. The first row has 12 seats, and each row after that has 2 more seats than the previous row. How many seats are there total? Mastery answer: 390 seats.\n- Find the total of four payments: $100, $110, $121, and $133.10. Mastery answer: $464.10.\n- Create an arithmetic series with 5 terms, first term 4, and sum 40. What common difference works? Mastery answer: d = 2.\n- A student tries to find the infinite sum of 5 + 6 + 7.2 + ... using a_1/(1 - r). Explain the problem. Mastery answer: The ratio is 1.2, so the infinite geometric sum formula cannot be used..","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P054\n- Phase: 054\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P054","phaseId":"P054","title":"Series"},"Tut-P055":{"Learning Goal":"Master conic sections by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use the distance formula to measure from a point to a center.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Complete the square by adding the same square-making constant inside the equation.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Recognize horizontal and vertical translations from expressions like x - h and y - k.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Connect squared terms to graph shapes on the coordinate plane.","Core Concept":"- A circle has equal squared denominators and stores all points a fixed distance from its center.\n- A parabola has one squared variable and is controlled by the directed distance p from vertex to focus.\n- An ellipse has two added squared terms with positive denominators and a longer major axis.\n- A hyperbola has two squared terms that subtract and has asymptotes through its center.\n- Completing the square reveals the translated center or vertex before graph features are read.","Worked Example":"- Classify the conic x^2 + y^2 = 25 and name its center and radius. Answer: Circle with center (0, 0) and radius 5. Reason: Both squared terms are added with the same coefficient, so the graph is a circle. Since x^2 + y^2 = 5^2, the center is (0, 0) and the radius is 5.\n- Find the center and radius of (x - 3)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 49. Answer: Center (3, -2), radius 7. Reason: A circle in standard form is (x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2. Here h = 3, k = -2, and r^2 = 49, so r = 7.\n- Write the equation of the circle with center (-1, 4) and radius 6. Answer: (x + 1)^2 + (y - 4)^2 = 36. Reason: Use (x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 = r^2. With h = -1, k = 4, and r = 6, the equation is (x + 1)^2 + (y - 4)^2 = 36.\n- Rewrite x^2 + y^2 - 6x + 4y - 12 = 0 in standard form and identify the circle. Answer: (x - 3)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 25; center (3, -2), radius 5. Reason: Group terms: x^2 - 6x and y^2 + 4y. Add 9 and 4 to complete squares, giving (x - 3)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 25. The center is (3, -2), and the radius is 5.","Common Mistakes":"- Treating every equation with squared variables as a circle.\n- Forgetting to add square-completion constants to the opposite side of the equation.\n- Reading the center signs directly instead of reversing the signs in x - h and y - k.\n- Using a^2 - b^2 for hyperbola foci instead of a^2 + b^2.\n- Calling the larger denominator a horizontal axis without checking which variable owns it.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: For y = (1/8)(x - 2)^2 - 3, find the vertex, focus, and directrix. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Vertex (2, -3), focus (2, -1), directrix y = -5..\n- Prompt: Write the equation of the parabola with vertex (1, -2) and focus (1, 1). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (x - 1)^2 = 12(y + 2)..\n- Prompt: Find the center, vertices, and covertices of (x - 1)^2/25 + (y + 2)^2/9 = 1. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Center (1, -2); vertices (-4, -2) and (6, -2); covertices (1, -5) and (1, 1)..\n- Prompt: Find the center, vertices, and asymptotes of (y + 1)^2/16 - (x - 2)^2/9 = 1. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Center (2, -1); vertices (2, 3) and (2, -5); asymptotes y + 1 = (4/3)(x - 2) and y + 1 = -(4/3)(x - 2)..\n- Prompt: Classify and rewrite 9x^2 + 4y^2 - 18x + 16y - 11 = 0 in standard form. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (x - 1)^2/4 + (y + 2)^2/9 = 1; ellipse centered at (1, -2)..\n- Prompt: Find the eccentricity of x^2/100 + y^2/64 = 1. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 3/5..","Independent Practice":"- Finding hyperbola foci: Find the foci of x^2/9 - y^2/16 = 1. Expected answer: (-5, 0) and (5, 0)..\n- Reading a horizontal parabola: For (y + 4)^2 = -12(x - 1), find the vertex, focus, and directrix. Expected answer: Vertex (1, -4), focus (-2, -4), directrix x = 4..\n- Checking a point on a circle: Does the point (6, 1) lie on the circle (x - 2)^2 + (y + 2)^2 = 25? Expected answer: Yes, because it satisfies the equation..\n- Writing an ellipse from vertices: Write the equation of the ellipse centered at (0, 0) with vertices (0, -7) and (0, 7), and covertices (-2, 0) and (2, 0). Expected answer: x^2/4 + y^2/49 = 1..\n- Writing a hyperbola from vertices and asymptotes: Write the equation of the hyperbola with center (-1, 3), vertices (-1, 1) and (-1, 5), and asymptotes y - 3 = 2(x + 1) and y - 3 = -2(x + 1). Expected answer: (y - 3)^2/4 - (x + 1)^2 = 1..\n- Completing the square for a hyperbola: Rewrite 4x^2 - 9y^2 - 16x - 18y - 29 = 0 in standard form and give the asymptotes. Expected answer: (x - 2)^2/9 - (y + 1)^2/4 = 1; asymptotes y + 1 = (2/3)(x - 2) and y + 1 = -(2/3)(x - 2)..","Mastery Check":"- The point (2, 3) lies on x^2/16 + y^2/k = 1. Find k. Mastery answer: k = 12..\n- Find the intersection points of x^2 + y^2 = 25 and y = 3. Mastery answer: (-4, 3) and (4, 3)..\n- A parabolic arch has vertex (0, 12) and passes through (-6, 0) and (6, 0). If its equation is y = ax^2 + 12, what is the height when x = 3? Mastery answer: 9 units..\n- Rewrite 16x^2 + 25y^2 - 64x + 150y - 111 = 0 in standard form, then give the center, vertices, covertices, and foci. Mastery answer: (x - 2)^2/25 + (y + 3)^2/16 = 1; center (2, -3); vertices (-3, -3) and (7, -3); covertices (2, -7) and (2, 1); foci (-1, -3) and (5, -3)..","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P055\n- Phase: 055\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P055","phaseId":"P055","title":"Conic sections"},"Tut-P056":{"Learning Goal":"Master vectors by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Subtract coordinates in the order terminal minus initial to form a displacement vector.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use the Pythagorean theorem to find lengths from horizontal and vertical components.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use sine and cosine to convert between magnitude-direction form and components.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Remember that scalar multiplication changes length and may reverse direction.","Core Concept":"- A vector represents magnitude and direction, not a fixed location.\n- Component form lets vectors be added, subtracted, and scaled coordinate by coordinate.\n- Magnitude measures vector length and comes from the distance formula.\n- The dot product measures alignment and can detect perpendicular vectors.\n- Projection gives the part of one vector that points in the direction of another.","Worked Example":"- Find the vector from A(2, -3) to B(7, 5). Answer: \u003c5, 8\u003e. Reason: Subtract initial coordinates from terminal coordinates: \u003c7 - 2, 5 - (-3)\u003e = \u003c5, 8\u003e.\n- Find the magnitude of \u003c6, -8\u003e. Answer: 10. Reason: The magnitude is sqrt(6^2 + (-8)^2) = sqrt(36 + 64) = sqrt(100) = 10.\n- Find a unit vector in the direction of \u003c3, 4\u003e. Answer: \u003c3/5, 4/5\u003e. Reason: The magnitude of \u003c3, 4\u003e is 5. Divide each component by 5 to get the unit vector \u003c3/5, 4/5\u003e.\n- Compute \u003c2, -1\u003e + \u003c-5, 4\u003e. Answer: \u003c-3, 3\u003e. Reason: Add corresponding components: \u003c2 + (-5), -1 + 4\u003e = \u003c-3, 3\u003e.","Common Mistakes":"- Subtracting coordinates in the wrong order when building a vector.\n- Adding magnitudes instead of adding components.\n- Treating a dot product as another vector instead of a scalar.\n- Forgetting to divide by magnitude when creating a unit vector.\n- Using the wrong angle reference when converting direction to components.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Compute -3\u003c4, -2\u003e. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: \u003c-12, 6\u003e..\n- Prompt: A player moves by \u003c3, 2\u003e, then \u003c-1, 5\u003e, then \u003c4, -3\u003e. What is the total displacement and its magnitude? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Total displacement \u003c6, 4\u003e; magnitude 2sqrt(13)..\n- Prompt: Compute \u003c2, 5\u003e dot \u003c-3, 4\u003e. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 14..\n- Prompt: Are \u003c4, -2\u003e and \u003c1, 2\u003e perpendicular? Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Yes, their dot product is 0..\n- Prompt: Find the angle between \u003c1, 0\u003e and \u003c1, sqrt(3)\u003e. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 60 degrees..\n- Prompt: Find the projection of \u003c6, 2\u003e onto \u003c3, 0\u003e. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: \u003c6, 0\u003e..","Independent Practice":"- Computing work with vectors: A force \u003c10, 4\u003e newtons moves an object by displacement \u003c3, 2\u003e meters. Find the work done. Expected answer: 38 joules..\n- Converting direction to components: Write a vector of magnitude 8 at an angle of 30 degrees from the positive x-axis in component form. Expected answer: \u003c4sqrt(3), 4\u003e..\n- Finding direction angle: Find the magnitude and direction angle of \u003c-3, 3\u003e measured from the positive x-axis. Expected answer: Magnitude 3sqrt(2), direction 135 degrees..\n- Solving a vector equation: Find x and y if \u003cx, 4\u003e + \u003c3, y\u003e = \u003c8, -2\u003e. Expected answer: x = 5 and y = -6..\n- Combining scalar multiples: Compute 2\u003c1, 3\u003e - \u003c4, -2\u003e. Expected answer: \u003c-2, 8\u003e..\n- Finding 3D magnitude: Find the magnitude of \u003c2, -3, 6\u003e. Expected answer: 7..","Mastery Check":"- Compute \u003c1, 2, -2\u003e dot \u003c3, 0, 4\u003e. Mastery answer: -5..\n- Find k so that \u003c2, k\u003e is perpendicular to \u003c-3, 4\u003e. Mastery answer: k = 3/2..\n- A boat\u0027s still-water velocity is \u003c8, 0\u003e miles per hour and the current is \u003c0, 3\u003e miles per hour. Find the resultant velocity and speed. Mastery answer: Resultant velocity \u003c8, 3\u003e; speed sqrt(73) miles per hour..\n- For A(1, 2), B(5, 5), and C(2, 9), find the area of triangle ABC and the angle at A. Mastery answer: Area 25/2 square units; angle at A is 45 degrees..","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P056\n- Phase: 056\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P056","phaseId":"P056","title":"Vectors"},"Tut-P057":{"Learning Goal":"Master complex numbers by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use i^2 = -1 whenever powers or products of i appear.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Combine real parts with real parts and imaginary parts with imaginary parts.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use the quadratic formula when real factoring no longer works.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use the Pythagorean theorem to find modulus in the complex plane.","Core Concept":"- A complex number a + bi has real part a and imaginary part b.\n- The conjugate a - bi helps divide complex numbers and creates real products.\n- Complex roots naturally appear when a quadratic has a negative discriminant.\n- The modulus of a + bi is its distance from the origin in the complex plane.\n- Polar form uses radius and angle, so multiplication scales radii and adds angles.","Worked Example":"- Simplify i^37. Answer: i. Reason: Powers of i cycle every 4: i, -1, -i, 1. Since 37 leaves remainder 1 when divided by 4, i^37 = i.\n- Compute (3 + 2i) + (5 - 7i). Answer: 8 - 5i. Reason: Add real parts and imaginary parts separately: 3 + 5 = 8 and 2i - 7i = -5i.\n- Compute (4 - 3i) - (1 + 6i). Answer: 3 - 9i. Reason: Distribute the subtraction: 4 - 3i - 1 - 6i = 3 - 9i.\n- Compute (2 + 3i)(4 - i). Answer: 11 + 10i. Reason: Multiply: 8 - 2i + 12i - 3i^2. Since i^2 = -1, -3i^2 = 3, so the result is 11 + 10i.","Common Mistakes":"- Treating i^2 as positive 1 instead of negative 1.\n- Combining real and imaginary terms as if they were like terms.\n- Dividing complex numbers without multiplying by the conjugate.\n- Forgetting that polar angles need the correct quadrant.\n- Keeping both plus and minus roots when a context requires a positive real input.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Compute (5 + 2i)(5 - 2i). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 29..\n- Prompt: Write (7 + 5i)/(2 - i) in a + bi form. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 9/5 + (17/5)i..\n- Prompt: Solve x^2 + 16 = 0. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = 4i or x = -4i..\n- Prompt: Solve x^2 - 6x + 13 = 0. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = 3 + 2i or x = 3 - 2i..\n- Prompt: Find the modulus of -3 + 4i. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 5..\n- Prompt: For z = 1 - 6i, find the conjugate of z and z times its conjugate. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Conjugate 1 + 6i; product 37..","Independent Practice":"- Interpreting the complex plane: In which quadrant is the complex number 2 - 5i located? Expected answer: Quadrant IV..\n- Writing rectangular form in polar form: Write 1 + sqrt(3)i in polar form using degrees. Expected answer: 2(cos 60 deg + i sin 60 deg)..\n- Converting polar form to rectangular form: Convert 4(cos 210 deg + i sin 210 deg) to a + bi form. Expected answer: -2sqrt(3) - 2i..\n- Multiplying in polar form: Multiply 3(cos 40 deg + i sin 40 deg) by 2(cos 80 deg + i sin 80 deg). Expected answer: 6(cos 120 deg + i sin 120 deg)..\n- Dividing in polar form: Divide 10(cos 150 deg + i sin 150 deg) by 2(cos 30 deg + i sin 30 deg). Expected answer: 5(cos 120 deg + i sin 120 deg)..\n- Using De Moivre\u0027s theorem: Compute (sqrt(3) + i)^4 in a + bi form. Expected answer: -8 + 8sqrt(3)i..","Mastery Check":"- Solve z^2 = -9. Mastery answer: z = 3i or z = -3i..\n- Find a and b if (a + 2i) + (3 + bi) = 7 - 5i. Mastery answer: a = 4 and b = -7..\n- Write 1/(3 - 4i) in a + bi form. Mastery answer: 3/25 + (4/25)i..\n- Solve z^2 - 5z + 7 + i = 0. Mastery answer: z = 2 + i or z = 3 - i..","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P057\n- Phase: 057\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P057","phaseId":"P057","title":"Complex numbers"},"Tut-P058":{"Learning Goal":"Master mixed review by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Name the topic family before choosing a method.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Check restrictions before and after solving equations.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Use exact values when the question is built from exact trig or radical data.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Translate between symbolic, graphical, numerical, and verbal representations.","Core Concept":"- Mixed review rewards method selection before computation.\n- A correct answer includes restrictions, units, or intervals when the context needs them.\n- Earlier algebra skills support later function, trigonometric, and modeling tasks.\n- Graph features often come from rewriting an equation into a more revealing form.\n- Boss readiness means explaining why a method applies, not only getting a number.","Worked Example":"- Solve 3(2x - 1) = 15. Answer: x = 3. Reason: Distribute to get 6x - 3 = 15. Add 3 to both sides: 6x = 18. Divide by 6 to get x = 3.\n- Solve |x - 4| \u003c 7. Answer: -3 \u003c x \u003c 11. Reason: The inequality |x - 4| \u003c 7 means -7 \u003c x - 4 \u003c 7. Add 4 to all three parts to get -3 \u003c x \u003c 11.\n- Solve the system y = 2x + 1 and x + y = 10. Answer: (3, 7). Reason: Substitute y = 2x + 1 into x + y = 10 to get x + 2x + 1 = 10. Then 3x = 9, so x = 3 and y = 7.\n- If f(x) = x^2 + 1 and g(x) = 3x - 2, find f(g(2)). Answer: 17. Reason: First find g(2) = 3(2) - 2 = 4. Then f(4) = 4^2 + 1 = 17.","Common Mistakes":"- Solving every problem with the most recent topic instead of the visible structure.\n- Forgetting domain restrictions after algebraic simplification.\n- Using decimal approximations when exact values are cleaner and requested.\n- Stopping after a setup without answering the question asked.\n- Treating a representation change as a different problem instead of the same relationship.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: Find the domain of h(x) = sqrt(x - 5)/(x - 2). Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: [5, infinity)..\n- Prompt: Find the inverse of f(x) = 2x - 7. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: f^-1(x) = (x + 7)/2..\n- Prompt: Factor x^2 - 9x + 20. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: (x - 4)(x - 5)..\n- Prompt: Solve x^2 + 2x + 5 = 0. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = -1 + 2i or x = -1 - 2i..\n- Prompt: Describe the end behavior of p(x) = -2x^5 + 3x^2. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: As x goes to infinity, p(x) goes to -infinity; as x goes to -infinity, p(x) goes to infinity..\n- Prompt: Find the remainder when f(x) = x^3 - 4x + 1 is divided by x - 2. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 1..","Independent Practice":"- Finding rational asymptotes: Find the vertical asymptote and slant asymptote of y = (2x^2 + 1)/(x - 3). Expected answer: Vertical asymptote x = 3; slant asymptote y = 2x + 6..\n- Evaluating exponential growth: An investment of 500 dollars grows by 6 percent per year for 3 years. Find its value to the nearest cent. Expected answer: $595.51..\n- Evaluating a logarithm: Evaluate log_3(81). Expected answer: 4..\n- Solving a logarithmic equation: Solve log_2(x - 1) = 5. Expected answer: x = 33..\n- Using an exact trig value: Find sin(5pi/6). Expected answer: 1/2..\n- Solving a trigonometric equation: Solve 2cos x = 1 on 0 \u003c= x \u003c 2pi. Expected answer: x = pi/3 or x = 5pi/3..","Mastery Check":"- Two sides of a triangle are 5 and 7 with included angle 60 degrees. Find the opposite side. Mastery answer: sqrt(39)..\n- Find the sum of the first 10 terms of the arithmetic sequence with first term 4 and common difference 3. Mastery answer: 175..\n- Add vectors \u003c2, -5\u003e and \u003c4, 1\u003e, then treat the result \u003ca, b\u003e as the complex number a + bi. What is its modulus? Mastery answer: 2sqrt(13)..\n- For f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 1, write vertex form and solve f(x) = 0. Mastery answer: f(x) = (x - 2)^2 - 3; zeros x = 2 + sqrt(3) and x = 2 - sqrt(3)..","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P058\n- Phase: 058\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P058","phaseId":"P058","title":"Mixed review"},"Tut-P059":{"Learning Goal":"Master final boss challenges by choosing the right representation, completing the computation, and interpreting the result.","Why It Matters":"This topic appears in later modeling, graphing, and boss challenges because it connects symbolic work with decisions about structure, restrictions, and meaning.","Prerequisite Check":"- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Read the full prompt and identify the topic families involved before solving.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? List restrictions and units before finalizing an answer.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Choose exact, decimal, graphical, or contextual form based on the question.\n- Can the player explain this prerequisite? Check solutions in the original relationship when squaring, logging, or simplifying rational expressions.","Core Concept":"- A final boss problem combines skills but still breaks into legal smaller moves.\n- Representation changes are tools: equations, graphs, vectors, and complex numbers can describe the same structure.\n- Restrictions protect the solution from extraneous answers and impossible inputs.\n- Modeling problems require a numeric result and an interpretation in context.\n- Mastery means selecting methods flexibly and checking the result against the original prompt.","Worked Example":"- A game pass costs 12 dollars plus 4 dollars per quest. If the total cost is 36 dollars, how many quests were purchased? Answer: 6 quests. Reason: Set up 12 + 4q = 36. Subtract 12 to get 4q = 24, then divide by 4 to get q = 6.\n- A shop sold 7 items total. Apples cost 2 dollars each, berries cost 3 dollars each, and the total was 19 dollars. How many apples and berries were sold? Answer: 2 apples and 5 berries. Reason: Let a be apples and b be berries. The system is a + b = 7 and 2a + 3b = 19. From a = 7 - b, substitute to get 2(7 - b) + 3b = 19, so b = 5 and a = 2.\n- Find the domain of h(x) = sqrt(12 - 3x)/(x + 1). Answer: (-infinity, -1) union (-1, 4]. Reason: The square root requires 12 - 3x \u003e= 0, so x \u003c= 4. The denominator requires x != -1. Combining these gives (-infinity, -1) union (-1, 4].\n- Let f(x) = 3x - 5 and g(x) = x^2 + 1. Solve f(g(x)) = 22. Answer: x = 2sqrt(2) or x = -2sqrt(2). Reason: First compose: f(g(x)) = 3(x^2 + 1) - 5 = 3x^2 - 2. Set 3x^2 - 2 = 22, so 3x^2 = 24 and x^2 = 8. Thus x = 2sqrt(2) or x = -2sqrt(2).","Common Mistakes":"- Trying to apply one memorized procedure to every part of a multi-stage task.\n- Forgetting that a simplified rational expression still carries original restrictions.\n- Keeping extraneous roots after squaring an equation.\n- Using a graph feature without connecting it to the equation that created it.\n- Reporting intermediate values as final answers without context.","Guided Practice":"- Prompt: A projectile has height h(t) = -16t^2 + 64t + 80. Find its maximum height and the positive time when it hits the ground. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Maximum height 144 at t = 2 seconds; it hits the ground at t = 5 seconds..\n- Prompt: Factor f(x) = x^3 - 2x^2 - 5x + 6 completely if x = 1 is a zero, then list all zeros. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: f(x) = (x - 1)(x - 3)(x + 2); zeros -2, 1, and 3..\n- Prompt: Simplify (x^2 - 9)/(x^2 - x - 6), then give the restrictions, hole, and vertical asymptote. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Simplified form (x + 3)/(x + 2); restrictions x != 3 and x != -2; hole (3, 6/5); vertical asymptote x = -2..\n- Prompt: How long does it take 200 dollars growing at 8 percent per year to reach 400 dollars? Give the answer to the nearest hundredth of a year. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: 9.01 years..\n- Prompt: For y = 3sin(2(x - pi/4)) + 1, give the amplitude, period, phase shift, and midline. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: Amplitude 3; period pi; phase shift right pi/4; midline y = 1..\n- Prompt: Solve sin^2 x + sin x = 0 on 0 \u003c= x \u003c 2pi. Coach move: ask which rule or condition applies first. Target: x = 0, pi, or 3pi/2..","Independent Practice":"- Law of sines boss: In a triangle, A = 30 degrees, B = 45 degrees, and side a = 8. Find side b. Expected answer: 8sqrt(2)..\n- Geometric series boss: Find the sum of the first 8 terms of the geometric sequence with a_1 = 3 and r = 2. Expected answer: 765..\n- Ellipse foci boss: For (x + 1)^2/16 + (y - 2)^2/9 = 1, find the center and foci. Expected answer: Center (-1, 2); foci (-1 - sqrt(7), 2) and (-1 + sqrt(7), 2)..\n- Vector navigation boss: An airplane flies with velocity \u003c120, 0\u003e miles per hour while wind adds \u003c0, -30\u003e miles per hour. After 2 hours, what is the displacement and total distance from the start? Expected answer: Displacement \u003c240, -60\u003e; distance 60sqrt(17) miles..\n- Complex polar boss: Compute [2(cos 30 deg + i sin 30 deg)]^3. Expected answer: 8i..\n- Rational equation boss: Solve 1/(x - 2) + 1/(x + 2) = 1. Expected answer: x = 1 + sqrt(5) or x = 1 - sqrt(5)..","Mastery Check":"- Solve 2log_3(x) = log_3(81). Mastery answer: x = 9..\n- A rectangle has perimeter 40. If one side is x, its area is A(x) = x(20 - x). What dimensions give the maximum area, and what is that area? Mastery answer: 10 by 10; maximum area 100 square units..\n- Solve sqrt(x + 5) = x - 1. Mastery answer: x = 4..\n- A robot starts at (0, 0), moves by \u003c3, 4\u003e and then by \u003c-1, 2\u003e. Treat its final point (a, b) as the complex number a + bi. Find the distance from the origin and compute (a + bi)(1 - i). Mastery answer: Distance 2sqrt(10); product 8 + 4i..","Adaptive Tutor Messages":"- If the player chooses the wrong method, ask them to name the visible structure before solving.\n- If arithmetic is the only error, preserve the strategy and have them recompute one line.\n- If notation or restrictions are missing, ask what values, units, or intervals the answer is allowed to use.\n- If the player is fluent, advance to a boss variant that mixes representations.","Tutorial Metadata":"- Tutorial ID: Tut-P059\n- Phase: 059\n- Estimated duration: 18-25 minutes\n- Required prior mastery: prerequisite review plus at least 70 percent accuracy on guided practice","tutorialId":"Tut-P059","phaseId":"P059","title":"Final boss challenges"}};
